\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 The country that is pushing hardest for military  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  in Niger cannot afford it.\b0\par\par\uc2 1,555 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new president, \uc2 Bola Tinubu\uc2 , has had a hectic few months. On May 29, he was sworn in as Nigeria's president after winning the elections in February. A little over a month later, he took over the one-year rotating presidency of Ecowas, the regional organization of 15 West African countries. In his speech, he was forceful in restoring democracy in an area decimated by coups d'\u233\'00\'E9tat.\par \par \uc2 "To our soldiers we did not give resources, we did not invest in them, in their boots, in their training to violate the freedom of the people. To point their weapons against civilian authorities is a violation of the principles for which they were hired, which is to defend the sovereignty of their nations. We should not sit in Ecowas like toothless bulldogs," he said in Bissau.\par \par \uc2 Well, then, just two weeks and two days later, it was his turn to experience a coup d'\u233\'00\'E9tat in his northern neighbor, Niger. Tinubu knew he could not let the opportunity pass him by. He called the rest of the \uc2 Ecowas\uc2  presidents together and convinced them of an unprecedented measure in recent years: threatening to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  militarily to restore power. If in seven days they did not return power to Bazoum, they would go in to restore his power. Their firmness caught everyone by surprise: it is not that \uc2 Ecowas\uc2  had not done it before, the last time was when they managed to remove Yahyah Jammeh from power in Gambia in 2017, but the organization did not dare to do it neither with Mali, nor Burkina Faso or Guinea Conakry in recent years.\par \par \uc2 It is often said that, from words to deeds, there is a stretch. Niger's military junta has not budged, and Tinubu has had to swallow his words after the deadline passed and in the face of rejection at home. The Nigerian Senate rejected his request to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , something technically necessary to send troops to another country, while senators from northern Nigeria, bordering Niger, warned him against it as it could bring war to their homes.\par \par \uc2 "We must engage all parties in serious discussions to convince them to relinquish power and reinstate Bazoum."\par \par \uc2 At the new extraordinary Ecowas summit, he was already changing the tone."It is crucial that we prioritize diplomatic negotiations and dialogue as the basis of our approach. We must engage all parties, including the coup leaders, in serious discussions to convince them to relinquish power and reinstate President Bazoum."\par \par \uc2 The resolution still leaves open the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it notes a change of heart from a president who has gone from being emboldened to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  militarily in another country to being reminded by his own politicians that he suffers enough problems at home and that, if he did so, they would worsen, worsening still the possibility he fears most: that the wave of coups will come to him in Nigeria as well.\par \par \uc2 The main problem facing Nigeria's leaders is how to pay for a war when your own citizens have nothing to eat. Africa's most populous country is experiencing a record inflationary spurt and traders tell them they can't even breathe, but, meanwhile, its leader is thinking of starting a war that could cost the public coffers $2 billion a year, according to estimates by an independent economic organization.\par \par \uc2 If we had to categorize his little more than 50 days in government, it would be a disaster. In his inauguration speech, the septuagenarian announced that he was going to implement a controversial maneuver that had been tried for 10 years without success in the face of public rejection, but which rating agencies such as \uc2 Moody's\uc2  saw as necessary for the health of the public coffers: to end the system of gasoline subsidies.\par \par \uc2 The new government tried to defend itself by saying that it could not afford the 10 billion dollars it meant for the public coffers, a monthly bill of 867 million dollars, but the solution has been worse than the disease. Two days after his inauguration, the public oil company \uc2 NNPC\uc2  had already tripled the price from 26 cents to 70 cents a liter , and the price has not stopped rising, reaching 78 cents a liter in mid-July.\par \par \uc2 Two weeks later, Tinubu decided to take another decision to put an end to the naira black market and increase dollar reserves, which had been falling for months. In February, the previous government's order to introduce a new banknote and withdraw the old one from the market with the intention of curbing vote-buying during the campaign led to an unprecedented shortage, which they had to retract after the elections.\par \par \uc2 Upon his arrival, Tinubu ordered the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to eliminate the fixed rate and switch to a floating exchange rate against the dollar, dictated by the market. Overnight, the official value of the Nigerian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plummeted by 36% to $750 per naira on June 14 from $447 per naira the previous day. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has continued to fall and was exchanged at 853 naira to the dollar on July 19. This new exchange rate was the one that was being used on the parallel market, but the fall has pushed it even lower, to the 862 dollars at which it is currently trading.\par \par \uc2 The two measures had a clear objective: to attract investors by making rating companies happy and to increase the availability of dollars in circulation. Now, the situation has been quite the opposite: in the month and a half since its flagship measures, the Nigerian government has had to declare a state of emergency because of the rebound in food prices.\par \par \uc2 Inflation soared in June to 25.5% y/y, up two points from the previous month in the face of rising prices. In July, it is expected to rise further, affected in parallel by the Russian blockade on grain exports. Nigeria imports 23% of its wheat from these countries and is among its top five importing countries, worth close to $1 billion.\par \par \uc2 The 1999 constitution states that the government can declare a state of emergency when the country is threatened, there is a breakdown of law and order or "there is some danger which clearly constitutes a threat to the existence of Nigeria." This is the first time in decades that this has been done for lack of food, having done so in 2020 for a rise in cases of rape of women and in 2013 in northern states for insecurity, as well as previously for other cases of political violence.\par \par \uc2 In Nigeria, 25 million people are food insecure every day, according to \uc2 UN\uc2  data. The government's decision to declare a state of emergency seeks to mitigate the impact by offering subsidies to farmers on fertilizer and rice grains.\par \par \uc2 Jollof rice is part of the daily diet of millions of Nigerians as a supplement. What for a Spaniard is bread, for a Nigerian is sweet potato or rice. However, several economists criticize that the current measure will not lower the price of the grain in the face of lack of supply. In 2015, the government decided to ban imports of certain products, including rice, to boost local production. Although a production record of 5.29 million tons was reached in 2021, that same year the country consumed 7.96 million tons, there being a deficit. In other words, another of the Government's measures intended to reduce public spending and boost the local economy caused the opposite: shortages and price increases.\par \par \uc2 The Government seeks to stop with one more measure an economic free fall that is shaking Africa's largest economy. Even so, it will be difficult to remedy some of the underlying structural problems that cause food insecurity for millions of Nigerians. \uc2 Boko Haram\uc2  jihadist violence in the north of the country and the effects of climate change. Recurrent droughts in the north are compounded by torrential rains. In 2022, Nigeria suffered its worst floods in a decade, which left more than 600 dead and 1.4 million people displaced in the southeast of the country.\par \par \uc2 For the moment, inflation and the state of emergency have not brought major protests across Nigeria among citizens simply trying to get by day to day. Now, inflation has shown that in fragile contexts it can bring chaos. In Sudan, the rise in the price of bread was the fuse that ignited the protests that brought down the dictatorial regime of \uc2 Omar Al-Bashir\uc2  and, four years later, have plunged the country into a state of civil war. No one envisions or wants that for Nigeria, but the government's actions are only sinking the continent's largest economy and testing the patience of Nigeria's more than 200 million people.\par \par \uc2 Nigeria's new president, \uc2 Bola Tinubu\uc2 , has had a frantic few months. On May 29, he was sworn in as Nigeria's president after winning the elections in February. A little more than a month later, he took over the one-year rotating presidency of Ecowas, the regional organization of 15 West African countries. In his speech, he was forceful in his efforts to restore democracy in an area decimated by coups d'\u233\'00\'E9tat.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230817ej8h002pr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
