\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Revises Nigeria Otlk To Stable From Neg; Afrms At 'B-/B'\b0\par\par\uc2 5,374 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2023\par 22:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 4 Aug 2023 16:08 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Revises Nigeria Otlk To Stable From Neg; Afrms At 'B-/B'\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- On May 29, 2023, Nigeria inaugurated former Governor of Lagos State, \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bola Tinubu\f28 \uc2 \f2 , as its 16th president, after he won the presidential elections. \par      -- The new administration has announced reforms, including exchange-rate \par liberalization and the end of fuel subsidies, which will benefit public \par finances. \par      -- In early May, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 ministry of finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  agreed to \par securitize long-standing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  financing, which will reduce interest \par expenses, further supporting fiscal consolidation. \par      -- Nevertheless, planned fiscal spending remains elevated and inflation \par is high. \par      -- Remaining challenges include relatively low (albeit rising) oil \par production, continued weak fiscal revenue mobilization, and high local and \par foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  borrowing costs. \par      -- The West African region could face heightened geopolitical risks tied \par to the recent coup in neighboring Niger. \par      -- We revised our outlook on our 'B-/B' long- and short-term ratings on \par Nigeria to stable from negative on increased reform momentum. We also raised \par our national scale ratings to 'ngBBB+/ngA-2' from 'ngBBB-/ngA-3'. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On Aug. 4, 2023, S&P Global Ratings revised the outlook on Nigeria to stable \par from negative. At the same time, we affirmed our 'B-/B' long- and short-term \par foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on Nigeria. \par  \par We also raised our long- and short-term Nigeria national scale ratings to \par 'ngBBB+/ngA-2' from 'ngBBB-/ngA-3'. The higher national scale rating reflects \par the improving reform momentum as well as our view of the sovereign as the most \par creditworthy entity in the domestic markets. \par  \par The transfer and convertibility assessment remains 'B-'. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The stable outlook indicates balanced risks to the rating. Relatively low oil \par production and modest revenue mobilization pose risks to public finances and \par external liquidity. However, the new government is moving ahead with a series \par of reforms that could, if delivered, benefit growth and fiscal outcomes. \par  \par Downside scenario \par We could lower the ratings over the next 12 months if we see increasing risks \par to Nigeria's capacity to repay commercial obligations, either because of \par declining external or domestic liquidity. This could arise, for instance, from \par much higher fiscal deficits or debt-servicing needs alongside significantly \par reduced net foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) reserves, or because domestic financial \par markets are unwilling to absorb additional local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debt issuance. \par  \par Upside scenario \par We could raise our ratings over the next 12 months if economic performance \par significantly exceeds our forecasts, and fiscal and external imbalances \par improve significantly. \par  \par RATIONALE \par In February of this year, former Lagos State Governor, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bola Tinubu\f28 \uc2 \f2 , won the \par presidential elections. Upon assuming office on May 29, 2023, the new \par administration announced a series of reforms including the removal of fuel \par subsidies, the liberalization of the exchange rate regime, and governance \par changes at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Nigeria (CBN)\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Earlier in May, the previous \par national assembly had already signed into law the securitization of Ways and \par Means advances (direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lending by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to the \par government to finance deficits) into long-term, lower-cost instruments. We \par believe these measures will gradually benefit Nigeria's public finances and \par its balance of payments. \par  \par However, challenges remain. Inflation is high and despite the recent \par introduction of offsetting measures to counterbalance the removal of fuel \par subsidies, labor unions have ramped up protests against their elimination. \par Interest costs on government debt are elevated and absorb a large share of \par government spending (over 30% of revenue last year, although we project this \par will gradually fall toward 23% by 2026). At the same time, government tax \par revenue collection, as a percentage of GDP, remains very low when compared \par with peers. \par  \par Institutional and economic profile: Despite short-term inflation-related \par obstacles, the new government's reform initiatives should support medium-term \par growth \par      -- After being sworn into power in late May, the new president's \par administration has delivered a series of reforms. \par      -- We expect the oil sector to pick up over 2023-2026 as crude oil \par production levels improve slightly, while significant oil refining (and \par petrochemical) capacity is launched. \par      -- In the medium term, exchange-rate liberalization should benefit the \par non-oil sector as domestic goods become more competitive. \par  \par Nigeria's newly elected government has moved quickly to implement a series of \par fiscal and monetary reforms, which we believe will gradually benefit public \par finances and the balance of payments. New measures include the decision to \par eliminate untargeted fuel subsidies, in an effort to contain spending \par pressures, and reduce the regressivity of fuel policy (where the majority of \par spending on fuel subsidies has historically benefited middle and upper income \par groups). \par  \par Despite the clear fiscal benefits of fuel subsidy reform (which has been \par attempted in the past), labor unions have threatened to strike in protest \par against their removal. The risk of such protests is likely to increase during \par periods of exchange rate depreciation or rising international oil prices. In \par order to mitigate inflationary pressures, in late July, the new government \par passed a supplementary fiscal package of "palliative measures," which is also \par being supported by \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  funding. With this package, the government aims \par to offset the effect of subsidy elimination on lower-income households, by \par providing them with cash transfers tied to their incomes. Via this targeted \par support, the government is attempting to ensure that the fuel subsidy removal \par does not impact the poorest sections of society. \par  \par S&P Global Ratings projects considerable fiscal savings from the elimination \par of fuel subsidies, and other recent reforms. Taken together, the elimination \par of subsidies and the securitization of the Ways and Means advances will \par generate fiscal savings of around 2.5% of GDP in 2023. We also believe the \par government will gradually improve tax intake: by as much as 2 percentage \par points of GDP by 2026, according to our forecasts. \par  \par Nigeria is the most populous economy in Africa. It is a sizable producer and \par exporter of hydrocarbons, ranking among the world's top 15 exporters. However, \par most Nigerians work in the non-oil economy, with 45% of the labor force \par employed in relatively low productivity agriculture, which accounts for an \par estimated 26% of total output. According to \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  data, another 37% of the labor \par force works in household enterprises outside of the agricultural sector, with \par just 11% of Nigerian workers engaged in wage employment, primarily in the \par public sector. Given the composition of the labor force, and high informality, \par increasing tax pressure is a key fiscal challenge, and explains the relative \par dependency on hydrocarbon production for tax revenue. \par  \par Complicating this fiscal challenge, Nigeria has struggled to maintain \par hydrocarbon production levels over the past several years. During 2022, due to \par technical and security issues, oil production (including about 230,000 barrels \par per day [b/d] equivalent of condensates) averaged about 1.3 million barrels \par per day (mbpd), down from 1.6 mbpd in 2021. Militancy in the Niger Delta and \par high incidents of theft and vandalism are the primary causes of dips in oil \par production. Technical and regulatory issues, as well as divestments by some \par large international oil companies, tied to environmental considerations, are \par also deterring new investments. However, a series of projects are in the \par pipeline and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nigerian National Petroleum Company\f28 \uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 NNPC\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) is confident that \par volumes will increase in the near future. \par  \par Crude oil production levels improved to 1.2 mbpd (excluding condensates) in \par May 2023, partly due to improved security surveillance tied to the \par establishment of a new security task force. Via the task force, oil producers \par have enhanced collaboration with government and defense forces, who are taking \par a more zero-tolerance stance to theft. Moreover, oil producers are also \par beginning to work on alternative evacuation methods and shutdown optimization, \par to reduce the loss of oil during episodes of pipeline and facility breaches. \par As a result of these efforts, we expect oil production to slowly recover but \par remain below Nigeria's \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  quota of 1.74 mbpd. An overhaul of the governance \par structure and fiscal terms in the oil sector under the Petroleum Investment \par Act, should, over time, unlock further investment in the hydrocarbon sector, \par despite environmental considerations. \par  \par We also anticipate that refining capacity will substantially increase as a \par large refinery (and petrochemicals facility), owned by the private-sector \par Dangote group, with capacity of 650,000 b/d, starts large-scale production by \par late 2023. In addition, several other refineries--such as at Port Harcourt, \par Warri, and Kaduna--are being rehabilitated, which will significantly \par contribute to the country's refining capacity in the next few years. This new \par refining capacity made it easier for the new president to stick to the removal \par of the petroleum subsidies as pledged in the 2023 budget. \par  \par Given recent reforms and exchange rate liberalization, we now expect Nigerian \par economic growth to average 3.5% over 2023-2026, up from our previous estimate \par of average 3%. Growth in terms of GDP per capita will, nevertheless, remain \par low, partly reflecting the country's high population growth as well as the \par recent depreciation of the Nigerian naira (NGN). Increasing digitization tied \par to the pandemic boosted growth rates in sectors like insurance, finance, and \par information and communication; innovation and entrepreneurship is relatively \par high in these areas. However, severe floods in large parts of the country \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4 Aug 2023 16:09 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Revises Nigeria Otlk To -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par constrained growth within the agriculture sector (which contributes around 20% \par of nominal GDP) in the second and third quarters of 2022. The economy expanded \par slowly in the first quarter of 2023 as a \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2 -imposed change to the naira notes \par in circulation (whereby old notes were replaced with higher denomination new \par ones, and old notes were no longer permitted as legal tender) led to a severe \par cash shortage, while the oil sector contracted. \par  \par The recent Nigerian elections were the first in Nigeria's history where \par electronic voting machines were used. This led to significant issues, with \par many voters denied a vote because they did not possess a voter card. \par Consequently, the turnout was exceptionally low at 27% of the 93 million \par eligible voters. This resulted in the president being elected with the support \par of only 8.8 million votes in a country with a population of over 220 million. \par Legal challenges to his win are still going through the courts, but, in our \par view, are unlikely to lead to significant changes to the overall outcome. The \par new president has not yet named his cabinet, but has submitted a list of names \par to be vetted and approved by the senate, and the cabinet, including the new \par finance minister, is likely to be announced soon. \par  \par Nigeria's internal security has deteriorated over the past several years, with \par banditry, kidnappings, and general instability having increased. Police and \par military forces tend to be overstretched and forced to deal with a multitude \par of security crises. The militant groups Boko Haram and Islamic State West \par Africa Province (ISWAP) are active in the northern regions. In the Middle \par Belt, tensions between farmers and herdsmen have increased, notably over \par scarce land and water resources, which is affecting food supplies. In the \par southern oil-producing states, Niger Delta militants have disrupted oil \par production when conditions have not worked in their favor. The security \par situation worsened in the run up to the elections, but reforms pledged by the \par new government should help improve the situation somewhat, and the new \par president has appointed a new Chief of Army Staff, other service chiefs, and a \par national security advisor. Nevertheless, we do not expect the overall security \par situation to immediately improve, as high unemployment, inflation, and rising \par poverty levels have stagnated social development, fueling militancy. \par  \par Nigeria's president currently holds the rotating position of chairperson for a \par regional body, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS)\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECOWAS\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par has imposed sanctions and warned of possible military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Nigeria\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's northern neighbour, Niger, if, following a military coup in Niger on \par July 26, the former democratic government is not reinstated. \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECOWAS\f28 \uc2 \f2  has also \par suspended Niger's membership. If \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECOWAS\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , it would likely involve \par Nigerian troops alongside other \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECOWAS\f28 \uc2 \f2  troops, and could complicate \par anti-militancy efforts already underway in Northern Nigeria. The issue is \par further complicated by Niger being a large uranium producer and hosting \par anti-insurgency military forces from both France and the U.S., and the coup \par follows four other coups in the region, with some regimes supported by \par Russia's Wagner mercenaries. \par  \par Flexibility and performance profile: Fiscal and external metrics have \par deteriorated in recent years, but liberalization efforts should support fiscal \par consolidation \par  \par      -- We forecast increased oil volume production and higher oil prices, \par improved collection of non-oil revenue, and exchange rate liberalization, \par which lead us to expect some modest fiscal consolidation over the next few \par years. \par      -- We expect interest payments will still consume about a quarter of \par general government revenue over 2023-2026. \par      -- We project usable foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves (gross reserves minus \par encumbered reserves and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  borrowed from domestic residents) will remain \par around $28 billion over 2023-2026, covering over three months of current \par account payments, which is sufficient to cover external debt repayment needs. \par  \par Over 2023-2026, we project modest fiscal consolidation to continue. \par Consolidation will be supported by savings via the elimination of the \par petroleum subsidies and an increase in collection of non-oil taxes and other \par revenue owing to ongoing digitization and improved compliance, savings on \par interest costs due to the securitization of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Ways and Means \par advances, as well reasonably strong oil prices. There could also be an upside \par to oil revenue projections if oil production meets \f28 \uc2 \f2 NNPC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's expectations over \par the next two-to-three years. We project the general government deficit--which \par includes the federal government, states, and local governments combined--will \par stand at 5.4% of GDP in 2023 and average 4.6% in 2024-2026. \par  \par After running a very tightly managed exchange-rate regime, the new government \par cited a long-standing policy of a misaligned exchange rate, announced the \par suspension of the governor of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (with one of the deputy \par governors, Folashodun Shonubi, taking the role of acting governor), and \par permitted the liberalization of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2  ended trading band \par limits and permitted a move to a "willing buyer-willing seller" model at the \par investor and exporter window, which soon led to a significant depreciation of \par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to N780/US$1 from N460/US$1. While the exchange rate is now \par trading significantly more in-line with market demand and supply fundamentals, \par the regime still more closely resembles a managed-float rather than a full \par free floating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par Given that roughly half of the federal budget revenue reflects \par dollar-denominated crude oil export revenue, converted into naira, the \par exchange rate and monetary policy have historically had a material impact on \par Nigeria's fiscal performance. The naira's fall from its official rate of \par N460/US$1 in early June to close to N780/US$1 currently, has improved the \par outlook for tax receipts over the next few years. Following the \par liberalization, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Budget Office has adopted an \par exchange rate of N700/US$1 for its accounts for the second half of the year \par (compared to the initial budgeted exchange rate of N460/US$1) thereby boosting \par revenue in naira. In April, S&P Global Ratings also revised upward its Brent \par oil price forecast to an average $83 per barrel (/b) in 2023, and $85/b in \par 2024-2026 (compared with our forecast of $90/b in 2023, $80/b in 2024, and \par $55/b in both 2025 and 2025) pointing to improving oil-related revenue in \par dollar terms as well, in particular in 2025 and 2026. \par  \par The 2023 federal budget (central government; excluding state budgets) has \par planned for a fiscal deficit of NGN11.34 trillion, which represents 5% of GDP. \par The budget is based on a reasonable oil price estimate of $75/bbl, but an \par ambitious oil production volume target of 1.69 mbpd. The 2023 budget assumed \par the removal of refined petrol subsidies by mid-2023, which cost around NGN4 \par trillion (2% of GDP) in 2022, and the new president followed through on his \par pledge to remove them. The removal of fuel subsides from late June, savings on \par debt service on Ways and Means, as well as the liberalization of the exchange \par rate, will compensate for lower-than-budgeted oil receipts achieved during the \par first half of the year. \par  \par The government is currently reforming the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Inland Revenue Service\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \par which should engender more transparency and improvement in fiscal governance. \par Nevertheless, general government revenue as a percentage of GDP in Nigeria \par average only about 8%--very low even by African and frontier market standards. \par This highlights the limited tax-generation capacity, partly explained by the \par high level of informality in the economy, and partly by the two-tiered federal \par and state tax system in which the states lack the institutional capacity to \par garner taxes. Nevertheless, the authorities have made recent efforts to \par increase revenue streams, especially non-oil revenue. The new administration \par has approved the establishment of a Presidential Committee on Fiscal Policy \par and Tax Reform. This will be chaired by a tax specialist and will comprise \par experts from both the private and public sectors. It will have the \par responsibility to push tax law reform with a view to increasing the tax take. \par We forecast fiscal revenue to GDP to increase to 10% by 2026 due to the \par aforementioned efforts and continued strong collection of VAT and corporation \par income tax. Nevertheless, high debt-servicing costs and security-related \par spending will continue to weigh on fiscal dynamics, leaving reduced funds for \par capital investment, infrastructure development, or social safety nets. \par  \par Nigeria benefits from its relatively deep domestic financial markets. For the \par remainder of 2023, the government plans to issue domestic naira-denominated \par Federal Government of Nigeria (FGN) bonds to finance over 75% of the 2023 \par deficit, with the rest in foreign financing split between concessional \par multilateral and bilateral external financing, and commercial borrowing. \par Between January and June 2023, the Debt Management Office managed to raise \par NGN3.59 trillion of the annual budgeted NGN7.04 trillion in FGN bonds, largely \par at rates between 13.9% and 16.0%, below the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Monetary Policy \par Rate (MPR), with subscription levels remaining high. On the external front, \par the government will likely only issue Eurobonds if market conditions change, \par due to the current prohibitively high yields demanded in the international \par markets, and may opt for either syndicated loans or more multilateral funding, \par if available. On July 12, 2023, the government repaid its $500 million \par Eurobond in full and on time and the next Eurobond maturity (of $1.1 billion) \par is due in 2025; the external commercial debt repayment profile is relatively \par low compared with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve levels. \par  \par The government restructured several years of debt amassed via borrowing from \par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (under the Ways and Means facility) amounting to NGN22.7 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4 Aug 2023 16:09 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Revises Nigeria Otlk To -3-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par trillion (12% of GDP). The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2  agreed to the \par securitization of this debt into an instrument with 40 years tenure, with a \par moratorium on principal repayment for three years and a 9% interest rate. The \par government was previously amassing dues at an interest rate of about 20% on \par this debt (MPR + 1%-3%). We incorporate the impact of this securitization deal \par in our interest expenditure, which along with the projected revenue increase, \par reduces interest cost as a percentage of revenue to 20% of general government \par revenue in 2023, compared with 31% in 2022. We do not consider the \par securitization of the obligations to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as a default under our \par criteria, as our ratings speak to the timely repayment of commercial debt \par obligations--and we classify borrowing from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as inherently \par noncommercial. \par  \par We forecast Nigeria's net general government debt stock (consolidating debt at \par the federal, state, and local government levels, and net of liquid assets) \par will average 40% of GDP for 2023-2026. This level of indebtedness is \par relatively low compared with what we typically observe for 'B' category rated \par peers. We include the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Asset Management Corporation of Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2  (created to \par resolve Nigerian banks' nonperforming loans) debt in our calculation of gross \par and net debt. We also include \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2  open-market-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (OMO) bill issuance, \par as well as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lending to the federal government (via the Ways and \par Means advances), in our debt stock calculations. From 2020, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2  sharply \par reduced the number of OMO bills that it rolled over, but this was partly \par counterbalanced by the increase in the Ways and Means advances. The \par depreciation of the naira has continued to inflate foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debt, \par which makes up approximately 40% of the total debt stock. We include interest \par payments on \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2  bills and Ways and Means in our calculation of current total \par interest costs, with the latter having fallen due to the recent securitization \par deal. \par  \par Despite being a sizable hydrocarbon exporter (close to 90% of exports receipts \par are from crude oil and gas exports), we project that Nigeria will post only \par small current account surpluses for 2023-2026 due to uncertainties regarding \par oil production volumes, alongside high demand for imports. Relatively high oil \par prices (we revised our forecast Brent oil prices for 2025 and 2026 upward to \par $85/b), and robust remittances will support current account receipts (CARs) \par while the country's increase in refining capacity will help to reduce the \par import bill from 2024 onward, in our view. Overall, we now project current \par account surpluses averaging 0.6% of GDP, following a small surplus of 0.2% in \par 2022. \par  \par Under our projections, costlier imports and the clearance of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  arrears will \par limit the rise in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves. We also deduct $10 billion from gross \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par reserves to account for reserves held for forward sale, which makes them \par unavailable for meeting external financing needs. We project usable reserves \par (gross reserves minus reserves held for forward) to remain steady at around \par $28 billion over 2023-2026. Relatively high imports and the reduced level of \par usable reserves will keep our estimate of gross external financing needs (all \par payments to nonresidents) averaging 106% of CARs plus usable reserves during \par 2023-2026. \par  \par Surging fuel, utility, and food prices caused inflation to hit 18.8% \par year-on-year in 2022, versus the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2 's 6%-9% target. Due to fast-paced \par increase in consumer price index (CPI) inflation, \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2  raised its benchmark \par rate by a cumulative 500 basis points in 2022 to 16.5%, followed by further \par rises to 18.75% currently. The removal of fuel subsidies and the exchange rate \par depreciation, alongside global inflationary pressures, will ensure that \par double-digit headline inflation will persist in 2023, but will gradually \par decline to 15% by 2026 owing to the monetary tightening and improvement in \par supply of refined crude products in line with increasing refining capacity. \par  \par The banking sector's sensitivity to oil prices and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  depreciation \par exposes it to short credit cycles and high credit risks. The sector has a \par material exposure to the oil and gas sector, averaging about 30% of total \par loans, which leads to concentration and exposure to energy transition risks. \par Banks have converted part of their foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loans to naira loans amid \par U.S. dollar scarcity that affected manufacturers. That said, the naira \par depreciation will result in an increase of the sector exposure in foreign \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loans, estimated at about 40% in 2022. \par  \par High inflation and interest rates will likely constrain real lending \par expansion, as will banks' high cash reserve ratios (CRRs). The \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2 's CRRs \par imposed on banks stand at a high statutory minimum of 32.5%, but for some \par banks these are significantly higher at around 45% of deposits. These elevated \par CRRs have also constrained lending. We expect credit losses will average 2% in \par 2023, while the nonperforming loan ratio deteriorates to 5.7% in 2023, above \par the 5.0% regulatory limit, amid high inflation and interest rates. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par  \par      -- General Criteria: Environmental, Social, And Governance Principles In \par Credit Ratings, Oct. 10, 2021 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For National And Regional Scale Credit \par Ratings, June 25, 2018 \par      -- Criteria | Governments | Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Principles Of Credit Ratings, Feb. 16, 2011 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments, May 18, 2009 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators, July 10, 2023. An interactive version is \par available at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.spratings.com/sri}}}\uc2 \f2  \par      -- Sovereign Ratings List, July 10, 2023 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings History, July 10, 2023 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to \par make an informed decision. \par  \par After the primary analyst gave 