\par\par \b \uc2 After inauguration fanfare, immense economic challenges await Nigeria's Tinubu\b0\par\par\uc2 754 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Mai 2023\par 12:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Debt servicing costs surpassed revenue in 2022\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Complicated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies make investing locally tough\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Foreign direct investment slid 79% since from 2014-2022 - NBS\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Oil production hit three-decade low, remains under pressure\par \par \uc2 By MacDonald Dzirutwe and Libby George\par \par \uc2 LAGOS, May 26 (Reuters) - Nigeria's incoming President \uc2 Bola Tinubu\uc2  will inherit anaemic economic growth, record debt and shrinking oil output, but before he can start fixing these pressing problems he will need to secure public support for painful decisions.\par \par \uc2 Life is tough for citizens of Africa's biggest economy, and a tangle of protectionist economic policies and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have spooked investors.\par \par \uc2 An attempt by \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce hugely expensive fuel subsidies a decade ago met with mass public protests and had to be dropped.\par \par \uc2 Tinubu, a member of President \uc2 Muhammadu Buhari\uc2 's All Progressives Congress, helped propel the outgoing president to power in 2015.\par \par \uc2 Now, businesses, international investors and citizens are hoping he can use his experience as governor of Lagos state to recharge Nigeria's struggling economy and finally confront its most difficult challenges.\par \par \uc2 IN DEBT, IN TROUBLE\par \par \uc2 Nigeria's debt has ballooned by nearly 60% since 2015, hitting $103 billion last year, according to the Debt Management Office. Its growth is outstripping GDP expansion, and the government has warned that once off-book loans from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  are added to the tally, it could hit 77 trillion naira ($167 billion).\par \par \uc2 While Nigeria's debt-to-GDP ratio is a modest 23.2%, compared with 60% in fellow oil producer Angola, experts say the portion of revenue needed to service the debt is alarming.\par \par \uc2 In January, ratings agency \uc2 Moody's\uc2  downgraded Nigeria, citing those figures. According to some calculations, debt servicing costs surpassed revenue last year.\par \par \uc2 Gregory Smith, emerging markets fund manager with M&G Investments said Nigeria's "shockingly low levels of government revenue" also raised questions around its ability to spend to boost growth.\par \par \uc2 "The debt pressures are symptomatic of that lack of government revenue," Smith said.\par \par \uc2 Increasing tax collection, Smith said, would be key for Tinubu.\par \par \uc2 OIL THEFT, SUBSIDIES\par \par \uc2 Some of the revenue troubles stem from rampant, industrial-scale theft that last year pressed oil output to its lowest in more than 30 years. Oil and gas typically fund half of Nigeria's budget and 90% of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Continued theft, underinvestment and industrial disputes, hinder output.\par \par \uc2 On top of this, crippling fuel subsidies drain what is left from oil sales. \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  estimates that the implicit petrol subsidy has cost the government approximately 2.4% of GDP in foregone revenue. Experts say taming the subsidy, and boosting oil output, are key.\par \par \uc2 "The market appears quite myopic in focusing on those two things in particular: the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and the removal of fuel subsidies in addition to broader change at the \uc2 CBN\uc2 ," said Yvette Babb of fund manager William Blair.\par \par \uc2 Buhari's government created a complicated web of official and parallel exchange rates in an effort to support the embattled naira. It also created a long list of items banned from using \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Businesses say resulting widespread dollar shortages are crushing, while investors say the difficulty in getting money out of the country has strangled investment.\par \par \uc2 Smith and Babb said naira bonds, and investing locally, are virtually impossible as a result.\par \par \uc2 "The main thing is problems with being able to exit the market even if you felt like you could make a return," Smith said.\par \par \uc2 Government data showed that foreign direct investment dropped from $2.2 billion in 2014, the year before Buhari took office, to $468 million last year.\par \par \uc2 CHANGES ARE HARD SELL\par \par \uc2 Getting Nigerians to stomach painful reforms hinges on convincing them that they will make life better - and that will be a tough sell.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is at a nearly two-decade high, eating into savings and salaries. Unemployment is at a record 33%, prompting a punishing brain drain. Additionally, Tinubu's 8.79 million votes are the fewest won by a Nigerian president since the country returned to democracy in 1999, limiting his goodwill.\par \par \uc2 "He may need to demonstrate what he can deliver for the Nigerian people before he can take something away that is obviously reducing the cost of living for a large share of the population," Babb said of fuel subsidies. Allowing the naira to weaken, she added, also "comes at a cost." ($1 = 460.0000 naira)\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Toby Chopra) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-5-26T11:10:56.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230526ej5q012w5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
