\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Revises Nigeria Outlook To Negative From Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 5,403 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2023\par 22:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 3 Feb 2023 16:07 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Revises Nigeria Outlook To Negative From Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- Low (albeit recently rising) crude oil production, large \par refined-petroleum subsidy costs, high debt service expenditure, and associated \par sizable fiscal deficits are exacerbating Nigeria's fiscal and external \par imbalances. \par      -- In addition, limited and expensive access to international capital \par markets, and a consequent increasing reliance on significant domestic funding \par at relatively high interest rates, is further weighing on net interest costs \par and the government's fiscal position. \par      -- Nigeria will hold general elections on Feb. 25, and a close three-way \par presidential race is being fought. All three leading presidential candidates \par have pledged significant reforms, but they will inherit a deteriorating fiscal \par story. \par      -- We therefore revised our outlook to negative from stable and affirmed \par our 'B-/B' sovereign credit ratings on Nigeria. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On Feb. 3, 2023, S&P Global Ratings revised its outlook on Nigeria to negative \par from stable. At the same time, we affirmed our 'B-/B' long- and short-term \par foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on the country. \par  \par We also lowered our long - and short-term Nigeria national scale ratings to \par 'ngBBB-/ngA-3' from 'ngBBB/ngA-2'. \par  \par The transfer and convertibility assessment remains 'B-'. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The negative outlook reflects increasing risks to Nigeria's debt servicing \par capacity over the next one-to-two years due to intensifying fiscal and \par external pressures. \par  \par Downside scenario \par We could lower the ratings if risks to Nigeria's capacity to repay commercial \par obligations continue to worsen, either because of declining external liquidity \par or a reduction in fiscal flexibility. This could occur, for instance, if we \par see higher fiscal expenditure, higher debt servicing costs, or significantly \par reduced liquid \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve levels. \par  \par Upside scenario \par We could revise the outlook to stable if Nigeria experiences significantly \par stronger economic performance than we expect, and external and domestic \par financing pressures prove to be contained, while fiscal deficits decrease \par faster than we project. \par  \par RATIONALE \par The outlook revision reflects our view that Nigeria's debt servicing capacity \par has weakened due to high fiscal deficits and increased external pressures. \par These stresses stem from low (albeit recently rising) oil production volumes, \par large refined-petroleum subsidy costs, high debt service expenditure, and a \par relatively large planned fiscal deficit in the 2023 budget. \par  \par The economy is estimated to have expanded by about 2.8% in 2022 and we \par forecast real GDP to average 3.1% in 2023-2026. Below-capacity oil production \par will likely continue to affect export growth, while inflationary pressure, \par fiscal constraints, and sluggish investment will weigh on consumption and \par investment growth. However, these factors are likely to be partially \par counterbalanced by a new, potentially more business-friendly administration \par after the elections. \par  \par Oil production (including condensates) averaged about 1.37 million barrels per \par day (mbpd) in 2022, below the budgeted 1.60 mbpd, and below Nigeria's \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par production quota of 1.8 mbpd. Low volumes more than offset relatively high \par average oil prices (at $98, compared with a budgeted $73) in 2022, keeping the \par general government fiscal deficit at 6.2% of GDP. Significant underperformance \par of oil revenue in 2022 and higher debt service expenditure support our view \par that the government's financial commitments appear strained unless there is \par credible fiscal consolidation after the elections. \par  \par External buffers are also under pressure. Rising prices of imported goods, \par payments to clear \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) backlogs, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Nigeria\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's (\f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2 's) \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to stabilize the naira (NGN), \par and low oil volume exports reduced gross \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves by about $3 \par billion in 2022, reaching $38 billion by year-end. We also lowered our \par estimate of usable reserves to $28 billion for 2023 to account for what S&P \par Global Ratings estimates to be encumbered reserves of about $10 billion. \par  \par The 2023 federal budget (excluding that of the states) estimates a fiscal \par deficit of NGN11.34 trillion, 5% of GDP for this year. Debt service \par expenditure is budgeted at NGN6.3 trillion, 57% of federal revenue of an \par estimated NGN11.9 trillion. The budget is based on the assumption that petrol \par subsidies will be removed by mid-2023. At the general government level \par (federal budget plus states), we forecast a fiscal deficit of 5.9% of GDP in \par 2023 before averaging about 5.4% over 2024-2026, on the assumption a new \par government will pursue a degree of fiscal consolidation. \par  \par Alongside fiscal and external imbalances, expensive access to external capital \par markets (due to currently high global interest rates demanded from emerging \par market issuers) and an increasing reliance on significant domestic funding at \par higher interest rates will weigh on ratios, with average annual interest as a \par share of general government revenue rising to a high average of 31% in \par 2023-2026, while the federal tax take--general government revenue as a \par percentage of GDP--will average only 10%, significantly lower than among \par peers. The government will largely rely on domestic borrowings to finance its \par deficit in 2023, with limited planned external issuance. \par  \par Despite increasing external and fiscal pressures, we think the government has \par sufficient debt-servicing capacity to pay the Eurobond maturity of $500 \par million due in July 2023. Parliamentary debates on a draft bill aiming at \par restructuring debt accumulated under the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Ways and Means facility (an \par overdraft facility provided to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 ministry of finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) are underway in the \par National Assembly. If implemented, it will not constitute a default as per S&P \par Global Ratings' criteria because we do not classify \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  financing as \par a commercial debt obligation, and will deliver significant interest-cost \par savings. We already include the Ways and Means facility in our debt stock \par calculations. \par  \par There may be some sporadic violence in the run-up to and during the \par presidential elections in February, but we expect a broadly smooth democratic \par transition between the governments as President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Muhammadu Buhari\f28 \uc2 \f2  will complete \par his second term in May and stand down. We expect the new government to \par undertake structural reforms, but these will likely take time. \par  \par Institutional and economic profile: Lively elections will deliver a new \par president, and we expect the new government to launch structural reforms \par      -- After elections in February, we expect a transition of power in May, \par followed by some positive reform momentum and a potentially more \par business-friendly president. \par      -- We forecast that the Nigerian economy will grow 3.3% in 2023, \par supported by non-oil growth, followed by average 3% annual growth over \par 2024-2026. \par      -- We expect the oil sector's prospects to improve over 2023-2026 as \par crude oil production levels increase, while significant refining capacity \par (classified as non-oil) is launched. \par  \par Presidential elections will take place on Feb. 25, and despite some likely \par sporadic violence during the election process, we expect a democratic \par transition to a new president. Incumbent president \f28 \uc2 \f2 Muhammadu Buhari\f28 \uc2 \f2  is \par completing his second term and, as per the constitutional two-term limit, will \par have to stand down. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bola Tinubu\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the national leader of President Buhari's \par ruling All Progressives Congress party and former governor of Lagos state, has \par been selected as the party's presidential candidate. He will face \f28 \uc2 \f2 Atiku \par Abubakar\f28 \uc2 \f2  of the opposition Peoples Democratic Party, while the Labor party's \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Peter Obi\f28 \uc2 \f2  has emerged as a strong third contender, especially among younger \par voters, who are shunning the traditional two parties. All three candidates are \par viewed as fairly business-friendly and have cited stimulating GDP growth, \par exchange rate liberalization, fiscal consolidation, revenue mobilization, and \par the removal of oil subsidies as top priorities. \par  \par In the past decade, notwithstanding sporadic violence, Nigeria, has achieved \par reasonably peaceful transfers of power via elections despite its large size, \par ethnic and religious diversity, and complex federal structure. However, \par relatively weak checks and balances, strained public finances (including \par frequent use of \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2  funds to finance fiscal deficits), and a myriad of \par security issues underscore significant institutional and economic weaknesses. \par  \par Nigeria is a sizable producer and exporter of hydrocarbons, ranking among the \par world's top 15 exporters. However, oil production (including condensates) in \par 2022 averaged about 1.37 mbpd due to technical and security issues. Militancy \par in the Niger Delta (which some commentators view as tied to largess ahead of \par the elections) and incidents of vandalism caused oil production to fall in \par 2002, although the situation has improved in the past few months. In addition, \par technical and regulatory issues and divestments by big international companies \par are holding back oil production levels and investments. Long-stalled liquefied \par natural gas (LNG) projects like Brass and Olakola are also delaying planned \par expansions of LNG production. \par  \par Crude oil production levels improved in late 2022 and early 2023, partly due \par to improved security surveillance tied to the establishment of a new security \par task force and security command and control center, while repairs at the \par Forcados terminal added to production. Via the task force, oil producers have \par enhanced collaboration with government and defense forces to improve the \par security situation. Moreover, oil producers are beginning to work on \par alternative evacuation methods and shutdown optimization to reduce the loss of \par oil and shutdowns via thefts and vandalism. Following these efforts, we expect \par oil production to slowly recover. However, production levels will still be \par below Nigeria's current \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  quota of 1.79 mbpd. An overhaul of the governance \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 3 Feb 2023 16:07 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Revises Nigeria Outlook To -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par structure and fiscal terms in the oil sector under the Petroleum Investment \par Act (PIA) should unlock further investment in the hydrocarbon sector. We \par assume Brent oil prices will average $90 per barrel (/bbl) in 2023 followed by \par $80 in 2024 (higher than the $85/bbl in 2023, $55/bbl in 2024 that we had at \par our most recent review), before falling in 2025-2026. \par  \par We anticipate a substantial increase in refining capacity: a large refinery, \par owned by the Dangote group, is to start large-scale production by March 2023. \par It has capacity of 650,000 barrels per day. In addition, several refineries \par such as those at Port Harcourt, Warri, and Kaduna are being rehabilitated, \par which will materially contribute to the country's refining capacity in the \par next couple of years. This new refining capacity should make it easier for any \par new president to cut refined-petroleum subsidies, currently budgeted to end in \par mid-2023. \par  \par We expect the Nigerian economy to maintain annual growth of about 3.1% over \par 2023-2026. Although we expect the economy to recover and exceed its \par pre-pandemic size in 2023, growth in GDP per capita terms will remain low, \par partly reflecting the country's high population growth. Increasing \par digitization tied to the pandemic has worked well for growth in sectors like \par insurance, finance, and information and communication. However, severe floods \par in large parts of the country constrained agricultural growth in the second- \par and third-quarters of 2022. Agriculture contributes about 20% of nominal GDP. \par Due to the sharp fall in oil production, the oil sector is estimated to have \par declined almost 15%. \par  \par Nigeria's internal security deteriorated over the past several years with \par violence, kidnappings, and general instability having increased. Police and \par military forces tend to be overstretched and are forced to deal with a \par multitude of security crises. The militant groups Boko Haram and Islamic State \par West Africa Province are active in the northern regions. In the Middle Belt, \par tensions between farmers and herdsmen have increased, notably over scarce land \par and water resources, and this is affecting food supplies. In the southern \par oil-producing states, Niger Delta militants have historically disrupted oil \par production when conditions have not worked in their favor, but since late 2022 \par volumes have risen slightly. We expect the Election Commission, supported by \par the security services, to manage a broadly democratic transition via the \par election. This will be the first election in Nigeria's history that will use \par electronic voting machines and will require voter IDs, the rollout of which \par has faced delays and could cause issues if a significant section of voters do \par not receive voter IDs before election day. We do not expect an immediate \par improvement to the security situation after the election because high \par unemployment, high inflation, and rising poverty levels have stagnated social \par development. \par  \par A recent exercise by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Nigeria (CBN) \f28 \uc2 \f2 calling for the \par withdrawal of high value Naira notes (NGN 200, 500 and 1,000 notes) has led to \par significant issues and bottlenecks owing to a shortage of new notes across the \par country. The note withdrawal deadline was extended to Feb. 10 from Jan. 31 \par (with a likely seven day grace period to Feb. 17) to give the public more time \par to exchange old notes, which will not be valid or exchangeable after the Feb. \par 10 deadline. A number of state governors have called on the administration and \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2  to extend the deadline further. \par  \par Flexibility and performance profile: Fiscal and external metrics have \par deteriorated, necessitating credible fiscal consolidation efforts post-election \par      -- We expect interest payments to consume more than a quarter of general \par government revenue over 2023-2026, indicating a strained debt trajectory. \par      -- Despite higher oil prices and improved collection of non-oil revenue, \par we estimate the consolidated fiscal deficit (federal and state) at a \par relatively high 6.2% of GDP in 2022, but post-election, fiscal consolidation \par efforts will see it average 5.5% a year in 2023-2026. \par      -- We expect usable foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves will remain near $29 \par billion over 2023-2026, covering over three months of current account payments \par and sufficient to cover planned upcoming external debt repayment needs. \par  \par Despite higher oil prices and better non-oil revenue collection, we estimate \par that the general government fiscal deficit increased to 6.2% of GDP in 2022 \par from 6.1% in 2021. This reflected low oil production volume and a \par supplementary budget approved in December 2022 to accommodate additional \par expenditure to repair flood-related damage and interest payments of about \par NGN1.5 trillion on Ways and Means funding, which was not included in the \par initial 2022 budget. Furthermore, oil revenue underperformed by about 60% of \par budget due to a fall in oil production volumes. \par  \par The 2023 federal budget (central government; excluding state budgets) \par envisages a fiscal deficit of NGN11.34 trillion, or 5% of GDP. The budget is \par based on a reasonable oil price estimate of $75/bbl (S&P Global Ratings \par estimates Brent at $90/bbl for 2023) but an ambitious oil production volume \par target of 1.69 mbpd. We project the general government deficit, which includes \par that of the federal government, states, and local governments, will stand at \par 5.9% of GDP in 2023 and average 5.4% in 2024-2026. The 2023 budget assumes \par removal of refined petroleum subsidies by mid-2023, which cost about NGN4 \par trillion (2% of GDP) in 2022. Subsidy removal might be done in a phased manner \par to avoid inflationary pressures. We account for modest fiscal slippage in 2023 \par at the state and central government levels during the election cycle. \par  \par For 2024-2026, we expect modest fiscal consolidation through the elimination \par of the petroleum subsidies and an increase in collection of non-oil taxes and \par other revenue owing to ongoing digitization and improved compliance, as well \par as the restructuring of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Ways and Means advances, which will reduce \par interest expenditure. \par  \par The government has been attempting to reform the Fiscal Responsibility Act \par 2007 and is reforming the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Inland Revenue Service (FIRS) \f28 \uc2 \f2 to improve \par transparency and increase the tax take. General government revenue averaged \par only about 8% of GDP--very low even by African and frontier market standards. \par This highlights the limited tax-generation capacity, partly explained by the \par high level of informality in the economy and the two-tiered federal and state \par tax system. The states are particularly poor at garnering taxes. Nevertheless, \par authorities have recently made efforts to increase revenue streams, especially \par non-oil revenue. The Finance Bill 2022 includes increases in the Tertiary \par Education Tax, a new corporate tax rate of 50% on companies engaged in gas \par flaring, a new import levy for goods imported from outside Africa, and an \par increase in scope of services liable for excise duty. We forecast fiscal \par revenue to GDP to increase to 10% by 2024 due to efforts and continued strong \par collection of value-added and corporate income tax. Nevertheless, high debt \par servicing costs and security-related spending will continue to weigh on fiscal \par dynamics, leaving less flexibility in allocating funds for capital investment, \par infrastructure development, or social safety nets. \par  \par In 2023, the Debt Management Office plans to finance the bulk of the budget \par via domestic issuances; of the NGN10.6 trillion due to be raised, NGN7.0 \par trillion is planned to be raised domestically, with only NGN3.5 trillion \par borrowed externally. External borrowing will constitute NGN1.8 trillion in \par multilateral borrowing (largely already granted), and NGN1.76 trillion ($4.1 \par billion) in commercial external borrowing--either Eurobonds or syndicated \par loans--down from NGN2.6 billion in the 2022 budget. The government will likely \par only issue Eurobonds if market conditions improve, due to high yields in the \par international markets, and could resort to syndicated loans in lieu. Nigeria \par last issued Eurobonds in March 2022. While issuance conditions for emerging \par and frontier markets have deteriorated since then, they recently improved \par somewhat. On the domestic market interest rates have also gone up sharply, \par partly due to a 600 basis point (bp) hike of the monetary policy rate (MPR) \par rate by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2 . So far, the market remains liquid, with recent auctions \par significantly oversubscribed (473% oversubscribed at the most recent auction \par on Jan. 25), partially due to the growing level of deposits and funds under \par management in the domestic pension and banking sector. \par  \par We forecast Nigeria's net general government debt stock (consolidating debt at \par the federal, state, and local government levels, and net of liquid assets) \par will continue rising and average about 45% of GDP for 2023-2026. We include \par debt issued by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Asset Management Corp. of Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2  (created to resolve \par Nigerian banks' nonperforming loans) in our calculation of gross and net debt. \par We also include \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2  open-market-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (OMO) bill issuance and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par lending to the federal government (via the Ways and Means advances) in our \par debt stock calculations. From 2020 onward, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2  sharply reduced rolling \par over its OMO bills, but this was partly counterbalanced by the increase in the \par Ways and Means advances. Naira depreciation has continued to inflate foreign \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debt, which makes up approximately 40% of total debt. However, the \par majority of external debt is concessional in nature, at very low interest \par rates. \par  \par Overall, government debt servicing costs as a percentage of revenue are high. \par We include interest payments on \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2  bills and Ways and Means in our \par calculation of total interest costs. The government is looking to restructure \par debt amassed under Ways and Means amounting to about NGN23 trillion (12% of \par GDP). The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 ministry of finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2  have proposed the securitization of this \par debt for 40 years, with a moratorium on principal repayment for three years \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 3 Feb 2023 16:07 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Revises Nigeria Outlook To -3-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and a 9% interest rate. The president recently submitted this proposal to the \par National Assembly for ratification. The government currently pays interest \par rate of almost 20.5% on this debt (MPR plus 3%). \par  \par Despite being a sizable hydrocarbon exporter (close to 90% of exports receipts \par are from crude oil and gas exports), we project only small current account \par surpluses of 0.2% of GDP for 2023-2024. Relatively high oil prices, and robust \par remittances will strengthen current account receipts (CARs) in 2023 and 2024 \par while the increase in the country's refining capacity owing to the new Dangote \par refinery will help reduce the import bill beyond 2023, in our view. \par  \par Despite the continued increase in CARs, costlier imports and clearance of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par arrears, \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the Investors and Exporters window will limit the \par proportionate increase in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves. We also deduct $10 billion from gross \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves to account for what we estimate as encumbered reserves, which \par could make them unavailable for meeting external financing needs. We project \par usable reserves (gross reserves minus encumbered reserves) to average about \par $28 billion over 2023-2026. Relatively high imports and reduced level of \par usable reserves will keep our estimate of gross external financing needs (all \par payments to nonresidents) averaging 110% of CARs plus usable reserves for \par 2023-2026. Nevertheless, currently, external liquidity remains sufficient for \par upcoming external redemptions, including the Eurobond maturity of $500 million \par due in July 2023. Over the past several years, Eurobond interest and \par redemptions have been paid on time and in full. \par  \par After running a very tightly managed exchange-rate regime, in May 2021, the \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2  devalued the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (by only 7.6%) and ostensibly abandoned its official \par exchange rate, adopting the Nigerian Autonomous \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Fixing \par Mechanism rate, which currently stands at NGN460 against the U.S. dollar. \par However, the naira continues to trade at a significant discount in the \par parallel market, where it stands at NGN750 per dollar. \par  \par Inflation hit 18.8% year on year in 2022--versus the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2 's 6%-9% target--on \par surging fuel, utility, and food prices. Due to the spike in consumer price \par index inflation, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2  raised its benchmark rate by a cumulative 500 bps in \par 2022 to 16.5%, followed by another 100 bps rise in January 2023. We project \par 17% headline inflation will persist in 2023 but will gradually decline to 12% \par by 2026 owing to monetary tightening and improvement in supply of refined \par crude products in line with increasing refining capacity. The government plans \par to remove petroleum subsidies by mid-2023, which will weigh on inflation for \par 2023. We understand that \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  financing will partially fund social \par spending intended to offset the impact of subsidy elimination on the \par population. \par  \par The banking sector's earnings resilience to economic downturns has improved \par over the past few years, supported by efforts to expand digital channels and \par noninterest income. The system's dollarization has moderated as banks continue \par to de-risk their credit portfolios in a tight \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. Still, credit risks \par stemming from sensitivity to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  depreciation, concentration, and energy \par transition risks persist because lending to the hydrocarbon sector still forms \par a sizable share of banks' lending (30% of total loans). This leaves banks \par vulnerable to asset price shocks, resulting in asset quality problems. The \par nonperforming loan ratio fell to 5% in 2021 and breached the 5% regulatory \par limit in 2022, when it fell to 4.2%. Nevertheless, we think pressure on asset \par quality metrics could re-emerge amid high inflation and interest rates. \par Similarly, we expect credit losses will increase in line with that trend to a \par moderate 1.2% of total loans. Moreover, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2 's naira and U.S dollar \par liquidity management creates some distortions in the banking sector, which in \par turn have negative implications for banks' cost of funding and risk-adjusted \par returns. Overall, we expect the sector will remain profitable. Banks' earnings \par will come under pressure in 2023 because of higher cost of funds amid elevated \par cash reserve requirements. Banking sector assets stand at about 35% of GDP, \par while the sector's exposure to government (and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) constitute a \par relatively high share of of banks' total exposure (approximately 40%). \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par  \par      -- General Criteria: Environmental, Social, And Governance Principles In \par Credit Ratings, Oct. 10, 2021 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For National And Regional Scale Credit \par Ratings, June 25, 2018 \par      -- Criteria | Governments | Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Principles Of Credit Ratings, Feb. 16, 2011 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments, May 18, 2009 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- Sovereign Ratings Score Snapshot, Jan. 18, 2023 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings List, Jan. 4, 2023 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings History, Jan. 4, 2023 \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators, Dec. 12, 2022. Interactive version \par available at "\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\c