\par\par \b \uc2 2023, A Year of Victories and Accomplishments\b0\par\par\uc2 1,932 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2023\par 09:26\par \uc2 InPR\par \uc2 BUINPR\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023 SeeNews. All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 2023, A Year of Victories and Accomplishments\par \par \uc2 Dear Esteemed Client,\par \par \uc2 As we set off on another 365 days of breaking boundaries and achieving new milestones, let me wish you all a happy and healthy new year.\u8239\'20\'2FCelebrating the end of one year and the beginning of another often fills one with a lot of hope and excitement.\par \par \uc2 Without a doubt, 2022 shocked, rocked, and rolled global markets. The year began with cautious optimism, given the 6.1% GDP growth of 2021, that the global economy would consolidate on its post-COVID recovery as lingering concerns \u8211\'20\'13 weak supply chain recovery, new COVID variants, and geopolitical tensions \u8211\'20\'13 formed the early negative signals. As such, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  in January 2022 lowered its earlier growth expectation for the year from 4.9% to 4.4%.\par \par \uc2 By late February, Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion of Ukraine further dented the growth outlook. This development negatively impacted financial markets and worsened inflationary conditions globally, leading to a major surge in commodity prices. This new reality sparked an aggressive hawkish policy stance by global systemic Central Banks. It is noteworthy that there were more than 60 increases in benchmark rates by global Central Banks to curb inflation which had risen to unprecedented highs. Consequently, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  was compelled to further revise its global growth projection downward for the year by a cumulative of 1.2% to 3.2%.\par \par \uc2 In Nigeria, while the domestic economy remained resilient through H1:2022, the combined effect of year-long structural challenges, monetary-fiscal policy mismatch, and negative externalities dampened growth momentum in the 3rd quarter. Real GDP growth slowed to 2.3% in the 3rd quarter of 2022 from 3.1% and 3.5% in the first and second quarters, respectively. Inflationary pressures also mounted, rising from 16.9% at the start of the year to 21.3% by year-end. On the fiscal side, sustained revenue underperformance and bourgeoning expenditure took Nigeria\u8217\'20\'19s debt profile up by 18.7% to \u8358\'20\'A667.8tn inclusive of the \uc2 CBN\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s ways & means of N22.8tn. This further worsened key debt sustainability metrics such as debt-to-GDP at 35.2% and debt service-to-revenue at 80.7% in the 3rd quarter of 2022 from 32.0% and 76.1%, respectively, at the end of 2021. Consequently, global credit rating agency, \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  downgraded Nigeria\u8217\'20\'19s long-term credit rating to B- from B with stable outlook. As if this was not enough, the National Bureau of Statistics reported that the number of people living in multidimensional poverty is now 132.9m, which translates to 63.0% of the population.\par \par \uc2 On the monetary policy front, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Nigeria\uc2  successively hiked rates four times by a cumulative of 500bps to 16.5% in a bid to combat inflation. However, this policy action underperformed expectations due to the counter-effect of \uc2 CBN\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s large-scale liquidity injection through ways & means advancement and other \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In addition, despite continuous attempts to harmonise the rate in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the parallel market rate rose by 31.0% in 2022 to \u8358\'20\'A6740.00/$1.00 by year-end, while the I&E rate rose 6.1% to N461.50/$1.00 translating to a spread of N279.53/$1.00 between the parallel and I&E \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 The spillover effect of these macroeconomic challenges impacted Naira-denominated assets in different ways. In the equity market, bullish momentum dominated proceedings in H1:2022 (NGX-ASI gained 21.3%) supported by relatively low fixed-income yield, positive corporate actions (listing of BUAFOODS & GEREGU and DANGOTE share buy-back programme), and impressive earnings. However, by mid-November, the return on the market had dropped to 2.6% following aggressive rotation into fixed-income assets due to an improved yield environment. Nevertheless, year-end portfolio rebalancing and positioning for full-year dividend payments helped rally the market in December, as the NGX-ASI closed 2022 up 20.0% in line with Afrinvest Research forecast of 19.1%. In the fixed-income market, yield momentum picked up noticeably in H2:2022 following four successive episodes of MPR hikes by the \uc2 CBN\uc2 , which prompted the repricing of yields across the ends of the curve. However, bond yields rose less steeply than T-bills due largely to the tight liquidity environment and sustained backdoor financing of FG\u8217\'20\'19s deficit through \uc2 CBN\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s ways & means facility.\par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, the outlook for the global economy in 2023 remains bleak with the \uc2 IMF\uc2  projecting growth of about 2.7%. At the same time, we believe the possibility of a global economic recession is less likely, although we do not rule out the possibility of some European countries sliding into a recession due to the heavy knock-on effect of the Russian-Ukraine War. Additionally, with global inflationary pressures expected to ease, we opine that global Central Banks are likely to reduce the magnitude of policy rate increases going forward.\par \par \uc2 In Nigeria, we expect the lingering structural inefficiencies to weigh in on economic growth in 2023 because of the political transition and the time it would take policy reforms (if any) by the incoming administration to manifest gains. Hence, Afrinvest Research projects a modest 3.0% GDP growth for the year. We also expect unfavourable external pricing conditions to keep the Federal Government active in the domestic debt market in 2023. In the face of expected wide budget financing gaps relative to the depth of the local market, we do not rule out the possibility of continued ways & means financing despite plans to securitise the liability which stands today at N22.0 trillion. Importantly, we expect monetary policy to be less aggressive in 2023 while inflation should soften, providing the basis for fixed income yields to taper.\par \par \uc2 Our Key Highlights and Milestones\par \par \uc2 For us at Afrinvest, the year 2022 was a pivotal one. \uc2 Pivotal\uc2  because, after nearly three decades of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we evolved into a Capital Market Holding Company by restructuring some of our existing businesses whilst adding extra layers to our bouquet of financial service offerings.\par \par \uc2 Among the restructuring was the morphing of our Investment Research unit into a full-fledged consultancy with the internal capacity to solve all your business needs, setting up a Trusteeship as a business offering, and establishing Andromeda \u8211\'20\'13 our Fintech arm that will create a pathway for Afrinvest to become Africa's preferred digital investment bank. We also changed our brand identity into an agile, futuristic, and innovative logo that will remain relevant to your wealth-building journey as the world evolves.\par \par \uc2 Our new identity was officially unveiled at the Afrinvest Sparkle event, where we also celebrated 27 years of excellence in the Nigerian financial industry\u8239\'20\'2Fand many other milestones, including new business expansion initiatives, new leadership team, the launch of our 2022 Nigerian Banking Sector Report, and the launch of the Optimus \u8211\'20\'13 our digital investment and savings app.\par \par \uc2 Please permit me \u8211\'20\'13 for the umpteenth time \u8211\'20\'13 to gush over our new digital investment platform, Optimus. It excites me so much because we are now better positioned to be a part of your financial journey. With Optimus, you can now do more with money \u8211\'20\'13 save, invest and spend \u8211\'20\'13 on one simple and secure app. Future iterations of the app will have loads of amazing features, as our goal is to make all our financial service offerings available on the platform. If you're looking for a way to invest in Naira or Dollars, click HERE to take Optimus for a spin!\par \par \uc2 AAA Infrastructure Nigeria Limited (\u8220\'20\'1C3AInfra\u8221\'20\'1D), an infrastructure-focused, asset management company, was also established during the year by the trio of Afrinvest, Africa Plus Partners, and ARC Asset Management Limited. 3AInfra is one of four asset managers selected by Infrastructure Company of Nigeria Limited (\u8220\'20\'1CInfraCorp\u8221\'20\'1D) to harness opportunities for infrastructure development in Nigeria by originating, structuring, executing and managing end-to-end bankable projects in partnership with public and private sector participants. InfraCorp was established by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Nigeria\uc2 , the \uc2 Africa Finance Corporation\uc2 , and the Nigerian Sovereign Investment Authority who have seeded the vehicle with N1 trillion to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively across all sectors of \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s infrastructure space. 3AInfra will take part in the active co-management of InfraCorp to invest a targeted N15 trillion across infrastructure assets in Nigeria. This appointment again reaffirms Afrinvest's position as a first-in-class solutions-driven organization.\par \par \uc2 Afrinvest Asset Management Limited received an investment manager's rating from Agusto & Co and Global Credit Ratings (GCR), two major rating agencies in Nigeria.\u8239\'20\'2FThe ratings \u8211\'20\'13 A-(IM) from Agusto, BBB(NG) and A3(NG) from GCR \u8211\'20\'13 were awarded based on our \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  track record, good governance structure, good asset quality, experienced management team, and liquidity profile, amongst other considerations.\par \par \uc2 Our mutual funds also performed excellently, with Afrinvest Equity Fund and Afrinvest Plutus Fund ranking 1st and the \uc2 Nigerian International Debt Fund\uc2  holding the 2nd spot, in terms of returns for the most part of 2022.\u8239\'20\'2F\par \par \uc2 Afrinvestor, our online trading app, received a facelift! After much reengineering and retooling, Afrinvestor 2.0 finally launched in 2022. This improved version offers the latest personal investment technology, a wide variety of investment options, and a more user-friendly interface.\u8239\'20\'2F You can click HERE to download it on your mobile device and enjoy the new trading experience.\par \par \uc2 So, what should you expect from Afrinvest in 2023?\par \par \uc2 1. Business Expansion\par \par \uc2 As our digital presence continues to grow across various channels, we also plan to physically spread our tentacles to other areas of Nigeria. Last year, we officially opened our Onitsha office, our first-ever service point in the Southeast. In the same fashion, we will establish another office in a yet-to-be-disclosed location where clients can have physical access to all our service offerings, including investment banking, securities trading, asset management, trust services, and investment research.\par \par \uc2 2. More Technology-led Financial Solutions\par \par \uc2 Following the launch of Optimus by Afrinvest last year, an app that allows you to save and invest in Naira and Dollar, we are constantly working on bringing all your financial needs to your fingertip. This year, we expect to include other features such as trading US stocks, Mutual Funds, and Payments (virtual cards - Naira and Dollar) to the Optimus platform.\par \par \uc2 As we begin to consolidate on the strides achieved last year, we solicit your continued patronage and partnership. Just as you have kept faith in us since 1995, we pledge to continue providing all the support you need to reach your financial goals.\par \par \uc2 Our 27-year history has indeed been a series of innovations. We have kicked off this year with renewed zeal to further leverage the power of technology in driving wealth creation, protection and sustenance for YOU, our amazing clients.\par \par \uc2 Whether it's 2023 or 2050, our goal will always be to simplify wealth for you. That for us means offering the best possible investment advice with our vast experience, digital capabilities and rich bouquet of financial services.\par \par \uc2 We very much appreciate the opportunity to serve you, and I personally thank you, once again, for choosing to be a part of the Afrinvest Story!\par \par \uc2 I wish you a prosperous new year full of victories and accomplishments.\par \par \uc2 Yours sincerely,\par \par \uc2 Ike Chioke\par \par \uc2 Group Managing Director\par \par \uc2 *****\par \par \uc2 SeeNews does not endorse in any way, the views, opinions or recommendations expressed above. The use of the Information is subject to the terms and conditions as published by the original source, which you have to read and accept in full prior to the execution of any actions taken in reliance on Information contained herein.\par \par \uc2 Dokument BUINPR0020230130ej1u0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
