\par\par \b \uc2 Mtn Group Limited\uc2  Quarterly Update For The Period Ended 31 March 2022 And B-BBEE Annual Compliance Report\b0\par\par\uc2 7,949 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2022\par 07:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TIDM10CM \par Mtn Group Limited Quarterly Update For The Period Ended 31 March 2022 And B-BBEE Annual Compliance ReportQuarterly update for the period ended 31 March 2022 and B-BBEE annual compliance report \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Group Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par (Incorporated in the Republic of South Africa) \par (Registration number 1994/009584/06) \par (Share code MTN) \par (ISIN: ZAE000042164) \par (\f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN\f28 \uc2 \f2   or the Company or the Group) \par  \par Quarterly update for the period ended 31 March 2022 and B-BBEE annual \par compliance report \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN\f28 \uc2 \f2   is a pan-African mobile operator with the strategic intent of 'Leading digital solutions for Africa's \par progress'. We have 276 million customers in 19 markets and are inspired by our belief that everyone \par deserves the benefits of a modern connected life. \par  \par  \par Highlights \par -    Group service revenue grew by 15.9% \par          o Group voice revenue up by 2.6% \par          o Group data revenue up by 37.3% \par          o Group fintech revenue up by 21.2% \par -    Group earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation (EBITDA) up by 21.1% \par -    Group EBITDA margin improved to 46.4% (from 44.2%) \par -    MTN South Africa service revenue up by 4.6%, with an EBITDA margin of 39.9% (from 39.8%) \par -    \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2  service revenue up by 22.0%, with an EBITDA margin of 54.6% (from 53.1%) \par -    Subscribers increased by 3.2% to 276.2 million, impacted by new SIM registration regulations in \par      Nigeria (excluding Nigeria, subscribers were up by 3.9%) \par -    Active data subscribers increased by 13.1% to 125.6 million \par -    Active Mobile Money (MoMo) customers increased by 25.9% to 58.7 million \par -    MoMo value of transactions up by 12.6% to US$59.9 billion (up by 18.9% in constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) \par -    Medium-term guidance is maintained \par  \par Unless otherwise stated, financial and non-financial growth rates are presented on a constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basis and are year-on-year (YoY, \par 3M to March 2022 vs 3M to March 2021). To enable like-for-like comparability, all growth rates for non-financials have been restated to \par exclude \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Syria\f28 \uc2 \f2   and MTN Yemen, which were deconsolidated from 25 February 2021 and 31 October 2021 respectively. \par  \par Service revenue excludes device and SIM card revenue. Data revenue is mobile and fixed access data and excludes roaming and wholesale. \par Fintech includes MoMo, insurance, airtime lending and e-commerce. Active data users is a count of all subscribers at a point in time which \par had a revenue generating event in the specified period of time (90 days) prior to that point in time and also during the past 30 days had data \par usage greater than or equal to 5 megabytes. MoMo users are 30-day active users. \par  \par  \par  \par Group President and CEO \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ralph Mupita\f28 \uc2 \f2   comments: \par Challenging operating environment \par "The \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Group\f28 \uc2 \f2   delivered a solid Q1 2022 performance, with double-digit service revenue growth and \par the expansion of EBITDA margins supported by the focused execution of our Ambition 2025 strategy. \par  \par This was delivered in a challenging macroeconomic environment in which we continued to navigate \par the impacts of COVID-19. Many of our markets have also experienced rising inflation and interest rates, \par and pressure on consumer spending driven by recent global events impacting food and energy prices \par in particular. \par  \par Monetary and fiscal positions in many of the countries we operate in were challenged during the \par period, resulting in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakness against the US dollar. Additionally supply chains experienced \par disruptions, and to mitigate the risks of network rollout we accelerated capital expenditure in the \par quarter under review. \par  \par In South Africa, loadshedding from the national grid has impacted network availability and we have \par had to increase investment in batteries ahead of the winter period when we anticipate that the risk of \par loadshedding may remain high. \par  \par Solid financial and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  performance in Q1 \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  's service revenue came in ahead of medium-term targets and grew by 15.9%. Group \par EBITDA increased by 21.1%, with the EBITDA margin expanding by 2.2 percentage points (pp) to 46.4%. \par This performance was supported by service revenue growth at our large \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which was \par delivered in line with medium-term targets, and the disciplined execution of our expense efficiency \par programme. \par  \par In the quarter, the Group grew subscriber numbers by 3.2% YoY to 276.2 million, which was impacted \par by new SIM registration regulations in Nigeria. Excluding this impact, subscribers increased by 3.9%. \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2 , however, added 1.7 million subscribers during Q1, demonstrating good momentum on \par the 1 million in net additions reported for Q4 2021. \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2  is assisting with providing IT hardware \par resources to support the National Identity Management Commission (NIMC) with accelerating \par National Identity Number (NIN) validations. \par  \par In driving our industry-leading connectivity business, we recorded voice revenue growth of 2.6%. \par Data revenue grew by 37.3% driven by structurally higher demand for data services evidenced by the \par growth in data usage and active data users of 45.4% and 13.1% YoY, respectively. \par  \par In terms of our platforms, fintech revenue rose by 21.2% YoY. MoMo active monthly users grew by \par 25.9% YoY and the volume of transactions processed through our fintech platform was up 32.5% in Q1 \par 2022 YoY to 2.9 billion transactions. The value of transactions increased by 12.6% YoY to US$59.9 \par billion (up 18.9% in constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ). New commission-earning rules were implemented in Ghana \par during Q3 2021 to improve margins, which impacted transaction value. Adjusting for this, the \par underlying transaction value grew by 34.0% in constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  terms. \par  \par The number of active merchants accepting MoMo payments increasing by 46.8% YoY to 765 211 and \par the total value of MoMo merchant payments rose by 125.2% YoY to US$4.3 billion. The total value of \par remittances grew by 7.4% YoY to US$573.9 million in Q1 2022; in addition, we facilitated a total loan \par value of US$280.4 million, a 27.8% increase YoY. At the end of Q1 2022, our InsurTech platform had \par 17.4 million registered aYo policies, reflecting growth of 37.8% YoY \par  \par In April 2022, our fintech subsidiary in Nigeria received final approval of a MoMo Payment Service Bank \par (PSB) licence from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Nigeria (CBN) \f28 \uc2 \f2 . This will enable us to invest in the launch and \par rollout of PSB and offer a broader range of services in line with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2  's guidelines. We are committed \par  \par  \par to supporting the government of Nigeria to fulfil its agenda of driving digital and financial inclusion. \par We plan to commercially launch PSB services before the end of Q2 2022. \par  \par Strong balance sheet and liquidity positions \par The further deleveraging of our balance sheet is progressing well. Group leverage of 0.3x, was down \par from 0.4x as at 31 December 2021, comfortably within covenant limits. Holding company (Holdco) \par leverage remained within our medium-term target and improved further to 0.9x, from 1.0x as at 31 \par December 2021. We maintained a strong liquidity position, with headroom of R53.2 billion (R20.6 \par billion in cash, and R32.6 billion in committed undrawn facilities) as at 31 March 2021. \par  \par We upstreamed dividends and management fees amounting to R2.9 billion in Q1, including \par R643 million from Nigeria. Post the period-end, we upstreamed an additional R708 million of proceeds \par from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2  Series 1 public offer. This is a key milestone in the execution of our asset \par realisation programme (ARP), as we pursue our commitment to further localise the shareholding in \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \par  \par We were pleased to note \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2   Investor Services' recent upgrade, on 6 April 2022, to our credit \par rating outlook to 'stable' from 'negative' reflecting our strong financial performance and reduction in \par leverage. \par  \par We continued to invest in our 'second-to-none' networks and technology platforms, deploying \par R7.0 billion of capex in Q1. We were pleased to secure permanent high-demand spectrum in MTN \par South Africa (MTN SA) and paid R3.3 billion in April 2022 for the mid-band allotment of spectrum. This \par will enable us to ramp up investment to increase coverage, including 5G offering, and to support data \par traffic growth. \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Group\f28 \uc2 \f2   achieves Level 1 B-BBEE Contributor Status \par We are pleased that, in a major transformation milestone, \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Group\f28 \uc2 \f2   has for the first time achieved \par Level 1 Broad-Based Black Economic Empowerment (B-BBEE) Contributor Status. This is up from Level \par 2 in 2020. The rating, dated 10 May 2022, was for the financial year ended 31 December 2021. MTN \par SA maintained its Level 1 Contributor Status, for the third year running. \par  \par Group medium-term guidance maintained \par Looking ahead, we anticipate that our markets will be impacted by the global trends of higher interest \par rates and energy and food price inflation. Geopolitical volatility exacerbated by the conflict in Ukraine \par is further impacting these trends and affecting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , asset prices and supply chains \par  \par On current macro-economic and operating assumptions, we anticipate these factors to slightly \par moderate our EBITDA margin evolution in the short-term. This is particularly when considering the \par additional investment in Nigeria to accelerate NIN enrolment and verification, the launch of PSB \par services in Nigeria and higher energy related expenses where we have pass-through costs in Towerco \par agreements. \par  \par We remain focused on executing our Ambition 2025 strategy to drive growth, continue implementing \par our expense efficiency programme, deleverage the Holdco balance sheet faster and unlock value for \par our stakeholders. Our capex guidance for FY 2022 is unchanged at R34.4 billion and we are maintaining \par our Group enhanced medium-term guidance framework. \par  \par The Group's results and segmental report are presented in line with the Group's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  structure. The Group's underlying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par are clustered as follows: South Africa (SA), Nigeria, the Southern and East Africa (SEA) region, the West and Central Africa (WECA) region and \par the Middle East and North Africa (MENA) region and their respective underlying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par The SEA region includes Uganda, Zambia, Rwanda, South Sudan, Botswana (joint venture-equity accounted), eSwatini (joint venture-equity \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 13 May 2022 01:07 ET \par Mtn Group Limited Quarterly Update For The Period -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par accounted) and Business Group. The WECA region includes Ghana, Cameroon, C\u244\'00\'F4te d'Ivoire, Benin, Congo-Brazzaville, Liberia, Guinea \par Conakry and Guinea Bissau. The MENA region includes Iran (joint venture-equity accounted), Sudan and Afghanistan. \par  \par In line with the Group's strategy announced in March 2021, \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Ghana\f28 \uc2 \f2   results have been reported under the WECA region effective 1 \par January 2021 (previously included in the SEAGHA region). Prior year numbers have been restated for SEA and WECA accordingly. \par  \par Although Iran, Botswana and eSwatini form part of their respective regions geographically and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operationally\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , they are excluded from their \par respective regional results because they are equity accounted for by the Group. \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Syria\f28 \uc2 \f2   results have been disclosed up to February for 2021; as a result of loss of control effective February 2021, following \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Syria\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par being placed under judicial guardianship. MTN Yemen results have been disclosed up to end of October 2021, as a result of \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN\f28 \uc2 \f2  's decision \par to exit the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par Certain information presented in this quarterly update constitutes pro forma financial information. The responsibility for preparing and \par presenting the pro forma financial information and for the completeness and accuracy of the pro forma financial information is that of the \par directors of \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN\f28 \uc2 \f2  . This is presented for illustrative purposes only. Because of its nature, the pro forma financial information may not fairly \par present \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN\f28 \uc2 \f2  's financial position, changes in equity, and results of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or cash flows. It has not been audited or reviewed or otherwise \par reported on by our external joint auditors. The financial information on which this quarterly update is based, including constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par information, has not been reviewed and reported on by \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN\f28 \uc2 \f2  's external auditors. Constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  information has been presented to \par remove the impact of movement in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates on the Group's results and has been calculated by translating the prior financial reporting \par period's results at the current period's average rates. The measurement has been performed for each of the Group's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , materially \par being that of the US dollar and Nigerian naira. The constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  growth percentage has been calculated based on the prior period \par constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  results compared to the current year results. In addition, in respect of MTN Irancell, MTN Sudan and MTN South Sudan the \par constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  information has been prepared excluding the impact of hyperinflation. The economies of Sudan, South Sudan and Iran \par were assessed to be hyperinflationary for the period under review and hyperinflation accounting was applied. \par  \par This quarterly update may contain forward-looking information. Any forecast information on which the quarterly update may be based has \par not been reviewed or reported on by the Group's external auditors. \par  \par  \par Navigating an increasingly volatile macroeconomic environment \par We continue to navigate the impacts of COVID-19 in our markets with our response plan in place which \par focuses on the same areas that we have extensively reported on previously. These areas are social \par (our people, communities and stakeholders); commercial (including our customers); network and \par supply chain; and funding and liquidity. \par  \par We have seen an increase in macroeconomic volatility with rising fuel and food inflation, as well as \par local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  depreciation, feeding into many of our markets and compounding the pressure being \par felt by consumers. The Russia-Ukraine war has exacerbated these effects with additional stress on \par supply chains. \par  \par In terms of social impacts, we are working on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and support in addition to our ongoing \par programmes. To safeguard our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , we continue to manage inflationary risks through our \par expense efficiency programme and disciplined capital allocation. We had to increase pricing slightly in \par some markets, such as South Africa, to ensure the sustainability of the business. We will continue to \par offer competitive, quality services to our customers. \par  \par In terms of supply chain, comprehensive risk mitigation strategies have been in place for a number of \par years. We continue to enhance these to ensure that orders for equipment and supplies are secured \par early, with enhancements to procurement agreements to ensure availability across all our markets. \par These include discussions with our Towerco partners to manage the resilience of our networks; we \par have put in place plans to advance capex and fuel orders. \par  \par We have reduced foreign debt in our markets and at Holdco, which remains a focus, to insulate our \par balance sheet from exchange rate volatility. Deleveraging is a core objective of Ambition 2025 and we \par have also built up a strong liquidity position to provide us with flexibility to withstand any further \par shocks. \par  \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  review \par MTN South Africa \par MTN SA recorded a healthy performance in Q1 with service revenue increasing by 4.6% in line with \par our medium-term guidance. This result was supported by solid commercial and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  execution \par yielding service revenue growth in all the core business units: the prepaid consumer business unit \par (CBU) up 1.5%; the postpaid CBU up 4.4%; the enterprise business unit (EBU) up 23.1%; and the \par wholesale business up 1.9%. \par  \par This performance shows the resilience of the business, against the backdrop of a challenging \par macroeconomic environment, with a rapidly rising unemployment rate, and increased pressure on \par consumer disposable income. This environment has contributed to a notable shift in customer \par spending patterns and greater competition for share of the consumer's wallet. \par  \par MTN SA closed the quarter with a total of 34.5 million subscribers, an increase of 7.1%, driven by \par higher gross additions. The postpaid subscriber base continued to expand, with an increase of 7.4% to \par 7.5 million on the back of solid growth in integrated voice and data-centric plans. There was a 7.0% \par increase in prepaid subscribers to 27.0 million. \par  \par  \par MTN SA's drive to grow mobile data revenue led to data traffic growth of 46.7% and a 14.1% increase \par in active data users to 17.3 million. This underpinned a 14.3% increase in overall mobile data revenue. \par  \par Data was made more affordable in the quarter with a 22.8% YoY reduction in the effective data tariff. \par An active prepaid data subscriber now consumes an average of 4.0GB of data a month, up 37% YoY, \par and an active postpaid data subscriber uses nearly 11.8GB per month, an increase of 20%. \par  \par The consumer postpaid business delivered a resilient performance with service revenue growth of \par 4.4% benefiting from data-oriented packages. \par  \par The strong growth in data revenue also supported the consumer prepaid business' performance with \par service revenue up by 1.5%. Pressure from the offset of voice substitution remains a challenge and \par MTN SA continues to focus on ERM optimisation initiatives to reduce this impact. MTN SA's prepaid \par segment was also impacted by economic pressure, including job losses, particularly in the lower- \par income consumer segment. \par  \par The enterprise business recorded a 23.1% increase in service revenue, driven by sustained growth in \par data deals, Bulk SMS and the ICT business. The core mobile business continues to gain market share, \par with sustained distribution channel expansion and strong products propositions. In 2021, MTN SA was \par appointed as a service provider to the national government under the national treasury transversal \par contracts, enabling an expansion of our mobile service offerings to public sector entities through the \par leveraging of \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN\f28 \uc2 \f2  's strong network quality. \par  \par Wholesale revenue increased by 1.9%, supported by national roaming deals with Cell C and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Telkom\f28 \uc2 \f2  . \par For Cell C, revenue recognition remains on a cash basis as Cell C works towards its recapitalisation. \par MTN SA recognised R643 million (down 4.7% YoY) in national roaming revenue from Cell C and a \par balance of R400 million remained unrecognised, as at 31 March 2022. \par  \par The new multi-year national roaming agreement with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Telkom\f28 \uc2 \f2  , which came into effect on 1 November \par 2021, continues to scale steadily and MTN SA is focused on further enablement of infrastructure \par sharing through our investment in the expansion of network capacity. \par  \par The performance of the fintech business was encouraging with approximately 5.0 million registered \par MoMo users and 840 043 monthly active users by 31 March 2022. MTN SA also progressed work on \par MoMo partnerships to accelerate the growth of the business. The platform continues to grow \par transactions driven by innovative and relevant solutions. \par  \par MTN SA reported EBITDA of R4.9 billion (up 3.7% YoY) with as slight increase in the EBITDA margin to \par 39.9% (up 0.1pp), supported by a solid service revenue performance and disciplined execution of the \par expense efficiency programme. \par  \par Network availability and quality has been affected by loadshedding from the national grid, as well as \par ongoing theft and vandalism of towers since Q4 2021. To mitigate these impacts, MTN SA rolled out a \par comprehensive resilience plan including additional batteries, generators and enhanced security \par features. We are committed to ensuring the quality of our customers' connectivity experience. \par  \par Pleasingly, MTN SA secured high-demand spectrum to support the expansion of 4G and 5G services, \par with total permanent spectrum holdings rising from 76MHz to 176MHz and payment of R3.3 billion \par was made in April 2022 for mid-band spectrum. The spectrum has been allotted for a 20-year period \par on a technology-neutral basis. \par  \par Since the award of temporary spectrum in 2020, MTN SA has ramped up its investment to increase \par network coverage, improve speeds and enhance the overall customer experience. MTN SA has also \par invested in an expansion drive into rural and peri-urban areas and a major 5G rollout, reaching a \par population coverage of 15%. \par  \par MTN SA will continue to use the spectrum to expand 4G and 5G connectivity, as well as capacity, to \par entrench its position as South Africa's best network while further enhancing rural, peri-urban, and \par urban network coverage. The spectrum allocation will allow for greater value for customers, \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 13 May 2022 01:07 ET \par Mtn Group Limited Quarterly Update For The Period -3-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par supporting a lower cost to communicate as we work to bridge the digital divide. \par  \par MTN SA retained its top position as the country's most valuable brand in the 2022 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brand Finance\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par South Africa survey. For the eighth consecutive year, MTN SA maintained its leadership position with \par its brand value increasing by 34% to R59.8 billion. \par  \par In mid-April 2022, the province of KwaZulu-Natal (KZN) in South Africa felt the effects of heavy rainfall \par causing floods in many parts of KZN. In addition to the impact on lives and livelihoods, the floods \par caused extensive damage to infrastructure and power outages. A total of more than 500 \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN\f28 \uc2 \f2   sites \par were down in the province. MTN SA activated contingency plans and has since restored to full capacity \par around 90% of the affected sites. We continue to work to fully restore connectivity. \par  \par To support the people and municipalities in KZN hit the hardest by the floods, MTN SA donated a total \par of R1 million in of food parcels and blankets. To assist families and friends to keep in touch during this \par difficult time, MTN SA also provided free three-day SMS bundles to over seven million customers in \par KZN. \par  \par MTN SA's performance outlook will be shaped by macroeconomic conditions in the country such as \par loadshedding as well as current global macroeconomic uncertainties which are expected to add to \par inflationary pressures, including on costs and availability of food and fuel. This will further impact \par consumer spending power and patterns. \par  \par MTN SA is focused on accelerating its high growth business areas, with ongoing emphasis on the \par overall expense efficiency programme to deliver strong EBITDA and cash flow growth. \par  \par MTN Nigeria \par MTN Nigeria recorded a solid Q1 2022 performance, tracking positively against its medium-term \par targets, with 22.0% growth in service revenue and the expansion of EBITDA margins. This was \par delivered through solid commercial momentum and execution in challenging trading conditions. \par  \par Voice revenue grew by 8.6%, driven by an increase in gross connections and higher usage from the \par active SIM base. This was enabled by expanded customer acquisition touchpoints, rural telephony \par initiatives and an enhanced customer value management (CVM) toolkit, reducing the impact of the \par SIM registration and activation restrictions. \par  \par Data revenue rose by 54.0%, maintaining the accelerated growth trajectory through growth in the \par subscriber base and data usage. This was sustained by an aggressive 4G network expansion and \par enhanced quality and capacity of our network to support rising data traffic. Data traffic rose by 84.8% \par YoY and usage (MB per user) was up by 69.8%. \par  \par In addition, \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2  added about a million new smartphones to its network in Q1, bringing \par smartphone penetration to 50.0%. The 4G network now covers 71.7% of the population, up from \par 70.3% in December 2021. \par  \par Home broadband penetration remains a focus for \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2 , reaching over 775 000 users, up by \par over 160 000 in Q1. The goal is to Own the Home through differentiated value propositions, creating \par experiences that position \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2  as the broadband service provider of choice. This will be \par achieved by leveraging its unique assets and capabilities to capture a significant share of market \par growth. \par  \par Fintech revenue rose by 46.7% on the back of the growing adoption of fintech services and an \par expanded user base. The MoMo agent network continues to widen with more than 800k registered \par agents and over 166k active agents (agents who perform a minimum of ten revenue-generating \par transactions within 30 days). \par  \par Having established a significant foundation of registered agent network, the focus is now on scaling \par the active base. Agent services have been extended to include card withdrawal services by deploying \par point of sales (POS) terminals to the agent network. This has contributed to growing total volume of \par transactions to over 56.1 million, up 132.7% YoY, by more than 10.7 million active users (up 135.2%). \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2 's fintech subsidiary received final approval of a MoMo PSB licence from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2  . This \par will enable the Operating company (Opco) to invest in the launch and rollout of PSB and offer a \par broader range of services, with a planned commercial launch before the end of Q2 2022. \par  \par Digital revenue grew by 37.4% as penetration of digital products continued to deepen, driven by \par increased usage from the active base. Digital subscriptions were up by 164.1% YoY to 7.4 million, with \par ayoba accounting for 45% of the subscriptions and Rich Media accounting for 55%. \par  \par Revenue from digital services was driven by Rich Media, Mobile Advertising and Content VAS. The \par Y'ello Digital Platforms are being transformed into a one-stop destination that caters to all consumer \par segments. \par  \par Revenue from the enterprise business rose by 34.0%, underpinned by the onboarding of new \par customers across segments and the uptake of our enhanced services. The enterprise business is \par transitioning from product to platform while leveraging core mobile and fixed connectivity to serve \par customers across all segments better. \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2  has commenced executing programmes to digitise and transform micro, small and \par medium enterprises (MSMEs) in Nigeria. Key among these is developing a cloud technology \par marketplace to empower, educate and enrich MSMEs. The goal is to drive further adoption of \par enterprise platforms (internet of things and cloud), creating additional value for our customers and \par enabling them to innovate while remaining profitable. \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2  progressed with the expense efficiency programme and remained disciplined with capital \par allocation. As a result, the Opco contained the 18.3% increase in operating expenses below the rate \par of inflation in Nigeria. This was achieved despite the continued effects of naira depreciation on lease \par rental costs and acceleration in site rollout. Consequently, \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2 's ability to drive operating \par leverage has enabled EBITDA growth of 25.7% and the expansion of EBITDA margin by 1.5pp to 54.6%. \par  \par Southern and East Africa (SEA) \par The SEA region delivered service revenue growth of 12.7% for the quarter, supported by top-line \par growth in most markets despite challenging trading conditions including muted economic recovery \par and higher inflation. The region's performance reflected strong growth in data revenue (up 35.6%) \par and fintech revenue (up 26.8%). The SEA region increased subscriber numbers by 2.8% YoY to 34.8 \par million. \par  \par MTN Uganda recorded service revenue growth of 11.1%. This was driven by solid growth in data \par revenue (up 45.1%) and fintech revenue (up 21.0%) resulting from increased usage and subscriber \par base growth. Voice revenue (down 4.6%) was adversely impacted by customers' disposable incomes \par coming under pressure from rising inflation. Despite the tough trading conditions, the Opco delivered \par EBITDA margin expansion of 0.4pp to 51.6% on the back of revenue growth and the effective \par implementation of expense efficiencies. \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Rwanda\f28 \uc2 \f2   delivered strong service revenue growth of 24.5%, supported by healthy growth in \par fintech revenue (up 56.8%), data revenue (up 13.2%) and voice revenue (up 11.8%). \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Rwanda\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par reported an EBITDA margin of 47.7%, down 3.7pp. This was impacted mainly by higher data cost of \par sales from higher 4G uptake as well as the introduction of an outsourced modernised Network \par Operating Centre to manage the monitoring of our network, boost the user experience and reduce \par the volume of customer complaint calls. \par  \par Overall the SEA portfolio reported a 1.3pp decrease in the blended EBITDA margin to 46.0% in Q1 \par 2022. \par  \par The results of our associate investment in eSwatini reflected increased pressure on voice and data in \par a challenging economic environment, amid increased competition and return-to-office work, which \par reduced demand for data. Plans are in place to accelerate demand through CVM and competitive \par offers. \par  \par West and Central Africa (WECA) \par Despite the challenging trading environment, the WECA region delivered a pleasing performance for \par the quarter with service revenue growth of 17.0%. The strong performance was enabled by an uptake \par of data services. Fintech revenue remained resilient despite aggressive pricing strategies from \par competitors in some markets. The region's performance also benefitted from a 8.2% increase in the \par subscriber base to 70.2 million. WECA delivered a blended EBITDA margin of 43.5%, up by 2.3pp, \par mainly driven by \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Ghana\f28 \uc2 \f2  . \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Ghana\f28 \uc2 \f2  's Q1 performance is testament to the resilience of the business to withstand a challenging \par macro-economic environment including increases in fuel prices and general inflation as well as local \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  depreciation. \par  \par Service revenue grew by 34.7%, largely attributable to growth in voice, data and fintech. This \par performance was underpinned by the continued execution of the Ambition 2025 strategy and \par sustained investments to support improvements in service quality and the expansion of network \par capacity and coverage. \par  \par An increase in active data users and usage led to an increase of 65.3% in data revenue. Fintech revenue \par grew by 27.1%, supported by higher active MoMo users. The EBITDA margin increased by 5.0pp to \par 59.7% on the back of the strong growth in the topline and the implementation of the expense \par efficiency programme. \par  \par MTN C\u244\'00\'F4te d'Ivoire's performance came under pressure with service revenue slightly down by 0.7% \par largely impacted by the decline in fintech revenue (down 38.4%) due to zero-rating of person-to- \par person (P2P) and price reductions for withdrawals driven by intensified competition in the market \par during H2 2021. This helped to accelerate user and usage growth in Q4 2021, with that momentum \par continuing Q1 2022 during which over 506 000 users were added in C\u244\'00\'F4te d'Ivoire. The business \par remains on a strong foundation to grow over the medium-term. \par  \par The decline in fintech revenue in Q1 was mitigated by a strong performance in data revenue, which \par grew by 24.9%, underpinned by initiatives to accelerate data penetration and usage. Encouragingly, \par the Opco continued to gain value share in the market. The EBITDA margin declined by 1.3pp to 33.0% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 13 May 2022 01:07 ET \par Mtn Group Limited Quarterly Update For The Period -4-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par due to the decline in the topline while maintaining the same commissions to the distribution network \par in fintech. \par  \par MTN Cameroon recorded service revenue growth of 12.9% in a challenging environment where the \par Opco leads on market and value share. This performance was supported by sustained growth in active \par data users and ongoing CVM initiatives which led to growth in data revenue (up 30.8%). Revenue \par growth in fintech (up 19.9%) and digital (up 28.1%) also aided overall service revenue growth. The \par EBITDA margin declined by 2.7pp to 33.7%, impacted by management fees and provisions for taxes \par on MoMo transfers and withdrawals. \par  \par Excluding \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Ghana\f28 \uc2 \f2  , the WECA markets grew service revenue by 6.7%, which remained ahead of \par blended inflation of 4.7%, with a 1.8pp decline in EBITDA margin to 31.5% in Q1 2022 largely reflecting \par the pressure in MTN C\u244\'00\'F4te d'Ivoire and MTN Cameroon. \par  \par Middle East and North Africa (MENA) \par The MENA region delivered a strong performance with service revenue growth of 83.4% (excluding \par MTN Irancell) notwithstanding tough macroeconomic conditions including \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  devaluation, \par inflationary impacts, sustained higher oil prices and higher food prices as well as food shortages. The \par region's performance was mainly supported by strong growth in data revenue and an increase in \par active data users. The total number of subscribers (excluding MTN Irancell) was 15.2 million. \par  \par MTN Sudan increased service revenue by 261.9%, underpinned by growth in voice (up 200.8%) and \par data (up 387.9%) revenue on the back of increased voice and data prices. The EBITDA margin \par expanded by 17.4pp to 51.4%, driven by strong growth in revenue as well as savings in maintenance \par costs. \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN\f28 \uc2 \f2   Irancell, our equity-accounted joint venture, delivered solid results through strong commercial \par execution of various initiatives. Service revenue grew by 26.6%, supported by increased data revenue. \par The EBITDA margin expanded by 3.6pp to 42.8% driven by strong revenue growth and expense \par efficiencies. \par  \par This result was delivered in a challenging environment where the Russia-Ukraine war is impacting the \par growth and economic development of the country. While the GDP of the country stands to benefit \par from the increased oil and gas prices, rising inflation will place pressure on the consumer. \par  \par The Iran Internet Group's ride-hailing app, Snapp, remained the market leader, ranking among the \par top ride-hailing apps globally with over three million daily rides. Snapp Box is the leading last-mile \par delivery network in the country. Market-leading food delivery app Snappfood orders grew by 61.0% \par YoY to almost 260 000 daily orders. Snapp Market orders grew by 88.9% YoY and remains the leading \par supermarket delivery app in the country. \par  \par Building the largest and most valuable fintech platform \par We continue to scale and accelerate our fintech ecosystem across our markets with a 32.5% increase \par in transaction volumes to 2.9 billion transactions in Q1, with the value of transactions up by 12.6% to \par US$59.9 billion (up 18.9% in constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ). This includes the impact of new commission-earning \par rules which were implemented in Ghana during Q3 2021, adjusting for which, underlying transaction \par value growth would have been 34.0% in constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  terms. \par  \par We responded to competitive pressures in Cote d'Ivoire by reducing P2P and withdrawals pricing, \par which affected service revenue development. However we are encouraged by the user growth \par recovery we have seen in Q1 2022 in that market. We remain confident of our recovery plan in Cote \par d'Ivoire where we anticipate fintech service revenue to be back in growth by Q4 2022 driven by user \par growth and the launch of advanced services. Overall fintech service revenue grew by 21.1% in \par Q1 2022. We are driving recurring usage with a focus on new product launches to grow advanced \par services in line with our strategy. \par  \par There was strong traction in growing our payments and e-commerce ecosystem, leveraging our \par strength in distribution. The number of active merchants accepting MoMo payments increased by \par 46.8% to 765 211 and the total value of MoMo merchant payments rose by 125.2% US$4.3 billion. We \par will continue the roll-out of self-onboarding of merchants to entrench the momentum and growth in \par our payments and e-commerce vertical. \par  \par We facilitated a total loan value of US$280.4 million, a 27.8% YoY increase, taking advantage of our \par scaled mobile wallet business and customer footprint. The total value of remittances grew by 7.4% \par YoY to US$573.9 million in Q1 2022. \par  \par At the end of Q1 2022, our InsurTech platform had 17.4 million registered aYo policies, reflecting \par growth of 37.8% YoY. Active policies reduced by 31.1% YoY due to a shift in the strategy to focus on a \par higher average revenue per policy and new revenue streams, particularly in Uganda, resulting in the \par termination of free policies. Active policy growth was also impacted by platform migration and \par integration challenges as we shifted to a proprietary infrastructure. In the year ahead we anticipate a \par return to growth in active paid policies with higher average revenue per policy. \par  \par The final approval of a MoMo PSB licence in Nigeria by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBN\f28 \uc2 \f2   will enable us to invest in the launch, \par before the end of Q2 2022, and rollout of PSB and offer a broader range of services. In terms of \par partnerships, our joint venture (JV) with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sanlam\f28 \uc2 \f2   is undergoing regulatory approvals across the various \par markets we operate in and we anticipate launching the JV in early H2 2022. \par  \par Asset realisation programme (ARP) and portfolio transformation \par Our ARP aims to reduce debt, simplify our portfolio, reduce risk and improve returns. \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN\f28 \uc2 \f2  's broader \par portfolio transformation plan is to accelerate these goals in line with our focus on pan-Africa, support \par our shared value objectives and structure the business to unlock value. \par  \par To date, the Group has received net proceeds of R708 million, post the period-end, from the Series 1 \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2  public offer. For the sale of MTN SA passive tower infrastructure, we are expecting to \par receive R5.2 billion in net proceeds from the transaction in Q2 2022. \par  \par The further localisation of \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Ghana\f28 \uc2 \f2   is in progress and we have increased our localisation from \par 17.5% to 18.7% in Q1, with new participation from some local pension funds. The Group has received \par cash proceeds of R272 million from this transaction. Post the quarter-end, we concluded a transaction \par to localise a further 5% of \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Ghana\f28 \uc2 \f2  , bringing the total localisation to 23.7%. We are committed to \par deepen local participation to achieve 30% localisation for \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Ghana\f28 \uc2 \f2  . \par  \par We continue to explore options to exit Afghanistan in an orderly manner. \par  \par Update on regulatory and legal considerations \par High-demand radio frequency in South Africa \par To support its 4G and 5G expansion drive, in March 2022, MTN SA secured high-demand radio \par frequency spectrum in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Independent Communications Authority of South Africa\f28 \uc2 \f2  's (\f28 \uc2 \f2 ICASA\f28 \uc2 \f2  ) landmark \par auction. This increased MTN SA's total permanent spectrum holdings from 76MHz to 176MHz. \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN\f28 \uc2 \f2   secured 2 x 10MHz of 800MHz, 4 x 10MHz of 2 600MHz and 4 x 10MHz of 3 500MHz for \par R5.2 billion, comparing favourably with global benchmark averages. This was the first time in 17 years \par that \f28 \uc2 \f2 ICASA\f28 \uc2 \f2   had released spectrum on a permanent basis, allotting it for 20 years on a technology- \par neutral basis. A payment of R3.3 billion was made in April 2022, for the mid-band spectrum. A \par payment of R1.9 billion is expected to be made in Q3 2022 for the low-band spectrum. \par  \par MTN SA will use the spectrum to expand 4G and 5G connectivity and capacity and further enhance \par rural, peri-urban and urban network coverage. The spectrum will also support our work to lower the \par cost to communicate. \par  \par SIM registration in Nigeria \par Following the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nigeria Communications Commission\f28 \uc2 \f2  's (NCC) directive on 4 April 2022 that all operators \par in Nigeria should restrict outgoing calls for subscribers whose SIMs are not associated with NINs, \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN \par Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2  implemented the directive on approximately 19 million affected subscribers at that date. \par However, subscribers whose outgoing calls were restricted for non-submission of their NINs require \par their NINs to be verified before being reactivated. \par  \par As at 25 April 2022, about 8.7 million of those restricted submitted their NINs for verification, of which \par approximately 1.2 million have been reactivated, many of whom are high-value customers. Outgoing \par voice revenue from the subscribers who are yet to be reactivated therefore contributed approximately \par 7.8% of total service revenue based on FY 2021 for \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \par  \par In terms of how general traffic trends have evolved since the implementation of the directive, \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN \par Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2  saw a drop in traffic following its compliance with the directive. However, as we execute our \par NIN recovery initiatives, there has been a gradual turnaround as the affected subscribers are \par reconnected to resume voice calls. Data revenue has continued a steady increase but has currently \par not fully compensated for the decline in voice revenue of restricted subscribers. Encouragingly, there \par has been an acceleration in new connections. \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2  continues to engage affected subscribers while providing hardware support for the NIMC \par to accelerate the verification of NINs submitted. It has taken significant steps to help drive NIN \par enrolment for subscribers who do not yet have a NIN, deploying over 4 200 points of enrolment across \par the country. \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2  has also provided multiple channels to enable customers to submit their \par NIN for verification and linkage. \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Ghana\f28 \uc2 \f2   e-levy \par On 29 March 2022, Ghana's parliament approved the Electronic Transfer Levy Bill. From 1 May 2022, \par a 1.5% e-levy will apply to selected electronic transactions of more than GHS100 per customer per day \par on Mobile Money, bank transfers, merchant payments and inward remittances. \par  \par The levy is a reduction from the 1.75% communicated previously through the 2022 Budget Statement \par and Economic Policy of Ghana. To reduce the impact of the levy on customers, from May 2022, \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN \par Ghana\f28 \uc2 \f2   is reducing fees on P2P transactions by 25%. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 13 May 2022 01:07 ET \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mtn Group Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2   Quarterly Update For The Period -5-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MTN Ghana\f28 \uc2 \f2   classification as a significant market power \par Following the \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Communications Authority\f28 \uc2 \f2  's (NCA) 2020 classification of \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Ghana\f28 \uc2 \f2   as a \par significant market power, the Opco has now implemented four of the seven '\f28 \uc2 \f2 SMP\f28 \uc2 \f2  remedies' being \par national roaming. \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Ghana\f28 \uc2 \f2   remains in constructive discussions with the NCA on the outstanding \par remedies. \par  \par Outlook \par We are encouraged by the healthy performance delivered across our markets in Q1 2022, despite \par challenging macro-economic conditions and new regulatory developments. Looking ahead, our \par markets will be impacted by rising inflation, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility, increased pressure on disposable \par income for consumers and further potential supply chain disruptions. \par  \par In this context, our medium-term outlook remains supported by the structurally higher demand for \par data and fintech services and \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN\f28 \uc2 \f2   is uniquely positioned to capture that growth. Our priority is to \par execute on the Ambition 2025 strategy to drive this growth as well as deleverage the Holdco balance \par sheet faster and unlock value, as we manage the rising risks and macroeconomic uncertainties we are \par seeing. \par  \par We are focused on accelerating growth in MTN SA and \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2  as we drive to be the industry- \par leading connectivity business. In addition to the macroeconomic pressures, MTN SA remains impacted \par by the continued disruptions to power supply from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Eskom\f28 \uc2 \f2   and the residual effects of the recent floods \par in the KZN province. . The SA prepaid consumer is under pressure, but we remain committed to drive \par overall service revenue growth in line with our medium-term guidance for MTN SA. \par  \par The focus in Nigeria will be on driving ongoing growth in the subscriber and revenue base in the \par context of prevailing SIM registration restrictions, as well as launching PSB services. While the EBITDA \par margin may be impacted in the near-term by these effects, we anticipate continued growth in service \par revenue of 'at least 20%', as per our medium-term guidance. \par  \par Within fintech, during Q1, we implemented new mobile money taxes in Benin and Cameroon; we also \par made further P2P pricing adjustments in response to ongoing competition in C\u244\'00\'F4te d'Ivoire to continue \par to stimulate user and usage growth. We also implemented the E-levy tax in Ghana and adjusted our \par pricing to limit the impact on usage in that market. \par  \par While these factors will have an impact on our performance, we remain on course to fulfil our fintech \par ambitions and our mission to drive financial inclusion by leveraging our platform data and capabilities. \par The final award of the PSB licence in Nigeria is a major milestone and will be key in driving our fintech \par growth over the medium-term. We are planning the commercial launch of PSB services before the end \par of Q2 2022. \par  \par On current macroeconomic conditions and operating assumptions, rising inflation in our markets and \par specific \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  investment in markets like \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Nigeria\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (NIN enrolments and the \par launch of PSB services), are anticipated to slightly dampen our EBITDA margin expansion in the near- \par term. \par  \par The expense efficiency programme is ongoing and supporting the overall profitability of the business. \par We will thus continue to execute on this programme to complement our topline growth in achieving \par our objective to drive further EBITDA margin expansion and return improvement over the medium- \par term. \par  \par In terms of some of our key strategic programmes, the work to structurally separate our fintech and \par fibre businesses is ongoing and remains on track. \par  \par To achieve the accelerated growth across our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the investment in sustaining our 'second- \par to-none' networks and platforms will remain a priority. To support our objectives, we plan to spend \par R34.4 billion in capex in FY 2022, which is unchanged from our previous guidance. \par  \par With the outlook for global economic growth remaining uncertain amid the increased geopolitical \par volatility, we will remain focused on delivering on our strategy and maintaining our position as a \par partner of choice to stakeholders across our markets. Our strategy is robust and we have adequate \par adaptability and resilience built into our operating model to weather the uncertainties. We maintain \par our enhanced Group medium-term guidance. \par  \par B-BBEE annual compliance report -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Group\f28 \uc2 \f2   and subsidiaries: \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Group\f28 \uc2 \f2   achieves \par Level 1 B-BBEE Contributor Status \par We are pleased that, in a major transformation milestone, \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Group\f28 \uc2 \f2   has for the first time achieved \par Level 1 Broad-Based Black Economic Empowerment (B-BBEE) Contributor Status. This is up from Level \par 2 in 2020. The rating, dated 10 May 2022, was for the financial year ended 31 December 2021. The B- \par BBEE Contributor Status for \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Group\f28 \uc2 \f2   considers both the South African operating entities as well as \par the South African group function. MTN SA maintained its Level 1 Contributor Status, for the third year \par running. \par  \par In accordance with paragraph 16.21(g) and Appendix 1 to Section 11 of the JSE Listings Requirements \par and Section 13G(2) of the Broad Based Black Economic Empowerment Act, the Company's B-BBEE \par compliance certificate and compliance report is available on the Company's website at \par  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mtn.com/investors-shareholders/?tablink=b-bbee-certificate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.mtn.com/investors-shareholders/?tablink=b-bbee-certificate }}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par  \par The full financial results are available on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN\f28 \uc2 \f2  's website at: \par  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mtn.com/investors/financial-results/?report_cat=quarterly-results"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.mtn.com/investors/financial-results/?report_cat=quarterly-results }}}\uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par Abbreviations: \par SIM: Subscriber Identity/Identification Module \par Business Group: Consist of internet service providers in Namibia, Kenya and Botswana \par Towerco: Tower companies \par Holdco leverage: Holdco net debt (including MTN GC)/SA EBITDA + cash upstreaming \par PSB: Payment service bank \par CVM: Customer value management \par ERM: Effective rate per minute \par MB: Megabyte \par GB: Gigabyte \par  \par  \par  \par 13 May 2022 \par Fairland \par  \par Lead sponsor \par  \par Tamela Holdings Proprietary Limited \par  \par Joint sponsor \par  \par JP Morgan Equities (SA) Proprietary Limited \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \par Date: 13-05-2022 07:05:00 \par Produced by the JSE SENS Department. The SENS service is an information dissemination service administered by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 JSE Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2   ('\f28 \uc2 \f2 JSE\f28 \uc2 \f2  '). \par The \f28 \uc2 \f2 JSE\f28 \uc2 \f2   does not, whether expressly, tacitly or implicitly, represent, warrant or in any way guarantee the truth, accuracy or completeness of \par  the information published on SENS. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 JSE\f28 \uc2 \f2  , their officers, employees and agents accept no liability for (or in respect of) any direct, \par indirect, incidental or consequential loss or damage of any kind or nature, howsoever arising, from the use of SENS or the use of, or reliance on, \par  information disseminated through SENS. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 13 May 2022 01:08 ET *\f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN GROUP LIMITED\f28 \uc2 \f2   1Q Service Revenue Up 15.9%\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 13 May 2022 01:09 ET *\f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Group\f28 \uc2 \f2   1Q Ebitda Up 21.1%\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 13 May 2022 01:09 ET *\f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Group\f28 \uc2 \f2   Ebitda Margin Improved to 46.4%\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 13 May 2022 01:10 ET *\f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Group\f28 \uc2 \f2   Maintains Medium-term Guidance\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 13 May 2022 01:11 ET *\f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Group\f28 \uc2 \f2   Grew Subscriber Numbers by 3.2% On-year\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 13 May 2022 01:26 ET \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Group\f28 \uc2 \f2   1Q Revenue Grew 16%; Maintains Medium-Term Guidance\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Ian Walker\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MTN Group Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2  . on Friday reported a 16% rise in group revenue for the first quarter and backed its medium-term guidance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South Africa-based telecommunications group said that voice revenue for the quarter ended March 31 grew 2.6%, while data revenue rose 37%. Group fintec revenue rose 21% during the quarter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Group earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortization rose 21%, while Ebitda margin improved to 46.4%, from 44.2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MTN\f28 \uc2 \f2  said that subscriber numbers grew 3.2% to 276.2 million, although this was hurt by new SIM registration regulations in Nigeria.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Looking ahead, we anticipate that our markets will be impacted by the global trends of higher interest rates and energy and food price inflation. Geopolitical volatility exacerbated by the conflict in Ukraine is further impacting these trends and affecting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , asset prices and supply chains," the company said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Ian Walker at ian.walker@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 May 13, 2022 01:26 ET (05:26 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220513ei5d000n5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw90\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1350\pichgoal600
