\par\par \b \uc2 MTN Group Limited\uc2  - Quarterly update for the period ended 31 March 2022 and B-BBEE annual compliance report\b0\par\par\uc2 7,612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2022\par 09:05\par \uc2 Johannesburg Stock Exchange\par \uc2 JSEXCH\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2022 Johannesburg Stock Exchange. All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Quarterly update for the period ended 31 March 2022 and B-BBEE annual compliance report\par \par \uc2 MTN Group Limited\par \par \uc2 (Incorporated in the Republic of South Africa)\par \par \uc2 (Registration number 1994/009584/06)\par \par \uc2 (Share code MTN)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 MTN\uc2  or the Company or the Group)\par \par \uc2 Quarterly update for the period ended 31 March 2022 and B-BBEE annual\par \par \uc2 compliance report\par \par \uc2 MTN\uc2  is a pan-African mobile operator with the strategic intent of \u8216\'20\'18Leading digital solutions for Africa\u8217\'20\'19s\par \par \uc2 progress\u8217\'20\'19. We have 276 million customers in 19 markets and are inspired by our belief that everyone\par \par \uc2 deserves the benefits of a modern connected life.\par \par \uc2 Highlights\par \par \uc2 - Group service revenue grew by 15.9%\par \par \uc2 o Group voice revenue up by 2.6%\par \par \uc2 o Group data revenue up by 37.3%\par \par \uc2 o Group fintech revenue up by 21.2%\par \par \uc2 - Group earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation (EBITDA) up by 21.1%\par \par \uc2 - Group EBITDA margin improved to 46.4% (from 44.2%)\par \par \uc2 - MTN South Africa service revenue up by 4.6%, with an EBITDA margin of 39.9% (from 39.8%)\par \par \uc2 - \uc2 MTN Nigeria\uc2  service revenue up by 22.0%, with an EBITDA margin of 54.6% (from 53.1%)\par \par \uc2 - Subscribers increased by 3.2% to 276.2 million, impacted by new SIM registration regulations in\par \par \uc2 Nigeria (excluding Nigeria, subscribers were up by 3.9%)\par \par \uc2 - Active data subscribers increased by 13.1% to 125.6 million\par \par \uc2 - Active Mobile Money (MoMo) customers increased by 25.9% to 58.7 million\par \par \uc2 - MoMo value of transactions up by 12.6% to US$59.9 billion (up by 18.9% in constant \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 - Medium-term guidance is maintained\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise stated, financial and non-financial growth rates are presented on a constant \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basis and are year-on-year (YoY,\par \par \uc2 3M to March 2022 vs 3M to March 2021). To enable like-for-like comparability, all growth rates for non-financials have been restated to\par \par \uc2 exclude \uc2 MTN Syria\uc2  and MTN Yemen, which were deconsolidated from 25 February 2021 and 31 October 2021 respectively.\par \par \uc2 Service revenue excludes device and SIM card revenue. Data revenue is mobile and fixed access data and excludes roaming and wholesale.\par \par \uc2 Fintech includes MoMo, insurance, airtime lending and e-commerce. Active data users is a count of all subscribers at a point in time which\par \par \uc2 had a revenue generating event in the specified period of time (90 days) prior to that point in time and also during the past 30 days had data\par \par \uc2 usage greater than or equal to 5 megabytes. MoMo users are 30-day active users.\par \par \uc2 Group President and CEO \uc2 Ralph Mupita\uc2  comments:\par \par \uc2 Challenging operating environment\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe \uc2 MTN Group\uc2  delivered a solid Q1 2022 performance, with double-digit service revenue growth and\par \par \uc2 the expansion of EBITDA margins supported by the focused execution of our Ambition 2025 strategy.\par \par \uc2 This was delivered in a challenging macroeconomic environment in which we continued to navigate\par \par \uc2 the impacts of COVID-19. Many of our markets have also experienced rising inflation and interest rates,\par \par \uc2 and pressure on consumer spending driven by recent global events impacting food and energy prices\par \par \uc2 in particular.\par \par \uc2 Monetary and fiscal positions in many of the countries we operate in were challenged during the\par \par \uc2 period, resulting in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness against the US dollar. Additionally supply chains experienced\par \par \uc2 disruptions, and to mitigate the risks of network rollout we accelerated capital expenditure in the\par \par \uc2 quarter under review.\par \par \uc2 In South Africa, loadshedding from the national grid has impacted network availability and we have\par \par \uc2 had to increase investment in batteries ahead of the winter period when we anticipate that the risk of\par \par \uc2 loadshedding may remain high.\par \par \uc2 Solid financial and \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  performance in Q1\par \par \uc2 MTN Group\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s service revenue came in ahead of medium-term targets and grew by 15.9%. Group\par \par \uc2 EBITDA increased by 21.1%, with the EBITDA margin expanding by 2.2 percentage points (pp) to 46.4%.\par \par \uc2 This performance was supported by service revenue growth at our large \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was\par \par \uc2 delivered in line with medium-term targets, and the disciplined execution of our expense efficiency\par \par \uc2 programme.\par \par \uc2 In the quarter, the Group grew subscriber numbers by 3.2% YoY to 276.2 million, which was impacted\par \par \uc2 by new SIM registration regulations in Nigeria. Excluding this impact, subscribers increased by 3.9%.\par \par \uc2 MTN Nigeria\uc2 , however, added 1.7 million subscribers during Q1, demonstrating good momentum on\par \par \uc2 the 1 million in net additions reported for Q4 2021. \uc2 MTN Nigeria\uc2  is assisting with providing IT hardware\par \par \uc2 resources to support the National Identity Management Commission (NIMC) with accelerating\par \par \uc2 National Identity Number (NIN) validations.\par \par \uc2 In driving our industry-leading connectivity business, we recorded voice revenue growth of 2.6%.\par \par \uc2 Data revenue grew by 37.3% driven by structurally higher demand for data services evidenced by the\par \par \uc2 growth in data usage and active data users of 45.4% and 13.1% YoY, respectively.\par \par \uc2 In terms of our platforms, fintech revenue rose by 21.2% YoY. MoMo active monthly users grew by\par \par \uc2 25.9% YoY and the volume of transactions processed through our fintech platform was up 32.5% in Q1\par \par \uc2 2022 YoY to 2.9 billion transactions. The value of transactions increased by 12.6% YoY to US$59.9\par \par \uc2 billion (up 18.9% in constant \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ). New commission-earning rules were implemented in Ghana\par \par \uc2 during Q3 2021 to improve margins, which impacted transaction value. Adjusting for this, the\par \par \uc2 underlying transaction value grew by 34.0% in constant \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  terms.\par \par \uc2 The number of active merchants accepting MoMo payments increasing by 46.8% YoY to 765 211 and\par \par \uc2 the total value of MoMo merchant payments rose by 125.2% YoY to US$4.3 billion. The total value of\par \par \uc2 remittances grew by 7.4% YoY to US$573.9 million in Q1 2022; in addition, we facilitated a total loan\par \par \uc2 value of US$280.4 million, a 27.8% increase YoY. At the end of Q1 2022, our InsurTech platform had\par \par \uc2 17.4 million registered aYo policies, reflecting growth of 37.8% YoY\par \par \uc2 In April 2022, our fintech subsidiary in Nigeria received final approval of a MoMo Payment Service Bank\par \par \uc2 (PSB) licence from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Nigeria (CBN)\uc2 . This will enable us to invest in the launch and\par \par \uc2 rollout of PSB and offer a broader range of services in line with the \uc2 CBN\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s guidelines. We are committed\par \par \uc2 to supporting the government of Nigeria to fulfil its agenda of driving digital and financial inclusion.\par \par \uc2 We plan to commercially launch PSB services before the end of Q2 2022.\par \par \uc2 Strong balance sheet and liquidity positions\par \par \uc2 The further deleveraging of our balance sheet is progressing well. Group leverage of 0.3x, was down\par \par \uc2 from 0.4x as at 31 December 2021, comfortably within covenant limits. Holding company (Holdco)\par \par \uc2 leverage remained within our medium-term target and improved further to 0.9x, from 1.0x as at 31\par \par \uc2 December 2021. We maintained a strong liquidity position, with headroom of R53.2 billion (R20.6\par \par \uc2 billion in cash, and R32.6 billion in committed undrawn facilities) as at 31 March 2021.\par \par \uc2 We upstreamed dividends and management fees amounting to R2.9 billion in Q1, including\par \par \uc2 R643 million from Nigeria. Post the period-end, we upstreamed an additional R708 million of proceeds\par \par \uc2 from the \uc2 MTN Nigeria\uc2  Series 1 public offer. This is a key milestone in the execution of our asset\par \par \uc2 realisation programme (ARP), as we pursue our commitment to further localise the shareholding in\par \par \uc2 MTN Nigeria\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We were pleased to note \uc2 Moody\u8217\'20\'19s\uc2  Investor Services\u8217\'20\'19 recent upgrade, on 6 April 2022, to our credit\par \par \uc2 rating outlook to \u8216\'20\'18stable\u8217\'20\'19 from \u8216\'20\'18negative\u8217\'20\'19 reflecting our strong financial performance and reduction in\par \par \uc2 leverage.\par \par \uc2 We continued to invest in our \u8216\'20\'18second-to-none\u8217\'20\'19 networks and technology platforms, deploying\par \par \uc2 R7.0 billion of capex in Q1. We were pleased to secure permanent high-demand spectrum in \uc2 MTN\par \par \uc2 South Africa (MTN SA) and paid R3.3 billion in April 2022 for the mid-band allotment of spectrum. This\par \par \uc2 will enable us to ramp up investment to increase coverage, including 5G offering, and to support data\par \par \uc2 traffic growth.\par \par \uc2 MTN Group\uc2  achieves Level 1 B-BBEE Contributor Status\par \par \uc2 We are pleased that, in a major transformation milestone, \uc2 MTN Group\uc2  has for the first time achieved\par \par \uc2 Level 1 Broad-Based Black Economic Empowerment (B-BBEE) Contributor Status. This is up from Level\par \par \uc2 2 in 2020. The rating, dated 10 May 2022, was for the financial year ended 31 December 2021. \uc2 MTN\par \par \uc2 SA maintained its Level 1 Contributor Status, for the third year running.\par \par \uc2 Group medium-term guidance maintained\par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, we anticipate that our markets will be impacted by the global trends of higher interest\par \par \uc2 rates and energy and food price inflation. Geopolitical volatility exacerbated by the conflict in Ukraine\par \par \uc2 is further impacting these trends and affecting \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , asset prices and supply chains\par \par \uc2 On current macro-economic and operating assumptions, we anticipate these factors to slightly\par \par \uc2 moderate our EBITDA margin evolution in the short-term. This is particularly when considering the\par \par \uc2 additional investment in Nigeria to accelerate NIN enrolment and verification, the launch of PSB\par \par \uc2 services in Nigeria and higher energy related expenses where we have pass-through costs in Towerco\par \par \uc2 agreements.\par \par \uc2 We remain focused on executing our Ambition 2025 strategy to drive growth, continue implementing\par \par \uc2 our expense efficiency programme, deleverage the Holdco balance sheet faster and unlock value for\par \par \uc2 our stakeholders. Our capex guidance for FY 2022 is unchanged at R34.4 billion and we are maintaining\par \par \uc2 our Group enhanced medium-term guidance framework.\par \par \uc2 The Group\u8217\'20\'19s results and segmental report are presented in line with the Group\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  structure. The Group\u8217\'20\'19s underlying \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 are clustered as follows: South Africa (SA), Nigeria, the Southern and East Africa (SEA) region, the West and Central Africa (WECA) region and\par \par \uc2 the Middle East and North Africa (MENA) region and their respective underlying \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The SEA region includes Uganda, Zambia, Rwanda, South Sudan, Botswana (joint venture-equity accounted), eSwatini (joint venture-equity\par \par \uc2 accounted) and Business Group. The WECA region includes Ghana, Cameroon, C\u244\'00\'F4te d\u8217\'20\'19Ivoire, Benin, Congo-Brazzaville, Liberia, Guinea\par \par \uc2 Conakry and Guinea Bissau. The MENA region includes Iran (joint venture-equity accounted), Sudan and Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 In line with the Group\u8217\'20\'19s strategy announced in March 2021, \uc2 MTN Ghana\uc2  results have been reported under the WECA region effective 1\par \par \uc2 January 2021 (previously included in the SEAGHA region). Prior year numbers have been restated for SEA and WECA accordingly.\par \par \uc2 Although Iran, Botswana and eSwatini form part of their respective regions geographically and \b \highlight22\uc2 operationally\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they are excluded from their\par \par \uc2 respective regional results because they are equity accounted for by the Group.\par \par \uc2 MTN Syria\uc2  results have been disclosed up to February for 2021; as a result of loss of control effective February 2021, following \uc2 MTN Syria\par \par \uc2 being placed under judicial guardianship. MTN Yemen results have been disclosed up to end of October 2021, as a result of \uc2 MTN\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision\par \par \uc2 to exit the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Certain information presented in this quarterly update constitutes pro forma financial information. The responsibility for preparing and\par \par \uc2 presenting the pro forma financial information and for the completeness and accuracy of the pro forma financial information is that of the\par \par \uc2 directors of \uc2 MTN\uc2 . This is presented for illustrative purposes only. Because of its nature, the pro forma financial information may not fairly\par \par \uc2 present \uc2 MTN\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s financial position, changes in equity, and results of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  or cash flows. It has not been audited or reviewed or otherwise\par \par \uc2 reported on by our external joint auditors. The financial information on which this quarterly update is based, including constant \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 information, has not been reviewed and reported on by \uc2 MTN\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s external auditors. Constant \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  information has been presented to\par \par \uc2 remove the impact of movement in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates on the Group\u8217\'20\'19s results and has been calculated by translating the prior financial reporting\par \par \uc2 period\u8217\'20\'19s results at the current period\u8217\'20\'19s average rates. The measurement has been performed for each of the Group\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , materially\par \par \uc2 being that of the US dollar and Nigerian naira. The constant \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  growth percentage has been calculated based on the prior period\par \par \uc2 constant \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  results compared to the current year results. In addition, in respect of MTN Irancell, MTN Sudan and MTN South Sudan the\par \par \uc2 constant \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  information has been prepared excluding the impact of hyperinflation. The economies of Sudan, South Sudan and Iran\par \par \uc2 were assessed to be hyperinflationary for the period under review and hyperinflation accounting was applied.\par \par \uc2 This quarterly update may contain forward-looking information. Any forecast information on which the quarterly update may be based has\par \par \uc2 not been reviewed or reported on by the Group\u8217\'20\'19s external auditors.\par \par \uc2 Navigating an increasingly volatile macroeconomic environment\par \par \uc2 We continue to navigate the impacts of COVID-19 in our markets with our response plan in place which\par \par \uc2 focuses on the same areas that we have extensively reported on previously. These areas are social\par \par \uc2 (our people, communities and stakeholders); commercial (including our customers); network and\par \par \uc2 supply chain; and funding and liquidity.\par \par \uc2 We have seen an increase in macroeconomic volatility with rising fuel and food inflation, as well as\par \par \uc2 local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation, feeding into many of our markets and compounding the pressure being\par \par \uc2 felt by consumers. The Russia-Ukraine war has exacerbated these effects with additional stress on\par \par \uc2 supply chains.\par \par \uc2 In terms of social impacts, we are working on \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and support in addition to our ongoing\par \par \uc2 programmes. To safeguard our \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we continue to manage inflationary risks through our\par \par \uc2 expense efficiency programme and disciplined capital allocation. We had to increase pricing slightly in\par \par \uc2 some markets, such as South Africa, to ensure the sustainability of the business. We will continue to\par \par \uc2 offer competitive, quality services to our customers.\par \par \uc2 In terms of supply chain, comprehensive risk mitigation strategies have been in place for a number of\par \par \uc2 years. We continue to enhance these to ensure that orders for equipment and supplies are secured\par \par \uc2 early, with enhancements to procurement agreements to ensure availability across all our markets.\par \par \uc2 These include discussions with our Towerco partners to manage the resilience of our networks; we\par \par \uc2 have put in place plans to advance capex and fuel orders.\par \par \uc2 We have reduced foreign debt in our markets and at Holdco, which remains a focus, to insulate our\par \par \uc2 balance sheet from exchange rate volatility. Deleveraging is a core objective of Ambition 2025 and we\par \par \uc2 have also built up a strong liquidity position to provide us with flexibility to withstand any further\par \par \uc2 shocks.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  review\par \par \uc2 MTN South Africa\par \par \uc2 MTN SA recorded a healthy performance in Q1 with service revenue increasing by 4.6% in line with\par \par \uc2 our medium-term guidance. This result was supported by solid commercial and \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  execution\par \par \uc2 yielding service revenue growth in all the core business units: the prepaid consumer business unit\par \par \uc2 (CBU) up 1.5%; the postpaid CBU up 4.4%; the enterprise business unit (EBU) up 23.1%; and the\par \par \uc2 wholesale business up 1.9%.\par \par \uc2 This performance shows the resilience of the business, against the backdrop of a challenging\par \par \uc2 macroeconomic environment, with a rapidly rising unemployment rate, and increased pressure on\par \par \uc2 consumer disposable income. This environment has contributed to a notable shift in customer\par \par \uc2 spending patterns and greater competition for share of the consumer\u8217\'20\'19s wallet.\par \par \uc2 MTN SA closed the quarter with a total of 34.5 million subscribers, an increase of 7.1%, driven by\par \par \uc2 higher gross additions. The postpaid subscriber base continued to expand, with an increase of 7.4% to\par \par \uc2 7.5 million on the back of solid growth in integrated voice and data-centric plans. There was a 7.0%\par \par \uc2 increase in prepaid subscribers to 27.0 million.\par \par \uc2 MTN SA\u8217\'20\'19s drive to grow mobile data revenue led to data traffic growth of 46.7% and a 14.1% increase\par \par \uc2 in active data users to 17.3 million. This underpinned a 14.3% increase in overall mobile data revenue.\par \par \uc2 Data was made more affordable in the quarter with a 22.8% YoY reduction in the effective data tariff.\par \par \uc2 An active prepaid data subscriber now consumes an average of 4.0GB of data a month, up 37% YoY,\par \par \uc2 and an active postpaid data subscriber uses nearly 11.8GB per month, an increase of 20%.\par \par \uc2 The consumer postpaid business delivered a resilient performance with service revenue growth of\par \par \uc2 4.4% benefiting from data-oriented packages.\par \par \uc2 The strong growth in data revenue also supported the consumer prepaid business\u8217\'20\'19 performance with\par \par \uc2 service revenue up by 1.5%. Pressure from the offset of voice substitution remains a challenge and\par \par \uc2 MTN SA continues to focus on ERM optimisation initiatives to reduce this impact. MTN SA\u8217\'20\'19s prepaid\par \par \uc2 segment was also impacted by economic pressure, including job losses, particularly in the lower-\par \par \uc2 income consumer segment.\par \par \uc2 The enterprise business recorded a 23.1% increase in service revenue, driven by sustained growth in\par \par \uc2 data deals, Bulk SMS and the ICT business. The core mobile business continues to gain market share,\par \par \uc2 with sustained distribution channel expansion and strong products propositions. In 2021, MTN SA was\par \par \uc2 appointed as a service provider to the national government under the national treasury transversal\par \par \uc2 contracts, enabling an expansion of our mobile service offerings to public sector entities through the\par \par \uc2 leveraging of \uc2 MTN\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s strong network quality.\par \par \uc2 Wholesale revenue increased by 1.9%, supported by national roaming deals with Cell C and \uc2 Telkom\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For Cell C, revenue recognition remains on a cash basis as Cell C works towards its recapitalisation.\par \par \uc2 MTN SA recognised R643 million (down 4.7% YoY) in national roaming revenue from Cell C and a\par \par \uc2 balance of R400 million remained unrecognised, as at 31 March 2022.\par \par \uc2 The new multi-year national roaming agreement with \uc2 Telkom\uc2 , which came into effect on 1 November\par \par \uc2 2021, continues to scale steadily and MTN SA is focused on further enablement of infrastructure\par \par \uc2 sharing through our investment in the expansion of network capacity.\par \par \uc2 The performance of the fintech business was encouraging with approximately 5.0 million registered\par \par \uc2 MoMo users and 840 043 monthly active users by 31 March 2022. MTN SA also progressed work on\par \par \uc2 MoMo partnerships to accelerate the growth of the business. The platform continues to grow\par \par \uc2 transactions driven by innovative and relevant solutions.\par \par \uc2 MTN SA reported EBITDA of R4.9 billion (up 3.7% YoY) with as slight increase in the EBITDA margin to\par \par \uc2 39.9% (up 0.1pp), supported by a solid service revenue performance and disciplined execution of the\par \par \uc2 expense efficiency programme.\par \par \uc2 Network availability and quality has been affected by loadshedding from the national grid, as well as\par \par \uc2 ongoing theft and vandalism of towers since Q4 2021. To mitigate these impacts, MTN SA rolled out a\par \par \uc2 comprehensive resilience plan including additional batteries, generators and enhanced security\par \par \uc2 features. We are committed to ensuring the quality of our customers\u8217\'20\'19 connectivity experience.\par \par \uc2 Pleasingly, MTN SA secured high-demand spectrum to support the expansion of 4G and 5G services,\par \par \uc2 with total permanent spectrum holdings rising from 76MHz to 176MHz and payment of R3.3 billion\par \par \uc2 was made in April 2022 for mid-band spectrum. The spectrum has been allotted for a 20-year period\par \par \uc2 on a technology-neutral basis.\par \par \uc2 Since the award of temporary spectrum in 2020, MTN SA has ramped up its investment to increase\par \par \uc2 network coverage, improve speeds and enhance the overall customer experience. MTN SA has also\par \par \uc2 invested in an expansion drive into rural and peri-urban areas and a major 5G rollout, reaching a\par \par \uc2 population coverage of 15%.\par \par \uc2 MTN SA will continue to use the spectrum to expand 4G and 5G connectivity, as well as capacity, to\par \par \uc2 entrench its position as South Africa\u8217\'20\'19s best network while further enhancing rural, peri-urban, and\par \par \uc2 urban network coverage. The spectrum allocation will allow for greater value for customers,\par \par \uc2 supporting a lower cost to communicate as we work to bridge the digital divide.\par \par \uc2 MTN SA retained its top position as the country\u8217\'20\'19s most valuable brand in the 2022 \uc2 Brand Finance\par \par \uc2 South Africa survey. For the eighth consecutive year, MTN SA maintained its leadership position with\par \par \uc2 its brand value increasing by 34% to R59.8 billion.\par \par \uc2 In mid-April 2022, the province of KwaZulu-Natal (KZN) in South Africa felt the effects of heavy rainfall\par \par \uc2 causing floods in many parts of KZN. In addition to the impact on lives and livelihoods, the floods\par \par \uc2 caused extensive damage to infrastructure and power outages. A total of more than 500 \uc2 MTN\uc2  sites\par \par \uc2 were down in the province. MTN SA activated contingency plans and has since restored to full capacity\par \par \uc2 around 90% of the affected sites. We continue to work to fully restore connectivity.\par \par \uc2 To support the people and municipalities in KZN hit the hardest by the floods, MTN SA donated a total\par \par \uc2 of R1 million in of food parcels and blankets. To assist families and friends to keep in touch during this\par \par \uc2 difficult time, MTN SA also provided free three-day SMS bundles to over seven million customers in\par \par \uc2 KZN.\par \par \uc2 MTN SA\u8217\'20\'19s performance outlook will be shaped by macroeconomic conditions in the country such as\par \par \uc2 loadshedding as well as current global macroeconomic uncertainties which are expected to add to\par \par \uc2 inflationary pressures, including on costs and availability of food and fuel. This will further impact\par \par \uc2 consumer spending power and patterns.\par \par \uc2 MTN SA is focused on accelerating its high growth business areas, with ongoing emphasis on the\par \par \uc2 overall expense efficiency programme to deliver strong EBITDA and cash flow growth.\par \par \uc2 MTN Nigeria\par \par \uc2 MTN Nigeria\uc2  recorded a solid Q1 2022 performance, tracking positively against its medium-term\par \par \uc2 targets, with 22.0% growth in service revenue and the expansion of EBITDA margins. This was\par \par \uc2 delivered through solid commercial momentum and execution in challenging trading conditions.\par \par \uc2 Voice revenue grew by 8.6%, driven by an increase in gross connections and higher usage from the\par \par \uc2 active SIM base. This was enabled by expanded customer acquisition touchpoints, rural telephony\par \par \uc2 initiatives and an enhanced customer value management (CVM) toolkit, reducing the impact of the\par \par \uc2 SIM registration and activation restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Data revenue rose by 54.0%, maintaining the accelerated growth trajectory through growth in the\par \par \uc2 subscriber base and data usage. This was sustained by an aggressive 4G network expansion and\par \par \uc2 enhanced quality and capacity of our network to support rising data traffic. Data traffic rose by 84.8%\par \par \uc2 YoY and usage (MB per user) was up by 69.8%.\par \par \uc2 In addition, \uc2 MTN Nigeria\uc2  added about a million new smartphones to its network in Q1, bringing\par \par \uc2 smartphone penetration to 50.0%. The 4G network now covers 71.7% of the population, up from\par \par \uc2 70.3% in December 2021.\par \par \uc2 Home broadband penetration remains a focus for \uc2 MTN Nigeria\uc2 , reaching over 775 000 users, up by\par \par \uc2 over 160 000 in Q1. The goal is to Own the Home through differentiated value propositions, creating\par \par \uc2 experiences that position \uc2 MTN Nigeria\uc2  as the broadband service provider of choice. This will be\par \par \uc2 achieved by leveraging its unique assets and capabilities to capture a significant share of market\par \par \uc2 growth.\par \par \uc2 Fintech revenue rose by 46.7% on the back of the growing adoption of fintech services and an\par \par \uc2 expanded user base. The MoMo agent network continues to widen with more than 800k registered\par \par \uc2 agents and over 166k active agents (agents who perform a minimum of ten revenue-generating\par \par \uc2 transactions within 30 days).\par \par \uc2 Having established a significant foundation of registered agent network, the focus is now on scaling\par \par \uc2 the active base. Agent services have been extended to include card withdrawal services by deploying\par \par \uc2 point of sales (POS) terminals to the agent network. This has contributed to growing total volume of\par \par \uc2 transactions to over 56.1 million, up 132.7% YoY, by more than 10.7 million active users (up 135.2%).\par \par \uc2 MTN Nigeria\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s fintech subsidiary received final approval of a MoMo PSB licence from the \uc2 CBN\uc2 . This\par \par \uc2 will enable the Operating company (Opco) to invest in the launch and rollout of PSB and offer a\par \par \uc2 broader range of services, with a planned commercial launch before the end of Q2 2022.\par \par \uc2 Digital revenue grew by 37.4% as penetration of digital products continued to deepen, driven by\par \par \uc2 increased usage from the active base. Digital subscriptions were up by 164.1% YoY to 7.4 million, with\par \par \uc2 ayoba accounting for 45% of the subscriptions and Rich Media accounting for 55%.\par \par \uc2 Revenue from digital services was driven by Rich Media, Mobile Advertising and Content VAS. The\par \par \uc2 Y\u8217\'20\'19ello Digital Platforms are being transformed into a one-stop destination that caters to all consumer\par \par \uc2 segments.\par \par \uc2 Revenue from the enterprise business rose by 34.0%, underpinned by the onboarding of new\par \par \uc2 customers across segments and the uptake of our enhanced services. The enterprise business is\par \par \uc2 transitioning from product to platform while leveraging core mobile and fixed connectivity to serve\par \par \uc2 customers across all segments better.\par \par \uc2 MTN Nigeria\uc2  has commenced executing programmes to digitise and transform micro, small and\par \par \uc2 medium enterprises (MSMEs) in Nigeria. Key among these is developing a cloud technology\par \par \uc2 marketplace to empower, educate and enrich MSMEs. The goal is to drive further adoption of\par \par \uc2 enterprise platforms (internet of things and cloud), creating additional value for our customers and\par \par \uc2 enabling them to innovate while remaining profitable.\par \par \uc2 MTN Nigeria\uc2  progressed with the expense efficiency programme and remained disciplined with capital\par \par \uc2 allocation. As a result, the Opco contained the 18.3% increase in operating expenses below the rate\par \par \uc2 of inflation in Nigeria. This was achieved despite the continued effects of naira depreciation on lease\par \par \uc2 rental costs and acceleration in site rollout. Consequently, \uc2 MTN Nigeria\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s ability to drive operating\par \par \uc2 leverage has enabled EBITDA growth of 25.7% and the expansion of EBITDA margin by 1.5pp to 54.6%.\par \par \uc2 Southern and East Africa (SEA)\par \par \uc2 The SEA region delivered service revenue growth of 12.7% for the quarter, supported by top-line\par \par \uc2 growth in most markets despite challenging trading conditions including muted economic recovery\par \par \uc2 and higher inflation. The region\u8217\'20\'19s performance reflected strong growth in data revenue (up 35.6%)\par \par \uc2 and fintech revenue (up 26.8%). The SEA region increased subscriber numbers by 2.8% YoY to 34.8\par \par \uc2 million.\par \par \uc2 MTN Uganda recorded service revenue growth of 11.1%. This was driven by solid growth in data\par \par \uc2 revenue (up 45.1%) and fintech revenue (up 21.0%) resulting from increased usage and subscriber\par \par \uc2 base growth. Voice revenue (down 4.6%) was adversely impacted by customers\u8217\'20\'19 disposable incomes\par \par \uc2 coming under pressure from rising inflation. Despite the tough trading conditions, the Opco delivered\par \par \uc2 EBITDA margin expansion of 0.4pp to 51.6% on the back of revenue growth and the effective\par \par \uc2 implementation of expense efficiencies.\par \par \uc2 MTN Rwanda\uc2  delivered strong service revenue growth of 24.5%, supported by healthy growth in\par \par \uc2 fintech revenue (up 56.8%), data revenue (up 13.2%) and voice revenue (up 11.8%). \uc2 MTN Rwanda\par \par \uc2 reported an EBITDA margin of 47.7%, down 3.7pp. This was impacted mainly by higher data cost of\par \par \uc2 sales from higher 4G uptake as well as the introduction of an outsourced modernised Network\par \par \uc2 Operating Centre to manage the monitoring of our network, boost the user experience and reduce\par \par \uc2 the volume of customer complaint calls.\par \par \uc2 Overall the SEA portfolio reported a 1.3pp decrease in the blended EBITDA margin to 46.0% in Q1\par \par \uc2 2022.\par \par \uc2 The results of our associate investment in eSwatini reflected increased pressure on voice and data in\par \par \uc2 a challenging economic environment, amid increased competition and return-to-office work, which\par \par \uc2 reduced demand for data. Plans are in place to accelerate demand through CVM and competitive\par \par \uc2 offers.\par \par \uc2 West and Central Africa (WECA)\par \par \uc2 Despite the challenging trading environment, the WECA region delivered a pleasing performance for\par \par \uc2 the quarter with service revenue growth of 17.0%. The strong performance was enabled by an uptake\par \par \uc2 of data services. Fintech revenue remained resilient despite aggressive pricing strategies from\par \par \uc2 competitors in some markets. The region\u8217\'20\'19s performance also benefitted from a 8.2% increase in the\par \par \uc2 subscriber base to 70.2 million. WECA delivered a blended EBITDA margin of 43.5%, up by 2.3pp,\par \par \uc2 mainly driven by \uc2 MTN Ghana\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MTN Ghana\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Q1 performance is testament to the resilience of the business to withstand a challenging\par \par \uc2 macro-economic environment including increases in fuel prices and general inflation as well as local\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation.\par \par \uc2 Service revenue grew by 34.7%, largely attributable to growth in voice, data and fintech. This\par \par \uc2 performance was underpinned by the continued execution of the Ambition 2025 strategy and\par \par \uc2 sustained investments to support improvements in service quality and the expansion of network\par \par \uc2 capacity and coverage.\par \par \uc2 An increase in active data users and usage led to an increase of 65.3% in data revenue. Fintech revenue\par \par \uc2 grew by 27.1%, supported by higher active MoMo users. The EBITDA margin increased by 5.0pp to\par \par \uc2 59.7% on the back of the strong growth in the topline and the implementation of the expense\par \par \uc2 efficiency programme.\par \par \uc2 MTN C\u244\'00\'F4te d\u8217\'20\'19Ivoire\u8217\'20\'19s performance came under pressure with service revenue slightly down by 0.7%\par \par \uc2 largely impacted by the decline in fintech revenue (down 38.4%) due to zero-rating of person-to-\par \par \uc2 person (P2P) and price reductions for withdrawals driven by intensified competition in the market\par \par \uc2 during H2 2021. This helped to accelerate user and usage growth in Q4 2021, with that momentum\par \par \uc2 continuing Q1 2022 during which over 506 000 users were added in C\u244\'00\'F4te d\u8217\'20\'19Ivoire. The business\par \par \uc2 remains on a strong foundation to grow over the medium-term.\par \par \uc2 The decline in fintech revenue in Q1 was mitigated by a strong performance in data revenue, which\par \par \uc2 grew by 24.9%, underpinned by initiatives to accelerate data penetration and usage. Encouragingly,\par \par \uc2 the Opco continued to gain value share in the market. The EBITDA margin declined by 1.3pp to 33.0%\par \par \uc2 due to the decline in the topline while maintaining the same commissions to the distribution network\par \par \uc2 in fintech.\par \par \uc2 MTN Cameroon recorded service revenue growth of 12.9% in a challenging environment where the\par \par \uc2 Opco leads on market and value share. This performance was supported by sustained growth in active\par \par \uc2 data users and ongoing CVM initiatives which led to growth in data revenue (up 30.8%). Revenue\par \par \uc2 growth in fintech (up 19.9%) and digital (up 28.1%) also aided overall service revenue growth. The\par \par \uc2 EBITDA margin declined by 2.7pp to 33.7%, impacted by management fees and provisions for taxes\par \par \uc2 on MoMo transfers and withdrawals.\par \par \uc2 Excluding \uc2 MTN Ghana\uc2 , the WECA markets grew service revenue by 6.7%, which remained ahead of\par \par \uc2 blended inflation of 4.7%, with a 1.8pp decline in EBITDA margin to 31.5% in Q1 2022 largely reflecting\par \par \uc2 the pressure in MTN C\u244\'00\'F4te d\u8217\'20\'19Ivoire and MTN Cameroon.\par \par \uc2 Middle East and North Africa (MENA)\par \par \uc2 The MENA region delivered a strong performance with service revenue growth of 83.4% (excluding\par \par \uc2 MTN Irancell) notwithstanding tough macroeconomic conditions including \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation,\par \par \uc2 inflationary impacts, sustained higher oil prices and higher food prices as well as food shortages. The\par \par \uc2 region\u8217\'20\'19s performance was mainly supported by strong growth in data revenue and an increase in\par \par \uc2 active data users. The total number of subscribers (excluding MTN Irancell) was 15.2 million.\par \par \uc2 MTN Sudan increased service revenue by 261.9%, underpinned by growth in voice (up 200.8%) and\par \par \uc2 data (up 387.9%) revenue on the back of increased voice and data prices. The EBITDA margin\par \par \uc2 expanded by 17.4pp to 51.4%, driven by strong growth in revenue as well as savings in maintenance\par \par \uc2 costs.\par \par \uc2 MTN\uc2  Irancell, our equity-accounted joint venture, delivered solid results through strong commercial\par \par \uc2 execution of various initiatives. Service revenue grew by 26.6%, supported by increased data revenue.\par \par \uc2 The EBITDA margin expanded by 3.6pp to 42.8% driven by strong revenue growth and expense\par \par \uc2 efficiencies.\par \par \uc2 This result was delivered in a challenging environment where the Russia-Ukraine war is impacting the\par \par \uc2 growth and economic development of the country. While the GDP of the country stands to benefit\par \par \uc2 from the increased oil and gas prices, rising inflation will place pressure on the consumer.\par \par \uc2 The Iran Internet Group\u8217\'20\'19s ride-hailing app, Snapp, remained the market leader, ranking among the\par \par \uc2 top ride-hailing apps globally with over three million daily rides. Snapp Box is the leading last-mile\par \par \uc2 delivery network in the country. Market-leading food delivery app Snappfood orders grew by 61.0%\par \par \uc2 YoY to almost 260 000 daily orders. Snapp Market orders grew by 88.9% YoY and remains the leading\par \par \uc2 supermarket delivery app in the country.\par \par \uc2 Building the largest and most valuable fintech platform\par \par \uc2 We continue to scale and accelerate our fintech ecosystem across our markets with a 32.5% increase\par \par \uc2 in transaction volumes to 2.9 billion transactions in Q1, with the value of transactions up by 12.6% to\par \par \uc2 US$59.9 billion (up 18.9% in constant \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ). This includes the impact of new commission-earning\par \par \uc2 rules which were implemented in Ghana during Q3 2021, adjusting for which, underlying transaction\par \par \uc2 value growth would have been 34.0% in constant \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  terms.\par \par \uc2 We responded to competitive pressures in Cote d\u8217\'20\'19Ivoire by reducing P2P and withdrawals pricing,\par \par \uc2 which affected service revenue development. However we are encouraged by the user growth\par \par \uc2 recovery we have seen in Q1 2022 in that market. We remain confident of our recovery plan in Cote\par \par \uc2 d\u8217\'20\'19Ivoire where we anticipate fintech service revenue to be back in growth by Q4 2022 driven by user\par \par \uc2 growth and the launch of advanced services. Overall fintech service revenue grew by 21.1% in\par \par \uc2 Q1 2022. We are driving recurring usage with a focus on new product launches to grow advanced\par \par \uc2 services in line with our strategy.\par \par \uc2 There was strong traction in growing our payments and e-commerce ecosystem, leveraging our\par \par \uc2 strength in distribution. The number of active merchants accepting MoMo payments increased by\par \par \uc2 46.8% to 765 211 and the total value of MoMo merchant payments rose by 125.2% US$4.3 billion. We\par \par \uc2 will continue the roll-out of self-onboarding of merchants to entrench the momentum and growth in\par \par \uc2 our payments and e-commerce vertical.\par \par \uc2 We facilitated a total loan value of US$280.4 million, a 27.8% YoY increase, taking advantage of our\par \par \uc2 scaled mobile wallet business and customer footprint. The total value of remittances grew by 7.4%\par \par \uc2 YoY to US$573.9 million in Q1 2022.\par \par \uc2 At the end of Q1 2022, our InsurTech platform had 17.4 million registered aYo policies, reflecting\par \par \uc2 growth of 37.8% YoY. Active policies reduced by 31.1% YoY due to a shift in the strategy to focus on a\par \par \uc2 higher average revenue per policy and new revenue streams, particularly in Uganda, resulting in the\par \par \uc2 termination of free policies. Active policy growth was also impacted by platform migration and\par \par \uc2 integration challenges as we shifted to a proprietary infrastructure. In the year ahead we anticipate a\par \par \uc2 return to growth in active paid policies with higher average revenue per policy.\par \par \uc2 The final approval of a MoMo PSB licence in Nigeria by the \uc2 CBN\uc2  will enable us to invest in the launch,\par \par \uc2 before the end of Q2 2022, and rollout of PSB and offer a broader range of services. In terms of\par \par \uc2 partnerships, our joint venture (JV) with \uc2 Sanlam\uc2  is undergoing regulatory approvals across the various\par \par \uc2 markets we operate in and we anticipate launching the JV in early H2 2022.\par \par \uc2 Asset realisation programme (ARP) and portfolio transformation\par \par \uc2 Our ARP aims to reduce debt, simplify our portfolio, reduce risk and improve returns. \uc2 MTN\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s broader\par \par \uc2 portfolio transformation plan is to accelerate these goals in line with our focus on pan-Africa, support\par \par \uc2 our shared value objectives and structure the business to unlock value.\par \par \uc2 To date, the Group has received net proceeds of R708 million, post the period-end, from the Series 1\par \par \uc2 MTN Nigeria\uc2  public offer. For the sale of MTN SA passive tower infrastructure, we are expecting to\par \par \uc2 receive R5.2 billion in net proceeds from the transaction in Q2 2022.\par \par \uc2 The further localisation of \uc2 MTN Ghana\uc2  is in progress and we have increased our localisation from\par \par \uc2 17.5% to 18.7% in Q1, with new participation from some local pension funds. The Group has received\par \par \uc2 cash proceeds of R272 million from this transaction. Post the quarter-end, we concluded a transaction\par \par \uc2 to localise a further 5% of \uc2 MTN Ghana\uc2 , bringing the total localisation to 23.7%. We are committed to\par \par \uc2 deepen local participation to achieve 30% localisation for \uc2 MTN Ghana\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We continue to explore options to exit Afghanistan in an orderly manner.\par \par \uc2 Update on regulatory and legal considerations\par \par \uc2 High-demand radio frequency in South Africa\par \par \uc2 To support its 4G and 5G expansion drive, in March 2022, MTN SA secured high-demand radio\par \par \uc2 frequency spectrum in the \uc2 Independent Communications Authority of South Africa\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s (\uc2 ICASA\uc2 ) landmark\par \par \uc2 auction. This increased MTN SA\u8217\'20\'19s total permanent spectrum holdings from 76MHz to 176MHz.\par \par \uc2 MTN\uc2  secured 2 x 10MHz of 800MHz, 4 x 10MHz of 2 600MHz and 4 x 10MHz of 3 500MHz for\par \par \uc2 R5.2 billion, comparing favourably with global benchmark averages. This was the first time in 17 years\par \par \uc2 that \uc2 ICASA\uc2  had released spectrum on a permanent basis, allotting it for 20 years on a technology-\par \par \uc2 neutral basis. A payment of R3.3 billion was made in April 2022, for the mid-band spectrum. A\par \par \uc2 payment of R1.9 billion is expected to be made in Q3 2022 for the low-band spectrum.\par \par \uc2 MTN SA will use the spectrum to expand 4G and 5G connectivity and capacity and further enhance\par \par \uc2 rural, peri-urban and urban network coverage. The spectrum will also support our work to lower the\par \par \uc2 cost to communicate.\par \par \uc2 SIM registration in Nigeria\par \par \uc2 Following the \uc2 Nigeria Communications Commission\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s (NCC) directive on 4 April 2022 that all operators\par \par \uc2 in Nigeria should restrict outgoing calls for subscribers whose SIMs are not associated with NINs, MTN\par \par \uc2 Nigeria implemented the directive on approximately 19 million affected subscribers at that date.\par \par \uc2 However, subscribers whose outgoing calls were restricted for non-submission of their NINs require\par \par \uc2 their NINs to be verified before being reactivated.\par \par \uc2 As at 25 April 2022, about 8.7 million of those restricted submitted their NINs for verification, of which\par \par \uc2 approximately 1.2 million have been reactivated, many of whom are high-value customers. Outgoing\par \par \uc2 voice revenue from the subscribers who are yet to be reactivated therefore contributed approximately\par \par \uc2 7.8% of total service revenue based on FY 2021 for \uc2 MTN Nigeria\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In terms of how general traffic trends have evolved since the implementation of the directive, MTN\par \par \uc2 Nigeria saw a drop in traffic following its compliance with the directive. However, as we execute our\par \par \uc2 NIN recovery initiatives, there has been a gradual turnaround as the affected subscribers are\par \par \uc2 reconnected to resume voice calls. Data revenue has continued a steady increase but has currently\par \par \uc2 not fully compensated for the decline in voice revenue of restricted subscribers. Encouragingly, there\par \par \uc2 has been an acceleration in new connections.\par \par \uc2 MTN Nigeria\uc2  continues to engage affected subscribers while providing hardware support for the NIMC\par \par \uc2 to accelerate the verification of NINs submitted. It has taken significant steps to help drive NIN\par \par \uc2 enrolment for subscribers who do not yet have a NIN, deploying over 4 200 points of enrolment across\par \par \uc2 the country. \uc2 MTN Nigeria\uc2  has also provided multiple channels to enable customers to submit their\par \par \uc2 NIN for verification and linkage.\par \par \uc2 MTN Ghana\uc2  e-levy\par \par \uc2 On 29 March 2022, Ghana\u8217\'20\'19s parliament approved the Electronic Transfer Levy Bill. From 1 May 2022,\par \par \uc2 a 1.5% e-levy will apply to selected electronic transactions of more than GHS100 per customer per day\par \par \uc2 on Mobile Money, bank transfers, merchant payments and inward remittances.\par \par \uc2 The levy is a reduction from the 1.75% communicated previously through the 2022 Budget Statement\par \par \uc2 and Economic Policy of Ghana. To reduce the impact of the levy on customers, from May 2022, MTN\par \par \uc2 Ghana is reducing fees on P2P transactions by 25%.\par \par \uc2 MTN Ghana\uc2  classification as a significant market power\par \par \uc2 Following the \uc2 National Communications Authority\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s (NCA) 2020 classification of \uc2 MTN Ghana\uc2  as a\par \par \uc2 significant market power, the Opco has now implemented four of the seven \u8216\'20\'18SMP remedies\u8217\'20\'19 being\par \par \uc2 national roaming. \uc2 MTN Ghana\uc2  remains in constructive discussions with the NCA on the outstanding\par \par \uc2 remedies.\par \par \uc2 Outlook\par \par \uc2 We are encouraged by the healthy performance delivered across our markets in Q1 2022, despite\par \par \uc2 challenging macro-economic conditions and new regulatory developments. Looking ahead, our\par \par \uc2 markets will be impacted by rising inflation, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility, increased pressure on disposable\par \par \uc2 income for consumers and further potential supply chain disruptions.\par \par \uc2 In this context, our medium-term outlook remains supported by the structurally higher demand for\par \par \uc2 data and fintech services and \uc2 MTN\uc2  is uniquely positioned to capture that growth. Our priority is to\par \par \uc2 execute on the Ambition 2025 strategy to drive this growth as well as deleverage the Holdco balance\par \par \uc2 sheet faster and unlock value, as we manage the rising risks and macroeconomic uncertainties we are\par \par \uc2 seeing.\par \par \uc2 We are focused on accelerating growth in MTN SA and \uc2 MTN Nigeria\uc2  as we drive to be the industry-\par \par \uc2 leading connectivity business. In addition to the macroeconomic pressures, MTN SA remains impacted\par \par \uc2 by the continued disruptions to power supply from \uc2 Eskom\uc2  and the residual effects of the recent floods\par \par \uc2 in the KZN province. . The SA prepaid consumer is under pressure, but we remain committed to drive\par \par \uc2 overall service revenue growth in line with our medium-term guidance for MTN SA.\par \par \uc2 The focus in Nigeria will be on driving ongoing growth in the subscriber and revenue base in the\par \par \uc2 context of prevailing SIM registration restrictions, as well as launching PSB services. While the EBITDA\par \par \uc2 margin may be impacted in the near-term by these effects, we anticipate continued growth in service\par \par \uc2 revenue of \u8216\'20\'18at least 20%\u8217\'20\'19, as per our medium-term guidance.\par \par \uc2 Within fintech, during Q1, we implemented new mobile money taxes in Benin and Cameroon; we also\par \par \uc2 made further P2P pricing adjustments in response to ongoing competition in C\u244\'00\'F4te d\u8217\'20\'19Ivoire to continue\par \par \uc2 to stimulate user and usage growth. We also implemented the E-levy tax in Ghana and adjusted our\par \par \uc2 pricing to limit the impact on usage in that market.\par \par \uc2 While these factors will have an impact on our performance, we remain on course to fulfil our fintech\par \par \uc2 ambitions and our mission to drive financial inclusion by leveraging our platform data and capabilities.\par \par \uc2 The final award of the PSB licence in Nigeria is a major milestone and will be key in driving our fintech\par \par \uc2 growth over the medium-term. We are planning the commercial launch of PSB services before the end\par \par \uc2 of Q2 2022.\par \par \uc2 On current macroeconomic conditions and operating assumptions, rising inflation in our markets and\par \par \uc2 specific \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  investment in markets like \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NIN enrolments and the\par \par \uc2 launch of PSB services), are anticipated to slightly dampen our EBITDA margin expansion in the near-\par \par \uc2 term.\par \par \uc2 The expense efficiency programme is ongoing and supporting the overall profitability of the business.\par \par \uc2 We will thus continue to execute on this programme to complement our topline growth in achieving\par \par \uc2 our objective to drive further EBITDA margin expansion and return improvement over the medium-\par \par \uc2 term.\par \par \uc2 In terms of some of our key strategic programmes, the work to structurally separate our fintech and\par \par \uc2 fibre businesses is ongoing and remains on track.\par \par \uc2 To achieve the accelerated growth across our \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the investment in sustaining our \u8216\'20\'18second-\par \par \uc2 to-none\u8217\'20\'19 networks and platforms will remain a priority. To support our objectives, we plan to spend\par \par \uc2 R34.4 billion in capex in FY 2022, which is unchanged from our previous guidance.\par \par \uc2 With the outlook for global economic growth remaining uncertain amid the increased geopolitical\par \par \uc2 volatility, we will remain focused on delivering on our strategy and maintaining our position as a\par \par \uc2 partner of choice to stakeholders across our markets. Our strategy is robust and we have adequate\par \par \uc2 adaptability and resilience built into our operating model to weather the uncertainties. We maintain\par \par \uc2 our enhanced Group medium-term guidance.\par \par \uc2 B-BBEE annual compliance report \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 MTN Group\uc2  and subsidiaries: \uc2 MTN Group\uc2  achieves\par \par \uc2 Level 1 B-BBEE Contributor Status\par \par \uc2 We are pleased that, in a major transformation milestone, \uc2 MTN Group\uc2  has for the first time achieved\par \par \uc2 Level 1 Broad-Based Black Economic Empowerment (B-BBEE) Contributor Status. This is up from Level\par \par \uc2 2 in 2020. The rating, dated 10 May 2022, was for the financial year ended 31 December 2021. The B-\par \par \uc2 BBEE Contributor Status for \uc2 MTN Group\uc2  considers both the South African operating entities as well as\par \par \uc2 the South African group function. MTN SA maintained its Level 1 Contributor Status, for the third year\par \par \uc2 running.\par \par \uc2 In accordance with paragraph 16.21(g) and Appendix 1 to Section 11 of the JSE Listings Requirements\par \par \uc2 and Section 13G(2) of the Broad Based Black Economic Empowerment Act, the Company\u8217\'20\'19s B-BBEE\par \par \uc2 compliance certificate and compliance report is available on the Company\u8217\'20\'19s website at\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mtn.com/investors-shareholders/?tablink=b-bbee-certificate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.mtn.com/investors-shareholders/?tablink=b-bbee-certificate}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The full financial results are available on the \uc2 MTN\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s website at:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mtn.com/investors/financial-results/?report_cat=quarterly-results"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.mtn.com/investors/financial-results/?report_cat=quarterly-results}}}\par \par \uc2 Abbreviations:\par \par \uc2 SIM: Subscriber Identity/Identification Module\par \par \uc2 Business Group: Consist of internet service providers in Namibia, Kenya and Botswana\par \par \uc2 Towerco: Tower companies\par \par \uc2 Holdco leverage: Holdco net debt (including MTN GC)/SA EBITDA + cash upstreaming\par \par \uc2 PSB: Payment service bank\par \par \uc2 CVM: Customer value management\par \par \uc2 ERM: Effective rate per minute\par \par \uc2 MB: Megabyte\par \par \uc2 GB: Gigabyte\par \par \uc2 13 May 2022\par \par \uc2 Fairland\par \par \uc2 Lead sponsor\par \par \uc2 Tamela Holdings Proprietary Limited\par \par \uc2 Joint sponsor\par \par \uc2 JP Morgan Equities (SA) Proprietary Limited\par \par \uc2 Date: 13-05-2022 07:05:00\par \par \uc2 Produced by the JSE SENS Department. The SENS service is an information dissemination service administered by the \uc2 JSE Limited\uc2  ('\uc2 JSE\uc2 ').\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 JSE\uc2  does not, whether expressly, tacitly or implicitly, represent, warrant or in any way guarantee the truth, accuracy or completeness of\par \par \uc2 the information published on SENS. The \uc2 JSE\uc2 , their officers, employees and agents accept no liability for (or in respect of) any direct,\par \par \uc2 indirect, incidental or consequential loss or damage of any kind or nature, howsoever arising, from the use of SENS or the use of, or reliance on,\par \par \uc2 information disseminated through SENS.\par \par \uc2 Dokument JSEXCH0020220513ei5d0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
