\par\par \b \uc2 *Fitch Affirms Sterling Bank at 'B-'; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 2,629 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2021\par 18:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 30 Sep 2021 12:45 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms Sterling Bank at 'B-'; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par       \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  -London-30 September 2021: \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed \uc2 Sterling Bank Plc\uc2  's Long-Term Issuer Default Rating (IDR) at 'B-' with a Stable Outlook. The National Long-Term Rating has been upgraded to 'BBB+(nga)' from 'BBB(nga)', reflecting the bank's increased creditworthiness relative to that of other issuers in Nigeria. A full list of rating actions is below.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 ISSUER DEFAULT RATINGS AND VIABILITY RATING\par \par \uc2 The IDRs of Sterling are driven by its standalone creditworthiness, as expressed by its Viability Rating (VR) of 'b-'. The VR reflects the concentration of the bank's activities within Nigeria's challenging operating environment, a fairly small franchise, high credit concentrations, and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) funding and liquidity weaknesses resulting from high depositor concentration. This is balanced against adequate asset quality and capitalisation for the bank's risk profile.\par \par \uc2 The Stable Outlook reflects \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view that risks to Sterling's credit profile are captured by the current rating, with sufficient headroom under our base case to absorb the fallout from operating-environment pressures.\par \par \uc2 Operating conditions in Nigeria are gradually stabilising. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  forecasts 1.9% GDP growth in 2021, following a 1.8% contraction in 2020. Our baseline scenario is that business volumes and earnings should continue to rebound in 2021, while the rally in oil prices is also a positive factor. Nevertheless, downside risks linger, given inherently volatile market conditions, with banks still exposed to FC shortages, potential further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation, rising inflation and regulatory \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2  (CBN) \uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Sterling has a fairly small franchise, representing 3% of domestic banking-system assets at end-2020. Single-borrower concentration is high, with the 20-largest customer loans representing 53% of gross loans or 286% of Fitch Core Capital (\uc2 FCC\uc2  ) at end-1H21. Its oil and gas exposure has reduced in recent years but remains material, representing 26% of gross loans or 139% of \uc2 FCC\uc2  at end-1H21, and is concentrated in the upstream and services segments, posing a significant risk to asset quality in the event of a prolonged period of low oil prices and production cuts.\par \par \uc2 Sterling's impaired loans (Stage 3 loans under IFRS 9) ratio increased to 2.9% at end-3Q20 from 2.2% at end-2019, as a result of the pandemic, but declined to 1.8% by end-1H21 on write-offs and robust loan growth. Specific loan loss allowance (\uc2 LLA\uc2 ) coverage of impaired loans (96% at end-1H21) is high and above peers'. Stage 2 loans (20% of gross loans at end-1H21; average \uc2 LLA\uc2  coverage of 3.6%) have increased significantly as a result of the pandemic, and are concentrated, but are not expected to lead to a material increase in impaired loans.\par \par \uc2 Sterling delivers adequate profitability - as indicated by operating returns on risk-weighted assets that have averaged 1.4% over the past four full years - notwithstanding its high cost base reflecting its limited economies of scale. Margins are reasonable and in line with peers', underpinned by lending to higher-margin segments and, in 2020 and 1H21, growth in low-cost deposits. Loan impairment charges (LICs) continue to weigh on performance (1H21: equal to 35% of pre-impairment operating profit).\par \par \uc2 Sterling's \uc2 FCC\uc2  ratio (14.5% at end-1H21) is a rating strength at the current rating level, underpinned by moderate pre-impairment operating profit (equal to 2.6% of average gross loans over the past four full years) and full \uc2 LLA\uc2  coverage of impaired loans. Its capital adequacy ratio (CAR; 15%) is comfortably above its 10% minimum requirement.\par \par \uc2 Funding is mainly in the form of customer deposits (78% in naira; 22% in FC at end-2020). The share of current and savings accounts (82% of customer deposits at end-1H21) has increased in recent years, reducing reliance on more price-sensitive and concentrated term deposits. Single-depositor concentration is moderate, with the 20-largest deposits representing 20% of customer deposits at end-1H21. However, FC single-depositor concentration is exceptionally high, with the two-largest depositors representing 34% of FC customer deposits. Its loans/deposits ratio is a low 66%. Liquidity coverage is strong in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but weak in FC in the context of high concentration risk.\par \par \uc2 SUPPORT RATING AND SUPPORT RATING FLOOR\par \par \uc2 Sovereign support to commercial banks cannot be relied on given Nigeria's weak ability to provide support, particularly in FC. The Support Rating Floor of all Nigerian banks is 'No Floor' and all Support Ratings are '5'. This reflects our view that senior creditors cannot rely on receiving full and timely extraordinary support from the Nigerian sovereign if any of the banks become non-viable.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATINGS\par \par \uc2 Sterling's National Ratings are driven by the bank's standalone strength. The upgrade of Sterling's National Long-Term Rating reflects the bank's increased creditworthiness relative to other Nigerian issuers'. Sterling's National Short-Term Rating of 'F2(nga)' is the lower of two possible options for a National Long-Term Rating of 'BBB+(nga)' under \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's criteria, reflecting weaknesses in the bank's funding and liquidity profile, which increases the vulnerability of default on its short-term local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  obligations within Nigeria.\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 - Erosion of capital buffers to levels close to or below the bank's minimum regulatory requirements, which could result from a significant increase in impaired loans leading to losses.\par \par \uc2 - A significant tightening in FC liquidity, most likely due to single-depositor concentration risk.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 - Upside to the ratings is unlikely at present unless the bank's franchise and funding and liquidity profile materially strengthen.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATINGS\par \par \uc2 The National Ratings are sensitive to \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of the entity's creditworthiness relative to other Nigerian issuers'.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria. ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\par \par \uc2 Sterling Bank Plc\uc2  ; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B-; Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; National Long Term Rating; Upgrade; BBB+(nga)\par \par \uc2 ; National Short Term Rating; Upgrade; F2(nga)\par \par \uc2 ; Viability Rating; Affirmed; b-\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating; Affirmed; 5\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating Floor; Affirmed; NF\par \par \uc2 Contacts:\par \par \uc2 Primary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Vincent Martin,\par \par \uc2 Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1828\par \par \uc2 vincent.martin@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings Ltd\par \par \uc2 30 North Colonnade, Canary Wharf\par \par \uc2 London E14 5GN\par \par \uc2 Secondary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Tim Slater,\par \par \uc2 Associate Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1791\par \par \uc2 tim.slater@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Committee Chairperson\par \par \uc2 Lindsey Liddell,\par \par \uc2 Seni