\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch Affirms Coronation Merchant Bank at 'B-'; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 2,951 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2021\par \uc2 Fitch Rating / Non Rating Action Commentary\par \uc2 FITRA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 by Fitch Ratings Inc., Fitch Ratings Ltd. and its subsidiaries. \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 -London-30 September 2021: \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed Nigeria-based Coronation Merchant Bank Limited's (CMB) Long-Term Issuer Default Rating (IDR) at 'B-' with a Stable Outlook. The National Long-Term Rating has been upgraded to 'BBB+(nga)' from 'BBB(nga)', reflecting the bank's improved creditworthiness relative to that of other issuers in Nigeria.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has also withdrawn the Long-Term Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR due to a publishing error.\par \par \uc2 A full list of rating actions is below.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 IDRS AND VIABILITY RATING (VR)\par \par \uc2 The Long- and Short-Term IDRs of CMB are driven by its standalone credit profile as expressed by its VR of 'b-'. Its VR reflects the bank's concentration in and sensitivity to Nigerian operating environment risks and is also constrained by the bank's niche franchise and structural funding and liquidity weaknesses resulting from the absence of a retail deposit license. This is balanced by the bank's sound asset-quality record and adequate capitalisation and performance, which constitute rating strengths.\par \par \uc2 The Stable Outlook reflects \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view that risks to CMB's credit profile are captured in the current rating, with sufficient headroom under our base case to absorb the fallout from operating-environment pressures.\par \par \uc2 Operating conditions in Nigeria are gradually stabilising and \uc2 Fitch\uc2  forecasts 1.9% GDP growth in 2021, following a 1.8% decline in 2020. Our baseline scenario is that business volumes and earnings should continue to rebound in 2021, while the rally in oil prices is also a positive factor. Nevertheless, downside risks linger, given inherently volatile market conditions, with banks still exposed to foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) shortages, potential further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation, rising inflation and regulatory \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2  (CBN)\b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Nigeria\uc2  (CBN).\par \par \uc2 CMB's company profile is a constraint on, and a factor of high importance to, the rating, reflecting the bank's niche - but leading - franchise as an independent Nigerian merchant bank engaged in corporate and trade finance, domestic capital markets and investment banking. It also considers CMB's small size (under 1% of banking-sector assets) and reliance on expensive, short-term and concentrated wholesale deposit and market funding. Management quality is a rating strength.\par \par \uc2 CMB has recorded zero Stage 3 loans since converting to a merchant bank in 2015, despite volatile market conditions, reflecting its prudent underwriting standards and risk controls. It reports no Stage 2 loans nor loans benefiting from Covid-19-related debt relief measures. Nevertheless, asset quality is sensitive to very high borrower concentrations (the 20 largest exposures equalled 2.6x equity at end-1H21) and FC-linked loans (51% of total loans). However, non-loan earning assets are large (43% of assets at end-1H21) - comprising mainly Nigerian government securities (B/Stable) held for liquidity and revenue purposes - and support asset quality.\par \par \uc2 CMB's profitability remains adequate, as indicated by operating returns on risk-weighted assets of 2.2% in 1H21 (four-year average: 4.7%), supported by fairly diversified revenue streams (non-interest income comprised 80% of operating income in 1H21), solid asset quality and good cost control (1H21: cost-to-income ratio of 57%, outperforming small-bank peers'). However, its fairly short performance record and potential for earnings volatility - resulting from high exposure to market-sensitive income - increases risks to profitability. Trading-related income made up a high 53% of operating income in 1H21.\par \par \uc2 The bank's profitability has also been negatively affected by margin pressure from low yield government securities (including special bills) and zero-earning restricted deposits with the \uc2 CBN\uc2  (equal to a combined 27% of total assets at end-1H21). In addition, its net interest margin (0.7% in 1H21) is inherently much lower than commercial banks' as CMB does not have a license to attract retail deposits - a cheaper source of funding.\par \par \uc2 CMB's Fitch Core Capital (\uc2 FCC\uc2 ) ratio of 15.6% at end-1H21 is adequate but has more than halved since 2017, due to rapid loan growth. Its tangible equity/assets ratio fell to 7.7% at end-1H21 (end-2020: 8.3%; end-2019: 11.4%). CMB's total capital adequacy ratio (CAR) of 18.5% at end-1H21 is well above its minimum regulatory requirement of 10%, supported by an NGN25 billion five-year Tier 2 debt issue in 2020. We expect the CAR to stabilise between 12.5% and 15% over the medium term as asset growth outpaces internal capital generation.\par \par \uc2 CMB's capital base also remains small in absolute terms, resulting in high single-name concentration risk and increasing the bank's sensitivity to individual credit events or unforeseen losses. Capitalisation remains sensitive to naira depreciation.\par \par \uc2 As a merchant bank, CMB relies on short-term wholesale funding, including a large share of corporate deposits (42% of funding), but also bank borrowings and commercial paper, which are price- and confidence-sensitive. Corporate deposits are concentrated by name, with the top 20 deposits forming 65% of total deposits at end-1H21. FC liabilities comprised 32% of CMB's liabilities at end-1H21, and are sensitive to investor sentiment and operating conditions, increasing refinancing and liquidity risks.\par \par \uc2 SUPPORT RATING AND SUPPORT RATING FLOOR\par \par \uc2 Sovereign support to CMB cannot be relied on given Nigeria's weak ability to provide support, particularly in FC. The Support Rating Floor of all Nigerian banks is 'No Floor' and all Support Ratings are '5'. This reflects our view that senior creditors cannot rely on receiving full and timely extraordinary support from the Nigerian sovereign if any of the banks become non-viable. Sovereign propensity to provide support to CMB is weak, in our view, given the bank's very low systemic importance.\par \par \uc2 Rating Sensitivities\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 CMB's ratings are sensitive to a weakening in capitalisation, including a further increase in leverage, likely resulting from further rapid loan growth or material asset-quality deterioration (given its high borrower concentrations) that significantly erode its capital buffers. Tightening FC funding and liquidity due to adverse market conditions could be negative for its ratings given its wholesale-funding reliance.\par \par \uc2 A strategic shift towards higher-risk exposures, increased borrower concentrations or material \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2 -risk losses could also trigger a negative rating action, although this is not our base case.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 A rating upgrade is unlikely at present given the bank's limited, niche franchise and constraints on its funding and liquidity profile.\par \par \uc2 OTHER DEBT AND ISSUER RATINGS: KEY RATING DRIVERS\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATINGS\par \par \uc2 The National Ratings of CMB reflect its creditworthiness relative to that of other issuers in Nigeria and are driven by its standalone strength. The upgrade of CMB's National Long-Term Rating reflects its increased creditworthiness relative to other Nigerian issuers'. Its National Short-Term Rating is the lower of two possible options for a 'BBB+(nga)' National Long-Term Rating under \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's criteria, reflecting weaknesses in the bank's funding and liquidity profile, which increase the vulnerability of default on its short-term local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  obligations within Nigeria.\par \par \uc2 OTHER DEBT AND ISSUER RATINGS: RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATINGS\par \par \uc2 The National Ratings are sensitive to a negative change in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of the entity's creditworthiness relative to other Nigerian issuers.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATINGS\par \par \uc2 The National Ratings are sensitive to a positive change in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of the entity's creditworthiness relative to other Nigerian issuers.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579}}}\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING\par \par \uc2 The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\par \par \uc2 Coronation Merchant Bank Limited; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B-; Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Withdrawn; WD\par \par \uc2 ; National Long Term Rating; Upgrade; BBB+(nga)\par \par \uc2 ; National Short Term Rating; Upgrade; F2(nga)\par \par \uc2 ; Viability Rating; Affirmed; b-\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating; Affirmed; 5\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating Floor; Affirmed; NF\par \par \uc2 Contacts:\par \par \uc2 Primary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Kurt Boere,\par \par \uc2 Associate Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 2707\par \par \uc2 kurt.boere@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings Ltd\par \par \uc2 30 North Colonnade, Canary Wharf\par \par \uc2 London E14 5GN\par \par \uc2 Secondary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Jamal El Mellali,\par \par \uc2 Associate Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1969\par \par \uc2 jamal.elmellali@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Committee Chairperson\par \par \uc2 Lindsey Liddell,\par \par \uc2 Senior Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1008\par \par \uc2 lindsey.liddell@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 MEDIA RELATIONS: Louisa Williams, London, Tel: +44 20 3530 2452, Email: louisa.williams@thefitchgroup.com\par \par \uc2 Additional information is available on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\par \par \uc2 ADDITIONAL DISCLOSURES\par \par \uc2 Dodd-Frank Rating Information Disclosure Form: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/dodd-frank-disclosure/10179145"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/dodd-frank-disclosure/10179145}}}\par \par \uc2 Solicitation Status: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10179145#solicitation-status"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10179145#solicitation-status}}}\par \par \uc2 Additional Disclosures For Unsolicited Credit Ratings: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10179145#unsolicited-credit-ratings-disclosures"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10179145#unsolicited-credit-ratings-disclosures}}}\par \par \uc2 Endorsement Status: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10179145#endorsement-status"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10179145#endorsement-status}}}\par \par \uc2 Endorsement Policy: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10179145#endorsement-policy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10179145#endorsement-policy}}}\par \par \uc2 ALL FITCH CREDIT RATINGS ARE SUBJECT TO CERTAIN LIMITATIONS AND DISCLAIMERS. PLEASE READ THESE LIMITATIONS AND DISCLAIMERS BY FOLLOWING THIS LINK:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://WWW.FITCHRATINGS.COM/UNDERSTANDINGCREDITRATINGS"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://WWW.FITCHRATINGS.COM/UNDERSTANDINGCREDITRA