\par\par \b \uc2 *Fitch Affirms Fidelity Bank at 'B-'; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 2,629 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2021\par 16:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 24 Sep 2021 10:51 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms Fidelity Bank  at 'B-'; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par       \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  -London-24 September 2021: \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed \uc2 Fidelity Bank PLC\uc2  's Long-Term Issuer Default Rating (IDR) at 'B-' with a Stable Outlook. The National Long-Term Rating has been upgraded to 'BBB+(nga)' from 'BBB(nga)', reflecting the bank's increased creditworthiness relative to other issuers in Nigeria.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 IDRS AND VIABILITY RATING\par \par \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  's IDRs are driven by its standalone creditworthiness, as expressed by its Viability Rating (VR) of 'b-'. The VR reflects its concentration and sensitivity to Nigeria's challenging operating environment and is constrained by a moderate franchise, high credit concentrations, an aggressive growth appetite and asset quality pressures. These constraints are balanced against reasonable profitability, capitalisation and liquidity.\par \par \uc2 The Stable Outlook reflects \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view that risks to \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  's credit profile are captured by the current rating, with sufficient headroom under our base case to absorb the fallout from operating environment pressures.\par \par \uc2 Operating conditions in Nigeria are gradually stabilising and \uc2 Fitch\uc2  forecasts 1.9% GDP growth in 2021, following a 1.8% decline in 2020. Our baseline scenario is that business volumes and earnings should continue to rebound in 2021, while the rally in oil prices is also a positive factor. Nevertheless, downside risks linger, given inherently volatile market conditions, with banks still exposed to foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) shortages, potential further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation, rising inflation and regulatory \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2  (CBN) \uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  is the sixth-largest bank in Nigeria, representing 6% of banking system assets at end-2020. Single-borrower concentration is high, with the 20-largest customer loans representing 253% of Fitch Core Capital (\uc2 FCC\uc2  ) at end-1H21. Exposure to the oil and gas sector is also high, representing 26% of gross loans and 160% of \uc2 FCC\uc2  , and largely concentrated within the upstream and services segments, posing a significant risk to asset quality in the event of a prolonged period of low oil prices and production cuts.\par \par \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  's impaired loans ratio (end-1H21: 2.7%) is among the lowest across the banking sector. It declined from 3.8% at end-2020, reflecting reclassifications but also strong loan growth. Loan growth has been the key driver of the fairly sharp decline in the bank's impaired loans ratio in recent years, but creates seasoning risks. Specific coverage of impaired loans (70%) is adequate in view of collateral coverage.\par \par \uc2 Restructured loans (end-1H21: 30% of loans; split 77%/20%/3% between Stage 1, 2 and 3) are material, largely reflecting on-lending facilities that were subject to mandatory restructuring at the onset of the pandemic. Stage 2 loans (20% of loans) - concentrated in the power and oil and gas sectors - were also high, although we do not expect them to become impaired given the improving outlook for these sectors.\par \par \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  reports reasonable profitability, as indicated by operating returns on risk-weighted assets that have averaged 2.1% over the past four full years. Profitability weakened slightly in 2020 as result of pandemic-related loan impairment charges (LICs), but improved in 1H21 due to lower LICs, reflecting easing asset quality risks, despite net interest margin pressure mainly from lower yields on government securities.\par \par \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  's \uc2 FCC\uc2  ratio declined 17.6% at end-1H21 from 19.1% at end-2020, eroded by strong loan growth. Internal capital generation was limited due to large mark-to-market losses on government securities held at fair value through other comprehensive income. Pre-impairment operating profit, which has averaged 3.2% of average gross loans over the past four full years, provides a moderate buffer to absorb loan impairment charges.\par \par \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  's customer deposit base (end-1H21: 76% of non-equity funding) comprises a high percentage of demand and savings accounts (75%) and a moderate percentage of retail accounts. The loans/customer deposits ratio (81%) is fairly high by domestic standards, partly reflecting its higher loans/assets ratio and on-lending. Depositor concentration is significant, with the 20 largest depositors representing 27% of customer deposits. Liquidity coverage is good in both local and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SENIOR UNSECURED DEBT\par \par \uc2 Senior unsecured debt is rated at the same level as the Long-Term IDR because in our view, the likelihood of default on these notes reflects that of the bank. The Recovery Rating assigned to these notes is 'RR4', indicating average recovery prospects.\par \par \uc2 SUPPORT RATING AND SUPPORT RATING FLOOR\par \par \uc2 Sovereign support to commercial banks cannot be relied on given Nigeria's weak ability to provide support, particularly in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Support Rating Floor of all Nigerian banks is 'No Floor' and all Support Ratings are '5'. This reflects our view that senior creditors cannot rely on receiving full and timely extraordinary support from the Nigerian sovereign if any of the banks become non-viable.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATINGS\par \par \uc2 Fidelity 's National Ratings reflect its creditworthiness relative to other issuers in Nigeria and are driven by its standalone strength. The upgrade of the National Long-Term Rating reflects its increased creditworthiness relative to other Nigerian issuers. Its National Short-Term Rating is the higher of the two possible options under \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's criteria, reflecting the relative strength of the bank's funding and liquidity profile, which reduces the vulnerability of default on its short-term local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  obligations within Nigeria.\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 - An upgrade of the Long-Term IDR would require a strengthening of the bank's company profile and franchise.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 - A combination of an increase in the impaired loans ratio above 10% and aggressive growth that results in very thin buffers over regulatory capital requirements or a sharp decline in the \uc2 FCC\uc2  ratio.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria. ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\par \par \uc2 Fidelity Bank PLC\uc2  ; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B-; Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; National Long Term Rating; Upgrade; BBB+(nga)\par \par \uc2 ; National Short Term Rating; Upgrade; F1(nga)\par \par \uc2 ; Viability Rating; Affirmed; b-\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating; Affirmed; 5\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating Floor; Affirmed; NF\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Long Term Rating; Affirmed; B-\par \par \uc2 Contacts:\par \par \uc2 Primary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Vincent Martin,\par \par \uc2 Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1828\par \par \uc2 vincent.martin@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings Ltd\par \par \uc2 30 North Colonnade, Canary Wharf\par \par \uc2 London E14 5GN\par \par \uc2 Secondary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Tim Slater,\par \par \uc2 Associate Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1791\par \par