\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch Affirms \uc2 Zenith Bank\uc2  at 'B'; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 1,275 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2021\par \uc2 ENP Newswire\par \uc2 ENPNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Electronic News Publishing. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Release date - 21092021\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed \uc2 Zenith Bank Plc\uc2 's Long-Term Issuer Default Rating (IDR) at 'B' with a Stable Outlook.\par \par \uc2 A full list of rating actions is below.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 IDRS AND VIABILITY RATING\par \par \uc2 The Long- and Short-Term IDRs of \uc2 Zenith\uc2  are driven by its standalone creditworthiness, as expressed by its Viability Rating (VR) of 'b'. The VR reflects the constraint of Nigeria's challenging operating environment and the bank's high exposure to the Nigerian sovereign (B/Stable). It also reflects the bank's strong domestic franchise and a solid financial profile by domestic standards.\par \par \uc2 The Stable Outlook reflects Fitch's view that risks to \uc2 Zenith\uc2 's credit profile are captured at the current rating level, with sufficient headroom, under our base case, to absorb the fallout from operating- environment pressures.\par \par \uc2 Operating conditions in Nigeria are gradually stabilising and Fitch forecasts 1.9% GDP growth in 2021, following a 1.8% decline in 2020. Our baseline scenario is that business volumes and earnings should continue to rebound in 2021, while the rally in oil prices is also a positive factor. Nevertheless, downside risks linger, given inherently volatile market conditions, with banks still exposed to foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) shortages, potential further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation, rising inflation and regulatory \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2  (CBN)\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Zenith\uc2  is the second-largest banking group in Nigeria, representing 16% of domestic banking-system assets at end-2020. It has a strong franchise in corporate banking and the centrepiece of its strategy is to deepen retail-market penetration through digital platforms.\par \par \uc2 Single-borrower concentration is moderate, with the 20-largest customer loans representing 96% of Fitch Core Capital (\uc2 FCC\uc2 ) at end-1H21. Its oil and gas exposure is material, representing 23% of gross loans and 62% of \uc2 FCC\uc2  at end-1H21, and is concentrated on the upstream segment, posing a significant risk to asset quality in the event of a prolonged period of low oil prices and production cuts. Restructured loans (a high 24% of gross loans at end-1H21) are concentrated in the upstream oil and gas sector.\par \par \uc2 Loan-quality metrics have weakened as a result of the pandemic. However, net loans represent a small percentage of assets (33% at end-1H21). Its impaired loans (Stage 3 loans under IFRS 9) ratio increased to 7.9% at end-1H21 from 6.8% at end-2019. Specific loan loss allowance coverage (54% at end-1H21) of impaired loans is adequate. Stage 2 loans represented 20% of gross loans at end-1H21. Most restructured loans are classified as Stage 2, but are sufficiently collateralised and not expected to become impaired.\par \par \uc2 Our asset-quality assessment also considers a substantial amount of non-loan assets, largely comprising government securities and cash reserves at the CBN. Sovereign exposure through government securities is high (estimated at over 150% of \uc2 FCC\uc2  at end-1H21) and represents a constraint on the VR.\par \par \uc2 Profitability is solid, as indicated by operating returns on risk-weighted assets that have averaged 6.3% over the past four years. It is supported by a low cost of funding, strong non-interest income and manageable loan impairment charges. Weaker profitability in recent years has been driven by a declining interest-rate environment and increased cash-reserve debits that have weighed on its net interest margin.\par \par \uc2 Capitalisation is a rating strength. \uc2 Zenith\uc2 's \uc2 FCC\uc2  ratio of 21.7% at end-1H21 reflects a balance sheet that exhibits low leverage, as indicated by a fairly high tangible leverage ratio of 13.3% at end-1H21. Pre-impairment operating profit is very strong, averaging 12% of average gross loans over the past four years, providing a large buffer to absorb loan impairment charges.\par \par \uc2 Funding is mainly in the form of a stable and inexpensive customer deposit base, comprising a high percentage of current and savings accounts (93% at end-1H21), with large volumes sourced from individuals and SMEs. Single-depositor concentration is very low, with the 20-largest depositors representing just 4% of customer deposits at end-1H21. Liquidity coverage is comfortable in both local and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SENIOR UNSECURED DEBT\par \par \uc2 Senior unsecured debt is rated at the same level as \uc2 Zenith\uc2 's IDRs because in our view the likelihood of default on these notes reflects that of the bank. The Recovery Rating of these notes is 'RR4', indicating average recovery prospects.\par \par \uc2 SUPPORT RATING AND SUPPORT RATING FLOOR\par \par \uc2 Sovereign support to commercial banks cannot be relied on given Nigeria's weak ability to provide support, particularly in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Support Rating Floor of all Nigerian banks is 'No Floor' and all Support Ratings are '5'. This reflects our view that senior creditors cannot rely on receiving full and timely extraordinary support from the Nigerian sovereign if any of the banks become non-viable.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATINGS\par \par \uc2 The National Ratings of \uc2 Zenith\uc2  reflect its creditworthiness relative to that of other issuers in Nigeria and are driven by its standalone strength. They are at the higher end of the scale, reflecting \uc2 Zenith\uc2 's comparatively strong domestic franchise, profitability, capitalisation, funding structure and liquidity coverage.\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 An upgrade of the Long-Term IDR would require a sovereign upgrade and a continued record of solid financial metrics.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 A sovereign downgrade would result in a downgrade of the Long-Term IDR given that \uc2 Zenith\uc2  does not meet Fitch's criteria to be rated above the sovereign.\par \par \uc2 A combination of an increase in the impaired loans ratio significantly above 10% and aggressive growth at home or abroad that results in very thin buffers over regulatory capital requirements or a sharp decline in the \uc2 FCC\uc2  ratio.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579}}}\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING\par \par \uc2 The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on Fitch's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\par \par \uc2 RATING ACTIONSENTITY/DEBT RATING RECOVERY PRIOR\par \par \uc2 Zenith Bank Plc\uc2  LT IDR B Affirmed B\par \par \uc2 ST IDR B Affirmed B\par \par \uc2 Natl LT AA-(nga) Affirmed AA-(nga)\par \par \uc2 Natl ST F1+(nga) Affirmed F1+(nga)\par \par \uc2 Viability b Affirmed b\par \par \uc2 Support 5 Affirmed 5\par \par \uc2 Support Floor NF Affirmed NF\par \par \uc2 senior unsecured\par \par \uc2 LT B Affirmed RR4 B\par \par \uc2 senior unsecured\par \par \uc2 ST B Affirmed B\par \par \uc2 VIEW ADDITIONAL RATING DETAILS\par \par \uc2 Additional information is available on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\par \par \uc2 [Editorial queries for this story should be sent to newswire@enpublishing.co.uk]\par \par \uc2 Dokument ENPNEW0020210922eh9m0002l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
