\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Nigerian stocks record biggest weekly fall in 8-years\b0\par\par\uc2 331 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2021\par 15:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds details)\par \par \uc2 By Chijioke Ohuocha\par \par \uc2 ABUJA, Feb 12 (Reuters) - Nigerian stocks recorded their biggest weekly fall in 8-years on Friday, as investors anticipated a rise in debt yields after the government laid out plans to borrow more on the domestic market.\par \par \uc2 Nigerian stocks had been rising since October with investors shifting into equities after two interest rate cuts last year and excess naira liquidity on money markets due to historic low yields on the debt market.\par \par \uc2 The all-share index, which rose 50% in 2020, shed 3.04% this week, the most since July 2013. Stocks dropped to 40,439.85 points on Friday, down 1.4% at a one-month low following eight days of losses.\par \par \uc2 The government on Thursday approved plans to trim offshore borrowings, in a new debt strategy, to strengthen domestic markets over the next three-years.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say local funds could pull out of equities.\par \par \uc2 At an open market Treasury auction on Thursday, investors sought one-year yields as high as 13%, traders said, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  almost doubled the rate to 11.22% last week. Yields traded close to zero last year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been exploring ways to attract dollars into Nigeria to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stimulate growth following a recession without devaluing the naira, which could further stoke double-digit inflation.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said renewed inflows into the local debt market were being held back by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially with some investors trapped in \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid a pause in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the spot market.\par \par \uc2 Shares of five companies fell on Friday while 27 advanced and another 100 recorded no trades.\par \par \uc2 Animal feeds maker, \uc2 Livestock Feeds Plc\uc2  shed 9.78%. Clinker mixer \uc2 BUA Cement\uc2  fell 7.22% while \uc2 Dangote Cement\uc2  dropped 3.51% and MTN Nigeria was down 0.49% (Reporting by Chijioke Ohuocha; Editing by Hugh Lawson, Kirsten Donovan) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-2-12T15:02:31.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210212eh2c02j1t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
