\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Nigerian regulators set deadline for 9mobile takeover bid\b0\par\par\uc2 359 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 2018\par 18:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, quote, background)\par \par \uc2 ABUJA, Jan 11 (Reuters) - Nigerian regulators have set a Jan. 16 deadline for receipt of binding offers from prospective bidders to acquire debt-laden telecom firm 9mobile, the telecoms regulator said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Nigerian Communications Commission (NCC) \uc2 alongside the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  approved the deadline after 9mobile's board requested a time extension, the NCC said.\par \par \uc2 It added that \uc2 Barclays Africa\uc2  will review bids submitted before the deadline and make recommendations to 9mobile.\par \par \uc2 Nigerian lenders picked \uc2 Barclays Africa\uc2  to try to find new investors for 9mobile after banks took over the telecoms firm, formerly called \uc2 Etisalat Nigeria\uc2 , for defaulting on its loan.\par \par \uc2 "The winner will now apply to NCC in order to commence the processes for securing the regulatory approvals ... to give full effect to the transfer," the regulator said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Etisalat Nigeria\uc2  took out a $1.2 billion syndicated loan from a group of 13 local banks but struggled to make repayments due to a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis and recession in \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Nigerian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  then \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to save the company from collapse and prevent creditors from putting it into receivership, leading to a change in its board and management, as well as the new name 9mobile.\par \par \uc2 The crisis forced the telecoms company's one-time parent Etisalat to terminate its management agreement with its Nigerian business and surrender its 45 percent stake to a trustee following the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Private equity firm \uc2 Helios Investment Partners\uc2  has submitted a bid to acquire 9mobile. Nigeria's Globacom and \uc2 Bharti Airtel\uc2 's local subsidiary have also submitted bids, sources say.\par \par \uc2 Since the debt issue, 9mobile, the country's fourth biggest operator, has lost subscribers. In October its total number of users had fallen to 17.1 million, giving it a 12.2 percent market share, from 20 million subscribers with a 14 percent share earlier this year, the telecoms regulator said.\par \par \uc2 South Africa's MTN, the market leader has 36.1 percent. (Reporting by Camillus Eboh; Writing by Chijioke Ohuocha, editing by David Evans) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-1-11T18:28:19.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180111ee1b00oxc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
