\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-AFRICA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Kenya shilling, Zambia kwacha to firm, Ugandan shilling to weaken\b0\par\par\uc2 557 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 2018\par 16:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds Nigeria)\par \par \uc2 NAIROBI, Jan 11 (Reuters) - Kenya's shilling and Zambia's kwacha are expected to strengthen against the dollar over the week to next Thursday, while the Ugandan and Tanzanian shillings are forecast to weaken, traders said.\par \par \uc2 KENYA\par \par \uc2 The Kenyan shilling is expected to strengthen due to improved dollar inflows from remittances and horticulture, while general importer demand is also seen increasing.\par \par \uc2 Commercial banks quoted the shilling at 103.15/35 per dollar, the same as last Thursday's close.\par \par \uc2 "It could gain against the dollar because of improved inflows from remittances from overseas," said a senior trader from a commercial bank.\par \par \uc2 UGANDA\par \par \uc2 The Ugandan shilling is expected to weaken and probably go past 3,700 in the coming days due to demand for dollars from manufacturing and energy companies and offshore investors selling government securities.\par \par \uc2 Commercial banks quoted the shilling at 3,655/3,665, weaker than last Thursday's close of 3,640/3,650. On Wednesday \uc2 Bank of Uganda\uc2  sold an undisclosed amount of dollars to relieve the downward pressure on the shilling.\par \par \uc2 "We anticipate that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be quite active ... and manage the depreciation but if they stay out we'll hit 3,700," Faisal Bukenya, head of treasury at \uc2 Exim Bank\uc2 , said.\par \par \uc2 NIGERIA\par \par \uc2 The naira is expected to be largely unchanged for foreign investors next week after a slow start of trading at the beginning of the year.\par \par \uc2 The naira traded at 360 to the dollar for investors, compared with last Thursday's close of 362.\par \par \uc2 Traders said volumes were low but foreign investors have resumed selling which would help boost dollar liquidity on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 On the official market, it was quoted at 305.80, supported by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's regular \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2  has at least five exchange rates for the naira.\par \par \uc2 TANZANIA\par \par \uc2 The Tanzanian shilling is expected to weaken slightly, weighed down by dollar demand from oil companies and subdued agriculture exports.\par \par \uc2 Commercial banks quoted the shilling at 2,240/2,245 to the dollar on Thursday, weaker than 2,239/2,244 a week ago.\par \par \uc2 "The shilling will likely weaken next week due to increased demand for dollars from oil importers," said a trader at CRDB Bank.\par \par \uc2 GHANA\par \par \uc2 Ghana's cedi is expected to remain stable on improving liquidity, helped by positive market sentiment and targeted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  support.\par \par \uc2 The local unit was trading at 4.5490 to the dollar compared with 4.5407 at last Thursday's close.\par \par \uc2 "We do not expect a significant departure in the dollar/cedi trend in the coming week due to slow activities in the early weeks of the year," said Raphael Adubila, Treasury officer of the Accra-based Northstar Home Finance.\par \par \uc2 ZAMBIA\par \par \uc2 The kwacha is expected to strengthen, helped by companies selling dollars to pay taxes.\par \par \uc2 Commercial banks quoted the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Africa\u8217\'20\'19s second-largest copper producer at 9.8200 per dollar, up from 10.1000 a week ago.\par \par \uc2 "The local unit is expected to remain bullish in the interim should supply continue to outweigh demand," Cavmont Bank said in a note. (Reporting by John Ndiso, Fumbuka Ng'wanakilala, Elias Biryabarema, Chris Mfula, Kwasi Kpodo and Chijioke Ohuocha; Compiled by George Obulutsa Editing by Jeremy Gaunt) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-1-11T16:14:25.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180111ee1b00kzy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
