\par\par \b \uc2 Helios, others bid for Nigerian telecoms firm 9mobile -sources\b0\par\par\uc2 321 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Dezember 2017\par 15:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LAGOS, Dec 8 (Reuters) - Private equity firm \uc2 Helios Investment Partners\uc2  has submitted a bid to acquire Nigeria's 9mobile under a sale process aimed at finding new investors for the debt-laden telecoms firm, one person fimilar with the matter told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Nigerian lenders have picked \uc2 Barclays\uc2  to try to find new investors for 9mobile after banks took over the telecoms firm, formerly called \uc2 Etisalat Nigeria\uc2 , for defaulting on its loan.\par \par \uc2 Since the debt issue, 9mobile, the country's fourth biggest operator, has lost subscribers. In October its total number of users had fallen to 17.1 million, giving it a 12.2 percent market share, from 20 million subscribers with a 14 percent share earlier this year, the telecoms regulator said.\par \par \uc2 South Africa's MTN, the market leader has 36.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 Nigeria's Globacom and \uc2 Bharti Airtel\uc2 's local subsidiary also submited bids, another source familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 Smile Telecoms\uc2  and Teleology Holdings Limited have been shortlisted, Business Day reported.\par \par \uc2 Helios declined to comment. Globacom, Airtel, Smile and Teleology could not be reached for comment.\par \par \uc2 Bidders are required to submit a firm financial bid and conduct due diligence by Dec 31, the local paper reported.\par \par \uc2 Etisalat Nigeria\uc2  took out a $1.2 billion syndicated loan from a group of 13 local banks but struggled to make repayments this year due to a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis and recession in \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Nigerian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to save the company from collapse and prevent creditors from putting it into receivership, leading to a change in its board and management, as well as the new name 9mobile.\par \par \uc2 The crisis forced the telecoms company's one-time parent Etisalat to terminate its management agreement with its Nigerian business and surrender its 45 percent stake to a trustee following the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (Editing by Greg Mahlich) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-12-8T15:33:53.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020171208edc800m2x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
