\par\par \b \uc2 Nigeria's Economy Expected to Grow 3.5% in 2018, President Says\b0\par\par\uc2 By Obafemi Oredein \par \uc2 411 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2017\par 23:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2    Special to Dow Jones Newswires \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IBADAN, Nigeria--Nigeria's economy is forecast to grow 3.5% in 2018, President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Muhammadu Buhari\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Tuesday in his budget speech.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The president also said the inflation rate is expected to reach 12.4% next year, while the naira's exchange rate is projected at NGN305 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Buhari said stability has been restored to the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market due to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2  to improve access to liquidity, discourage \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation and increase net \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows.\par \par \uc2 "Indeed, by the second quarter of 2017, exports significantly outpaced imports, resulting in a trade surplus of NGN506.5 billion ($1.4 billion)," Mr. Buhari said.\par \par \uc2 The naira has stabilized recently as the CBN \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  with dollar sales in the official market. The naira is trading around NGN306 to the dollar in the official market but has been fetching around NGN360 to the dollar in the parallel market.\par \par \uc2 Last year, a shortage of dollars due to plummeting oil prices adversely affected businesses, with traders and manufacturers unable to import essential goods and raw materials for production. This resulted in a scarcity of essential goods, the closure of factories and high unemployment.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Buhari said Nigeria's external reserves had increased to $34 billion as of Oct. 30, higher than the $32 billion on Sept. 22 announced by the CBN. Increases in oil prices accounted for the rise in external reserves.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Tuesday, Mr. Buhari's office unveiled a proposed national budget of NGN8.612 trillion for 2018 that would result in a projected deficit of NGN2.005 trillion.\par \par \uc2 As of June 30, Nigeria's total debt had climbed to NGN19.6 trillion, according to the Debt Management Office. For 2018, the government has allocated NGN2.014 trillion for debt service, up from NGN1.841 trillion in 2017.\par \par \uc2 The country has been borrowing to support its 2017 budget. Mr. Buhari recently sought Senate approval for a $5 billion loan package.\par \par \uc2 "We are closely monitoring our debt service to revenue ratio. We shall address this ratio through our non-oil revenue-generation drive and restructuring of the existing debt portfolio," Mr. Buhari said. "Presently, domestic debt accounts for about 78% of the total debt."\par \par \uc2 Write to Obafemi Oredein at barcelonaeditors@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 07, 2017 17:18 ET (22:18 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020171107edb70056f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
