\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Nigerian lenders pick \uc2 Barclays\uc2  to find new investors for 9mobile -sources\b0\par\par\uc2 439 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2017\par 19:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, background)\par \par \uc2 By Chijioke Ohuocha\par \par \uc2 LAGOS, Oct 19 (Reuters) - Nigerian lenders have picked \uc2 Barclays\uc2  to try to find new investors for debt-laden 9mobile, two banking sources said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Barclays\uc2  has started work on the mandate and is in the process of setting up a database for prospective investors to conduct due diligence, they said.\par \par \uc2 Banking sources had previously said \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  and \uc2 Standard Bank\uc2  were in the running for the role.\par \par \uc2 But the lenders decided against them due to their previous links with 9mobile, the sources said on Thursday. Standard's local unit, \uc2 Stanbic IBTC Bank\uc2 , is among the group of lenders to 9mobile while Citi has advised the telecom company, formerly known as \uc2 Etisalat Nigeria\uc2 , in the past, they said.\par \par \uc2 Barclays\uc2  was not immediately available for comment.\par \par \uc2 Etisalat Nigeria\uc2  took out a $1.2 billion syndicated loan from a group of 13 local banks but struggled to make repayments this year due to a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis and recession in \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Nigerian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to save the company from collapse and prevent creditors from putting it into receivership, leading to a change in its board and management, as well as the new name 9mobile.\par \par \uc2 The crisis forced the telecoms company's one-time parent Etisalat to terminate its management agreement with its Nigerian business and surrender its 45 percent stake to a trustee following the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 9mobile CEO Boye Olusanya has said he is focused on getting the telecoms company back on track to make a profit, while working on the paperwork to eventually raise new capital, adding the company was open to new investors.\par \par \uc2 Lenders have put a freeze on collecting the principal and interest payments on the syndicate loan pending new investors, in order to help the company survive, the sources told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 They have also held back on taking provisions for the syndicated loan and agreed to extend it after the regulatory \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in July.\par \par \uc2 The sources said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had asked the lenders to take a five percent provision on the loan as part of their-quarter results due this month.\par \par \uc2 Some lenders, such as \uc2 Zenith Bank\uc2 , UBA , and \uc2 Access Bank\uc2 , have made already provisions to cover direct lending to 9mobile outside the syndicated loan.\par \par \uc2 9mobile has 20 million subscribers with a 14 percent share of the Nigerian market. South Africa's MTN is the market leader with 47 percent, Nigeria's Globacom has 20 percent and \uc2 Bharti Airtel\uc2 's local subsidiary has 19 percent. (Editing by Mark Potter) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-10-19T18:28:48.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020171019edaj00q08\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
