\par\par \b \uc2 MTN - \uc2 MTN GROUP LIMITED\uc2  - Reviewed Condensed Consolidated Financial Results for the six months ended 30 June 2017\b0\par\par\uc2 13,173 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2017\par 09:09\par \uc2 Johannesburg Stock Exchange\par \uc2 JSEXCH\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2017 Johannesburg Stock Exchange. All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reviewed Condensed Consolidated Financial Results for the six months ended 30 June 2017\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Group Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par (Incorporated in the Republic of South Africa)\par (Registration number 1994/009584/06)\par (Share code MTN)\par (ISIN ZAE000042164)\par ("MTN" or "the Group")\par \par REVIEWED CONDENSED CONSOLIDATED FINANCIAL RESULTS FOR THE SIX MONTHS ENDED 30 JUNE 2017\par \par SALIENT FEATURES\par Group revenue                                 Group service revenue       \par up by 6,7%*                                   up by 7,5%*                      \par (decreased by 18,5%)                          (decreased by 19,1%)        \par to R64 315 million                            to R60 003 million          \par \par Data revenue                                  Digital revenue             \par up by 31,9%*                                  up by 24,7%*                     \par (increased by 9,6%)                           (decreased by 9,3%)         \par to R13 952 million                            to R6 460 million           \par \par Active MTN Mobile Money customers             EBITDA                       \par up by 2,7 million                             up by 3,1%*              \par to 17,9 million                               (decreased by 27,7%)\par                                               to R21 179 million  \par                                                 \par EBITDA margin                                 Positive HEPS of  \par down by 4,2 percentage                        217 cents**\par points to 32,9%                                       \par \par Adjusted free cash flow                       Interim dividend of         \par down by 2,7%*                                 250 cents            \par (29,8%)                                       per share declared          \par to R10 874 million               \par \par *  Constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  information.\par ** Reported - includes the impact of pro forma adjustments as defined.\par EBITDA margin and numbers in brackets exclude pro forma adjustments as defined.\par \par \par RESULTS OVERVIEW\par Group President and CEO, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rob Shuter\f28 \uc2 \f2  comments: \par "We are seeing pleasing progress in our key growth drivers of data and digital services against headwinds of\par challenging macro-economic conditions and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pressures. We continue to strengthen our \par focus on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  excellence with our six strategic pillars integrated in our new BRIGHT strategy. Our \par focus during the second half of the year will be to entrench our BRIGHT strategy, complete our network \par investment programme and build medium-term financial KPIs and targets for the BRIGHT strategy."\par \par Note: Certain financial information presented in these interim financial results constitutes pro forma \par financial information. The pro forma financial information is the responsibility of the Group's Board of \par directors (the Board) and is presented for illustrative purposes only. Because of its nature, the pro forma \par financial information may not fairly present MTN's financial position, changes in equity, results of \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or cash flows. The pro forma financial information, including the constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  information \par (refer below) as well as any forward looking financial information incorporated in these condensed \par consolidated interim financial results, have not been audited or reviewed or otherwise reported on by \par our external joint auditors.\par 1. The financial information presented in these condensed consolidated financial results has been prepared \par    excluding the impact of hyperinflation and the relating goodwill and asset impairments, tower profits \par    (including a profit of R6 017 million relating to the profit realised on the exercise of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 IHS\f28 \uc2 \f2  exchange \par    right whereby the Group's interest in the Nigeria Tower Company was exchanged for additional shareholding \par    in IHS Holdings) and the Nigerian regulatory fine (consisting of the re-measurement impact when the \par    settlement was entered into and the finance costs recognised as a result of the unwind of the initial \par    discounting of the liability) (the pro forma adjustments) and constitutes pro forma financial information \par    to the extent that it is not extracted from the segment disclosure included in the reviewed condensed \par    consolidated interim financial statements for the period ended 30 June 2017. This pro forma financial \par    information has been presented to eliminate the impact of the pro forma adjustments from the financial \par    results in order to achieve a comparable analysis period on period. The pro forma adjustments have been \par    calculated in terms of the Group's accounting policies disclosed in the consolidated financial statements \par    for the year ended 31 December 2016. \par 2. Constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  information has been presented to illustrate the impact of changes in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates on \par    the Group's results. In determining the change in constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  terms, the current financial reporting \par    period's results have been adjusted to the prior period average exchange rates determined as the average \par    of the monthly exchange rates which can be found on \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mtn.com/investors"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.mtn.com/investors}}}\uc2 \f2 . The measurement has been performed \par    for each of the Group's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , materially being that of the US dollar and Nigerian naira. The constant \par    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  growth percentage has been calculated based on the current year constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  results compared \par    to the prior year results. In addition, in respect of MTN Irancell, MTN Sudan, MTN South Sudan and MTN Syria, \par    the constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  information has been prepared excluding the impact of hyperinflation. Hyperinflation \par    accounting was discontinued for MTN Irancell and MTN Sudan on 1 July 2015 and 1 July 2016 respectively. \par    The economy of South Sudan was assessed to be hyperinflationary effective 1 January 2016, and hyperinflation \par    accounting was applied from December 2016 onwards.\par *  Constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  information. \par ** Reported - includes the impact of the pro forma adjustments as defined.\par \par The Group's results are presented on a regional basis in line with the Group's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  structure. \par This comprises South and East Africa (SEA), West and Central Africa (WECA) and Middle East and North Africa \par (MENA) and their respective underlying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as further outlined below.\par \par The SEA region includes: South Africa, Uganda, Zambia, Rwanda, South Sudan, Botswana (joint venture, equity \par accounted), Swaziland (joint venture, equity accounted) and Business Group. The WECA region includes: Nigeria, \par Ghana, Cameroon, Ivory Coast, Benin, Congo-Brazzaville, Liberia, Guinea-Conakry and Guinea-Bissau. The MENA \par region includes: Iran (joint venture, equity accounted), Syria, Sudan, Yemen, Afghanistan and Cyprus. \par Although Iran, Botswana and Swaziland form part of their respective regions geographically and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operationally\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \par they are excluded from their respective regional results because they are equity accounted for by the Group.\par \par OVERVIEW\par MTN delivered improved results for the six months ended 30 June 2017 and is on track to meet the financial \par year 2017 (FY2017) guidance communicated in March 2017. Macro-economic conditions remain challenging across \par a number of our markets, with Nigeria continuing to experience a weaker naira as well as hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par liquidity challenges. Although South Africa entered a technical recession in the first quarter, the rand \par strengthened considerably against the US dollar during the period, while many of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in our \par other markets weakened. Despite these macro-challenges, the Group continues to deliver on its \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  targets.\par \par During the past six months the management team undertook a thorough review of the Group strategy and developed \par a clear growth plan for MTN that will be arranged under six strategic pillars comprising: Best customer experience; \par Returns and efficiency focus; IGNITE commercial performance; Growth through data and digital; Hearts and minds; \par and Technology excellence. We refer to this as the "BRIGHT" strategy. \par \par BRIGHT builds on work done over the past 18 months, in particular the Project IGNITE, our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  execution\par programme embarked on last year, in our two largest markets MTN South Africa and MTN Nigeria.\par \par Group revenue in constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  grew by 6,7%* to R64 315 million, underpinned by 10,8%* growth in revenue in\par Nigeria and a 5,2% (on an organic basis) improvement in service revenue growth in South Africa. MTN Uganda, MTN \par Ghana and MTN Ivory Coast also contributed positively to the Group's top-line growth. MTN Cameroon experienced \par a challenging period, negatively impacted by the data network shutdown in some parts of the country in the \par first quarter.\par \par The improvement in Group revenue was mostly attributable to strong growth in data and digital revenue, supported \par by stable outgoing voice revenue. Data revenue increased by 31,9%* to R13 952 million, supported by the improved \par quality and capacity of our data networks in key markets. Digital revenue increased by 24,7%*, driven mainly by \par mobile financial services (MFS). MFS continued to gain traction, with the Group adding 2,7 million active MTN \par Mobile Money (MoMo) customers in the first half of the year. Outgoing voice revenue remained flat for the period \par at R32 768 million. \par \par The Group's reported earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation, impairment of goodwill, net\par monetary gains and share of results of joint ventures and associates after tax (EBITDA) margin was 37,9%**. This \par was mainly as a result of the once-off profit (R6 017 million) realised on the exercise of the exchange right \par where the Group's interest in the Nigeria Tower InterCo B.V. (INT) was exchanged for a higher shareholding in \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 IHS Holding Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 IHS\f28 \uc2 \f2 ). This was partly offset by fixed asset impairments for MTN Sudan (R1 690 million) and \par MTN Syria (R1 125 million) relating to the carrying value previously written up under hyperinflation. \par \par On an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basis (excluding pro forma adjustments), the Group's EBITDA margin declined by 4,2 percentage points\par (pp) to 32,9%. MTN Nigeria's EBITDA margin (excluding the Nigerian regulatory fine) declined by 11,5pp* to 38,3%*. \par This was largely as a result of higher foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -denominated expenses in Nigeria following the depreciation \par of the naira against the US dollar. This was partly offset by a 3,4pp growth to 33,5% in the EBITDA margin in \par South Africa. The MTN South Africa margin improvement was largely supported by lower handset subsidies and volumes, \par as well as a strong rand benefiting the cost of handsets in the period. MTN Uganda, MTN Ghana and MTN Ivory Coast \par reported an increase in EBITDA margins while MTN Cameroon's margin declined largely as a result of lower \par revenue growth.\par \par Reported headline earnings per share (HEPS) were 217 cents** compared to a 271 cents** headline loss per share in \par the comparable period, impacted by the Nigerian regulatory fine of 474 cents** (454 cents of the Nigerian fine fully\par expensed and 20 cents of the interest unwind). In the current period, the Nigerian regulatory fine interest unwind \par reduced HEPS by 24 cents. \par \par Subscriber numbers in the period decreased by 3,6% to 231,8 million impacted by a decline in subscriber numbers in \par MTN Nigeria and MTN Ghana. This was largely a result of the Group's initiative to modernise subscriber definitions \par to reflect the business's changing mix of revenue streams. The implementation of the modernised definitions continues \par and is expected to be completed by the end of the year.\par \par The Group continued to invest in the roll out of network and information technology across its markets. Capital\par expenditure (capex) was lower than expected, impacted by limited foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  availability in Nigeria, some \par execution challenges as well as the seasonality of the capex cycle. In the period, a total of 4 404 3G and \par 3 478 4G co-located sites were rolled out. \par \par PROSPECTS AND GUIDANCE \par Africa and the Middle East remain among the world's key growth regions over the medium to long term. As the prospects\par for telecommunications are closely aligned to this growth trajectory, we see a number of opportunities and believe that\par MTN is well placed to benefit given our unique position in the markets in which we operate. \par \par With a strengthened leadership team, the Group continues to work towards achieving our vision "to lead the delivery of\par a bold, new Digital World to our customers". We will continue to leverage our scale and enhance our competitive\par position, benefiting from favourable demographic growth (that will mitigate expected future declines in voice revenue), \par low data penetration in our markets and the unique opportunity we have to provide our customers with a wide range of \par digital services across our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \par To ensure that management remains focused on delivering on the BRIGHT strategy to the benefit of our stakeholders, \par we will establish clearly defined initiatives and KPIs for each of the six areas of "BRIGHT" in the second half \par of the year. We expect these initiatives to support improved top-line growth, EBITDA margins and cash flow over \par the medium term. \par \par Our recent extensive capex investments across our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will enable the business to provide a superior customer\par experience and competitive data networks. This will support the increasing demand for data and digital services. \par We have reduced our capex guidance for 2017 to R30,0 billion from previous guidance of R34,8 billion. We expect \par to accelerate expenditure in the second half and meet the revised capex guidance for the full year.\par \par MTN Nigeria continues to make progress with preparations to list MTN Nigeria shares on the Nigerian Stock Exchange. \par We anticipate completing this process in 2018, should market conditions permit. MTN Ghana is working with relevant\par regulators on a transaction to increase Ghanaian ownership of MTN Ghana shares. We expect to complete this in the \par course of 2017.\par \par We remain on track to meet our FY2017 guidance despite the muted economic growth forecast across our markets. \par \par In South Africa, we expect mid-single-digit service revenue growth and EBITDA margin expansion of 50 and 100 basis\par points year-on-year (YoY). This will be supported by a strong focus on customer service and significantly improved \par network quality, capacity and speed. Targeted cost-optimisation initiatives will support EBITDA growth. We do not \par expect the recent credit ratings downgrade of MTN following the sovereign downgrade of South Africa to have a \par material impact on existing facilities or the cost of new funding in 2017.\par \par In Nigeria, we anticipate upper single-digit revenue growth YoY. This will be supported by an improved competitive\par position, improved network quality and capacity, smartphone penetration and an increased focus on growing data and \par digital revenue. We expect continued improvement in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  liquidity following \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par since February 2017. Encouragingly, post period end MTN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Nigeria\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  declared an interim dividend which after adjusting \par for withholding taxes and minorities will result in USD95 million being received by \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Group\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par \par In Iran, we expect to benefit from growth in the Iranian economy and from the country's youthful population,\par particularly in the digital services space. The Group will continue to focus on the upstreaming of dividends across \par all \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , including MTN Irancell.\par \par Any forward-looking financial information disclosed in this results announcement has not been reviewed or audited \par or otherwise reported on by our external joint auditors.\par \par DIVIDENDS\par The Board has declared an interim dividend of 250 cents per share. This is in line with the FY2017 guidance \par of 700 cents per share communicated in March 2017.\par \par 2017 Capital expenditure guidance          \par                   Old      Capitalised      Capitalised    \par                                    guidance             June             June    \par ZAR (million)       Estimated          2017             2017             2016    \par SEA                    13 152        13 368            4 129            5 626    \par South Africa           11 501        11 526            3 475            4 773    \par Uganda              886           992              356              364    \par Other                     765           850              298              489    \par WECA                   13 840        16 314            5 368            6 975    \par Nigeria                 6 966         9 543            2 749            2 534    \par Ghana                   2 435         2 164              912            1 646    \par Cameroon                1 077           834              694            1 121    \par Ivory Coast             1 575         1 690              499              842    \par Other                   1 787         2 083              514              832    \par MENA                    1 888 