,9%      23,9%                                         37,1%     (4,2)pp    \par Effective tax rate              31,9%                                        43,1%    (309,6%)                                        49,2%     (6,1)pp    \par \par (1) Represents the exclusion of the impact of hyperinflation and related goodwill and asset impairments for certain\par     of the Group's subsidiaries (MTN Syria, MTN South Sudan and MTN Sudan) being accounted for on a hyperinflationary \par     basis in accordance with International Financial Reporting Standards (IFRS) on the respective financial statement \par     line items affected. \par \par     The economies of Iran and Sudan were assessed to no longer be hyperinflationary effective 1 July 2015 and \par     1 July 2016 respectively  and hyperinflation accounting was discontinued from this date onwards.\par \par     The economy of South Sudan was assessed to be hyperinflationary effective 1 January 2016 and hyperinflation\par     accounting was applied from this date onwards.\par \par     Included in EBITDA is the fixed asset impairments for MTN Sudan (R1 690 million) and MTN Syria (R1 125 million)\par     relating to the carrying value previously written up for the impact of hyperinflation. \par \par #   An amount of R192 million of the goodwill impairment on MTN Sudan relates to the carrying value previously written\par     up for the impact of hyperinflation. \par \par (2) Represents the exclusion of the financial impact relating to the sale of tower assets during the financial period\par     on the respective financial line items impacted, which include: \par     - Tower profits, including R6 017 million relating to the profit realised on the exercise of the exchange right\par       where the interest in the Nigeria tower company was exchanged for an increased shareholding in IHS Holdings.\par     - Release of Ghana deferred gain of R13 million (1H16: R18 million). \par \par (3) Represents the impact of the Nigerian regulatory fine subsequent to conclusion of the settlement agreement during\par     2016 on the respective financial line items impacted, which include: \par     - 2016: The remeasurement impact when the settlement agreement was entered into on 10 June 2016, constituting the\par       difference between the balance of the provision recorded on this date (after taking into account finance cost \par       accrued from the beginning of the financial year up to 9 June 2016) and the present value of the financial \par       liability arising on this date in accordance with IFRS (included in the EBITDA line);\par     - 2016 and 2017: The finance cost impact recognised as a result of the unwind of the discounting of the provision\par       (for the period from 1 January to 9 June 2016) and the financial liability (for the period from 10 June 2016 \par       to 30 June 2016 and from 1 January 2017 to reporting date).\par \par EXCHANGE RATES\par The stronger rand and the significant year-on-year (YoY) depreciation of the naira against the US dollar had a\par negative translation impact on rand reported results for the period. The average naira depreciated by 35,3% against \par the US dollar in comparison to the prior period. The average rand strengthened by 14,7% against the US dollar YoY \par and closed 5,2% higher than the previous period.\par \par REVENUE\par Group total revenue increased by 6,7%* to R64 315 million. This was supported by encouraging revenue growth in \par Nigeria (up 10,8%*), Uganda (up 9,4%*), Ghana (up 22,6%*) and Ivory Coast (up 13,0%*). This was mainly a result \par of strong data and digital revenue growth in these markets. MTN Cameroon reported a 4,2%* decline in revenue, \par while total revenue growth in MTN South Africa increased by 1,6%. \par \par Data revenue increased by 31,9%*, benefiting from significantly improved data network quality and capacity across \par our key markets and a 9,1% increase in data users to 122,7 million. Data revenue increased in South Africa \par (up 14,4%), Nigeria (up 70,4%*), Uganda (up 29,2%*), Ghana (up 65,9%*), Cameroon (up 14,1%*) and Ivory Coast \par (up 74,6%*). \par \par Digital revenue increased by 24,7%*, underpinned by solid growth in MFS. This was partly offset by slower growth in\par value-added services (VAS) revenue, impacted by the ongoing review of VAS across our markets, in particular Nigeria.\par \par Outgoing voice revenue remained flat. This is a positive reinforcement of our work to stem the decline in the\par contribution of voice to the business, particularly in Nigeria. \par \par COSTS\par Total costs increased by 12,5%*, negatively impacted by foreign-denominated expenses in Nigeria and costs \par associated with the rollout of network sites in the period. Lower handset cost subsidies and volumes as well \par as a strong rand benefited the total cost of handsets in South Africa. \par \par EBITDA\par Group EBITDA declined by 3,1%*, mainly impacted by a 14,8%* decrease in MTN Nigeria's EBITDA as a result of higher\par foreign-denominated expenses following the depreciation of the naira against the US dollar. This was, however, \par offset by a 13,1% increase in MTN South Africa's EBITDA. MTN Uganda (up 20,5%*), MTN Ghana (up 15,6%*) and \par MTN Ivory Coast (up 10,2%*) contributed positively to Group EBITDA while MTN Cameroon recorded a 22,1%* \par decline in EBITDA. Head office costs were lower than the prior comparable period mainly as a result of \par a number of once-off costs incurred in the prior period.\par \par Consequently, the Group EBITDA margin decreased by 4,2 percentage points to 32,9%.\par \par DEPRECIATION, AMORTISATION AND IMPAIRMENT OF GOODWILL\par The Group depreciation charge increased by 9,0%* to R9 123 million as a result of higher capex in 2016. \par Amortisation costs increased by 25,7%* to R2 090 million, mainly because of higher spend on software in the \par previous period. Non-hyperinflation-related goodwill impairments consisted of impairments in MTN Afghanistan \par (R841 million), MTN Sudan (R791 million) and MTN Yemen (R807 million).\par \par NET FINANCE COSTS\par Net finance costs decreased by 46,3% to R2 935 million. This was mainly due to a 58,0% reduction in net \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) losses to R1 515 million for the period. The decline in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  losses was largely as a result \par of lower foreign-denominated receivables in Mauritius following the repatriation of funds from MTN Irancell \par in 2016. A 23,5% decrease in the Group's net interest charge was driven by the stronger rand against the \par US dollar, resulting in lower finance costs paid on US dollar bonds as well as the translation of the Nigeria \par finance charge following a weaker naira against the rand. South Africa and Dubai reported \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gains in\par the period.\par \par Net \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  losses mainly included:\par - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  losses in head office of R578 million due to the Iran dividend repatriation and the short-term loan \par   to MTN Irancell;\par - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  losses in Nigeria of R893 million incurred on US dollar-denominated third-party payables; and\par - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  losses of R191 million in Sudan on foreign-denominated third-party funding and payables.\par \par SHARE OF RESULTS OF JOINT VENTURES AND ASSOCIATES AFTER TAX\par Joint ventures and associates reported a profit of R1 242 million compared to a loss of R653 million in the\par comparative period. This was mainly as a result of the exercise of the exchange right where MTN exchanged \par its 51% shares of Nigeria Tower InterCo B.V. for an increased equity stake in \f28 \uc2 \f2 IHS\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Following this \par transaction (effective 1 February 2017), we no longer own and equity account for a share of the results \par of INT. \par \par TAXATION\par The reported effective tax rate was 31,9%**, impacted mainly by lower normalised profit before tax (PBT) \par and the effects of tower profits, the turnover-based tax system in Sudan, the Sudanese impairments, as well \par as withholding taxes on upstreamed dividends and other fees.\par \par The Group's reported taxation charge decreased by 51,1%** to R2 312 million for the period. This was impacted \par by higher taxable income in the prior period.\par \par EARNINGS\par Reported headline earnings per share (HEPS) were 217 cents** compared to a 271 cents** headline loss per share \par in the comparable period. \par \par CASH FLOW\par Cash inflows from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  decreased to R17 763 million**. This was mainly as a result of lower attributable \par profits in Nigeria. The repatriation of cash from MTN Irancell of R6 509 million supported cash flows for the \par period. Key cash outflows for the period included dividends paid of R8 475 million** and cash capex of \par R12 419 million**.\par \par CAPITAL EXPENDITURE\par Capex decreased by 25,2% (3,6%*) to R10 305 million. Capex in the first half of the year was slower than expected,\par impacted by limited foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  availability in Nigeria, some execution challenges as well as the seasonality \par of the capex cycle.\par \par FINANCIAL POSITION\par Net debt increased to R52 337 million** from R51 902 million** reported at year-end. The increase was mainly impacted\par by lower cash generated from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  offset by cash repatriated from Iran amounting to R6 509 million** and an\par increase in head office net debt.\par \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 OPERATIONAL\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  REVIEW OF KEY MARKETS\par \par South and East Africa (SEA)\par - Subscribers increased by 0,3% to 54,9 million\par - Revenue increased by 2,9%*\par - Data revenue increased by 14,5%*\par - Digital revenue increased by 29,6%*\par \par South Africa\par - Subscribers increased by 1,5% to 31,2 million\par - Revenue increased by 1,6%\par - Service revenue increased by 5,2%# \par - Data revenue increased by 18,5%# \par - Digital revenue increased by 37,6%\par - EBITDA margin increased by 3,4pp to 33,5%\par - Capex decreased by 27,2% to R3 475 million\par # On an organic basis.\par \par MTN South Africa delivered an encouraging performance, supported by a strong prepaid performance, network \par expansion and a strengthened leadership team. The subscriber base increased by 1,5% to 31,2 million. The \par prepaid segment's subscriber base increased by 1,7% to 26,0 million, while the postpaid segment showed \par early signs of a recovery and increased its subscriber base by 0,2% to 5,2 million. Total revenue increased \par by 1,6% to R20 156 million. Service revenue increased by 5,2%# to R16 753 million, supported by strong growth \par in data revenue and digital revenue, up 18,5%# and 37,6% respectively. Prepaid service revenue increased \par by 9,2% while we saw a decline in postpaid service revenue, down by 3,9%. \par \par MTN Uganda increased its subscriber base by 5,8% to 11,2 million, driven by attractive personalised bundled \par products, superior network quality and effective distribution. MTN Uganda successfully registered 89% of \par the subscriber base under the new SIM registration requirements ahead of the end-August 2017 deadline. \par Total revenue increased by 9,4%*, supported by strong growth in data and digital. Data revenue increased \par by 29,2%*. This was mainly underpinned by an increase in data traffic and good growth in data bundle adoption. \par Digital revenue increased by 19,7%*, supported mainly by MFS. The number of active MoMo customers increased \par by 11,3% to 4,6 million.\par \par West and Central Africa (WECA) \par - Subscribers decreased by 8,5% to 102,3 million\par - Revenue increased by 9,1%*\par - Data revenue increased by 56,7%*\par - Digital revenue increased by 23,1%*\par \par Nigeria\par - Subscribers decreased by 14,3% to 53,1 million\par - Revenue increased by 10,8%*\par - Data revenue increased by 70,4%*\par - Digital revenue increased by 14,1%*\par - EBITDA margin declined by 11,5pp to 38,3% (excluding the impact of the fine)\par - Capex increased by 92,4%* to R2 749 million \par \par MTN Nigeria continued to focus on healthy growth despite a challenging macro-economic environment. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par continued to focus on reviewing and optimising both subscriber definitions and VAS subscriptions, placing some \par short-term pressure on subscriber net additions and VAS revenue. The subscriber base declined by 14,3% to \par 53,1 million, following both this review as well as lower gross connections as a result of new regulations \par that require all subscriber connections take place in permanent structures. Total revenue increased by 10,8%*, \par largely attributable to strong data revenue growth. Data revenue increased by 70,4%*, benefiting from \par customised data offerings and improved network quality driving data usage. Digital revenue grew by 14,1%* \par with active MoMo customers growing by 22,9% to 1,9 million.\par \par MTN Ghana continued to benefit from an improving economy. MTN Ghana's subscriber base declined by 10,3% to 17,3\par million, impacted by the internal review of subscriber definitions. Strong growth in revenue, up by 22,6%*, was \par supported by data and digital revenue growth. Data revenue increased by 65,9%*, led by a data-bundled smartphone \par drive and youth value propositions. Digital revenue grew by 43,6%* with the number of active MoMo subscribers \par growing by 9,5% to 6,2 million.\par \par MTN Cameroon continued to experience a challenging operating environment, following the data shutdown and a \par slowdown in economic activity which had a material impact on subscriber and revenue growth in the period. \par The subscriber base declined by 3,3% to 9,5 million and total revenue decreased by 4,2%*. Despite this, \par active MoMo customers more than doubled to 730 776.\par \par MTN Ivory Coast showed a positive turnaround in the period. Benefiting from significant network investments, \par it increased its subscriber base by 16,2% to 11,0 million. Total revenue increased by 13,0%*, underpinned \par by good growth in outgoing voice and data revenue, up by 9,8%* and 74,6%* respectively. Digital revenue \par increased by 41,9%* with active MoMo customers increasing by 8,2% to 1,3 million. \par \par Middle East and North Africa (MENA)\par - Subscribers increased by 1,1% to 74,5 million\par - Revenue increased by 6,5%* (excluding Iran)\par - Data revenue increased by 33,4%*(excluding Iran)\par - Digital revenue increased by 22,2%* (excluding Iran)\par \par IRAN (joint venture, equity accounted, 49%)\par - Subscribers increased by 3,0% to 49,0 million\par - Revenue increased by 17,2%*\par - Data revenue increased by 67,7%*\par - Digital revenue increased by 3,7%*\par - EBITDA margin declined by 1,4pp to 36,3% \par - Capex increased by more than 100% to R3 850 million \par \par MTN Irancell delivered a solid performance despite pressure on data pricing. The number of subscribers \par increased by 3,0% to 49,0 million, supported by attractive data bundles and a superior quality 3G and \par 4G network. Total revenue increased by 17,2%*, driven by increased data revenue. Data revenue increased \par by 67,7%*, supported by growth in data bundles, modernisation of 2G and 3G sites and expansion of the \par 4G network. Digital revenue increased by 3,7%*, aided by an increase in local content offers.\par \par LEGAL AND REGULATORY \par The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Turkcell\f28 \uc2 \f2  lawsuit currently before the South Gauteng High Court in South Africa is not a new action \par and was initiated by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Turkcell Iletisim Hizmetleri A.S\f28 \uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Turkcell\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) and East Asian Consortium (\f28 \uc2 \f2 EAC\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) in 2013. \par It relates to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Turkcell\f28 \uc2 \f2 's alleged grievances arising from its unsuccessful bid to obtain a mobile licence \par in Iran, and the awarding of that licence to MTN Irancell in 2005. Recent developments in the matter were \par procedural in nature and had nothing to do with the merits of the case. MTN continues to believe that \par there is no legal merit to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Turkcell\f28 \uc2 \f2 's claim and will accordingly oppose it.\par \par BOARD CHANGES\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alan van Biljon\f28 \uc2 \f2 , who served as lead independent director from 2011, stepped down from this role on \par 31 May 2017. Alan will remain an independent non-executive director on the Board until his retirement at \par the end of the 2017 financial year. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alan Harper\f28 \uc2 \f2  was appointed as the new lead independent director of the \par Board with effect from 1 June 2017. \par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rob Shuter\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ralph Mupita\f28 \uc2 \f2  were appointed as executive directors of the Board with effect from \par 13 March 2017 and 3 April 2017 respectively.\par \par DECLARATION OF INTERIM ORDINARY DIVIDEND\par Notice is hereby given that a gross interim dividend of 250 cents per share for the period to \par 30 June 2017 has been declared. The number of ordinary shares in issue at the date of this declaration \par is 1 884 269 758 (including 9 983 286 treasury shares held by MTN Holdings and 76 835 378 shares held by \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Zakhele\f28 \uc2 \f2  Futhi).\par \par The dividend will be subject to a maximum local dividend tax rate of 20% which will result in a net \par dividend of 200 cents per share to those shareholders who bear the maximum rate of dividend \par withholding tax of 50 cents per share. The net dividend per share for the respective categories \par of shareholders for the different dividend tax rates is as follows:\par 0%      250 cents per share\par 5%      237.50 cents per share\par 7,5%    231.25 cents per share\par 10%     225 cents per share\par 12,5%   218.75 cents per share\par 15%     212.50 cents per share\par \par These different dividend tax rates are a result of the application of tax rates in various double-taxation \par agreements as well as exemptions from dividend tax.\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Group Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2 's tax reference number is 9692/942/71/8. In compliance with the requirements of Strate, \par the electronic settlement and custody system used by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 JSE Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the salient dates relating to the \par payment of the dividend are as follows:\par Declaration date                                Thursday, 3 August 2017\par Last day to trade cum dividend on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 JSE\f28 \uc2 \f2        Tuesday, 22 August 2017\par First trading day ex dividend on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 JSE\f28 \uc2 \f2       Wednesday, 23 August 2017\par Record date                                      Friday, 25 August 2017\par Payment date                                     Monday, 28 August 2017\par \par No share certificates may be dematerialised or rematerialised between Wednesday, 23 August 2017 and \par Friday, 25 August 2017, both days inclusive. \par \par On Monday, 28 August 2017, the dividend will be transferred electronically to the bank accounts of \par certificated shareholders who make use of this facility.\par \par In respect of those who do not use this facility, cheques dated Monday, 28 August 2017 will be posted \par on or about this date. Shareholders who hold dematerialised shares will have their accounts held by \par the Central Securities Depository Participant or broker credited on Monday, 28 August 2017.\par \par For and on behalf of the Board\par \par RA Shuter                      PF Nhleko\par Group President and CEO        Chairman \par \par 2 August 2017\par Fairland\par \par REVIEWED CONDENSED CONSOLIDATED INTERIM FINANCIAL STATEMENTS IN ACCORDANCE WITH INTERNATIONAL FINANCIAL \par REPORTING STANDARD (IAS) 34 INTERIM FINANCIAL REPORTING\par The Group's reviewed condensed consolidated interim financial statements for the six months ended 30 June 2017 have\par been independently reviewed by the Group's external auditors. The preparation of the Group's reviewed condensed\par consolidated interim financial statements was supervised by the Group chief financial officer, RT Mupita, BSc Eng \par (Hons), MBA, GMP. \par \par The interim results were made available on 3 August 2017.\par \par Condensed consolidated income statement\par for the\par                                                                                                      Financial    \par                                            Six months       Six months             year    \par                                                                        ended            ended            ended    \par                                                                      30 June          30 June      31 December    \par                                                                         2017           2016             2016    \par                                                                     Reviewed         Reviewed          Audited    \par                                                        Note               Rm               Rm               Rm    \par Revenue                                                               64 386           79 115          147 920    \par Other income                                        7            6 090              367              335    \par Direct network and techn