  \par Net cash and cash equivalents at                                                                \par end of the year                                                     23 162           26 937           27 375    \par                                          \par \par Notes to the condensed consolidated interim financial statements\par for the six months ended 30 June 2017\par 1.  INDEPENDENT review                 \par     The directors of the Company take full responsibility for the preparation of the condensed consolidated interim \par     financial statements. The condensed consolidated interim financial statements have been reviewed by our joint \par     independent auditors, \f28 \uc2 \f2 PricewaterhouseCoopers Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . and SizweNtsalubaGobodo Inc., who have expressed an unmodified \par     conclusion. The joint external auditors have performed their review in accordance with International Standard on \par     Review Engagements (ISRE) 2410. A copy of the independent auditors' review report on the condensed consolidated \par     interim financial statements is available for inspection at the Company's registered office, together with the \par     interim financial statements identified in the independent auditors' review report.                 \par \par     The independent auditors' review report does not necessarily report on all of the information contained in this \par     announcement. Shareholders are therefore advised that in order to obtain a full understanding of the nature of \par     the independent auditors' engagement they should obtain a copy of the independent auditors' review report \par     together with the accompanying interim financial statements from the Company's registered office.     \par \par 2.  GENERAL INFORMATION       \par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Group Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  (the Company) carries on the business of investing in the telecommunications industry through \par     its subsidiary companies, joint ventures, associates and related investments. \par \par 3.  BASIS OF PREPARATION \par     These condensed consolidated interim financial statements for the six months ended 30 June 2017 have been prepared \par     in accordance with International Financial Reporting Standard (IFRS), IAS 34 Interim Financial Reporting, the SAICA \par     Financial Reporting Guides as issued by the Accounting Practices Committee, Financial Pronouncements as issued by \par     the Financial Reporting Standards Council (FRSC) and the requirements of the South African Companies Act, No 71 \par     of 2008. The condensed consolidated interim financial statements should be read in conjunction with the annual \par     financial statements for the year ended 31 December 2016, which have been prepared in accordance with International \par     Financial Reporting Standards (IFRS).    \par \par 4.  PRINCIPAL ACCOUNTING POLICIES\par     The Group has adopted all the new, revised or amended accounting pronouncements as issued by the International Accounting \par     Standards Boards (IASB) which were effective for the Group from 1 January 2017, none of which had a material impact \par     on the Group. \par \par     The accounting policies applied in the preparation of the condensed consolidated interim financial statements are in \par     terms of IFRS and are consistent with those accounting policies applied in the preparation of the previous consolidated \par     annual financial statements.\par \par 5.  HYPERINFLATION\par     The financial statements of the Group entities whose functional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of hyperinflationary \par     economies are adjusted in terms of the measuring unit current at the end of the reporting period.\par \par     The economy of Sudan was assessed to no longer be hyperinflationary, effective 1 July 2016, and hyperinflation \par     accounting was discontinued from this date onwards. As at 30 June 2017 the historical increase in the asset value \par     as a result of hyperinflation accounting has been fully impaired, which resulted in a R1 690 million decrease in \par     EBITDA for the period under review. \par \par     The economy of South Sudan was assessed to be hyperinflationary, effective 1 January 2016, and hyperinflation \par     accounting was applied for the year ended 31 December 2016. Upon first application of hyperinflation, prior period \par     losses of R123 million arising from the net monetary position were recognised directly in equity. As at 31 December \par     2016 and 30 June 2017, the property, plant and equipment of South Sudan was fully impaired, resulting in no hyperinflation \par     adjustment on capital expenditure (capex) for the period under review or during the year ended 31 December 2016.\par \par     In 2015, the Iranian economy was assessed to no longer be hyperinflationary and hyperinflation accounting was discontinued \par     effective 1 July 2015. The Group's results from Iran includes expenses resulting from the discontinuation of hyperinflation \par     accounting mainly relating to the subsequent depreciation of assets that were historically written up under hyperinflation \par     accounting. The additional income statement charge reduced equity accounted earnings from Iran by R640 million for the six \par     months ended 30 June 2017 (June 2016: R1 039 million, December 2016: R1 853 million).\par \par     The economy of Syria was assessed to be hyperinflationary, effective 1 January 2014, and hyperinflation accounting has \par     been applied since. As at 30 June 2017, R1 125 million of assets previously written up for hyperinflation have been \par     impaired with the impact being included in EBITDA for the period under review. \par \par     The impact of hyperinflation on the segment analysis is as follows:    \par                                        Six mo