        5            3               78               348    \par     Iran capex exclusion      (3 850)          (2 313)           (5 138)   \par                                                      10 308           13 850            35 268    \par \par 7.  INVESTMENT IN \f28 \uc2 \f2 IHS\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par     In January 2017, the Group exchanged its 51% interest in Nigeria Tower InterCo B.V., the parent company of INT Towers \par     Limited (INT), the Nigerian telecom tower operator, for an additional shareholding in \f28 \uc2 \f2 IHS Holding Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  (IHS Group) \par     ("the transaction"). As a result of the transaction, the Group's economic interest in the IHS Group increased from \par     approximately 15% class B non-voting shares to an economic interest of approximately 29% comprising class A voting \par     shares and class B non-voting shares. The original IHS Group shareholders' agreement remains in place and there are \par     no changes to IHS Group's independence as an operator. Neither the interest prior to, nor the interest obtained \par     subsequent to the transaction will allow the Group to appoint a board member. In addition, IHS Group has the right \par     to decide what strategic, financial and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  information is shared with the Group. As a result of these \par     restrictions, the Group's vote is limited to matters which relate to fundamental changes in the business or which \par     apply in exceptional circumstances and are considered to be protective in nature. The Group's rights do not constitute \par     significant influence to participate in the financial and operating policy decisions of IHS Group. Consequently, \par     the Group continues to account for its investment in IHS Group as an available-for-sale financial instrument.\par \par     The exchange, which closed on 23 February 2017, has been accounted for as a disposal of the Group's equity accounted \par     interest in INT and an acquisition of an additional investment in the IHS Group. The net impact on profit before tax is \par     R6 017 million, which was determined as the difference between the fair value of the new interest obtained and the \par     carrying value of the equity-accounted interest in INT and after recycling the applicable amount included in the foreign \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  translation reserve (FCTR) (note 18) to the income statement. This resulted in a decrease of R4 452 million in \par     investments in associates and an increase of R13 767 million in available-for-sale investments.\par \par     The decision to exchange the shares was made following a thorough review of the commercial benefits of the exchange and \par     an agreement on the number of shares that the Group will qualify for in IHS Group. Consensus on these matters and board \par     approval for the transaction was obtained in January 2017. As a result, the investment in INT was not accounted for as \par     held for sale in accordance with the requirements of IFRS 5 Non-Current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par     at 31 December 2016.\par \par     The transaction had no tax impact.\par \par 8.  NIGERIA REGULATORY FINE\par     On 10 June 2016, \f28 \uc2 \f2 MTN Nigeria Communications Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  (MTN Nigeria) resolved the matter relating to the previously imposed \par     regulatory fine with the Federal Government of Nigeria (FGN) after the completion of an extensive negotiation process. \par \par     In terms of the settlement agreement reached on 10 June 2016, MTN Nigeria agreed to pay a total cash amount of \par     N330 billion over three years (the equivalent of R25,1 billion 1) to the FGN as full and final settlement of the matter.\par \par     The regulatory fine was fully expensed in the prior period with an additional expense recognised in the income statement \par     amounting to R10,5 billion for the periods ended 30 June 2016 and 31 December 2016. A discount unwind of R537 million \par     (June 2016: R452 million; December 2016: R1,0 billion) was recognised in finance costs during the current period relating \par     to the outstanding liability. The balance of the liability at 30 June 2017 amounts to R7,2 billion (December 2016: \par     R8,7 billion) after taking into account the payment of N30 billion (R1,3 billion 2) on 24 March 2017 and the unwinding \par     of the interest.\par     1 Amount translated at 10 June 2016 rate R1 = N13,15.\par     2 Amount translated at March 2017 average rate R1 = N23,68.\par                                                                   \par 9.  IMPAIRMENT OF GOODWILL AND PROPERTY, PLANT AND EQUIPMENT \par     Current period impairments     \par     In a number of the Group's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the MENA region the socio-political instability experienced in these \par     markets resulted in suppressed revenue growth and lower operating margins being experienced resulting in decreased \par     forecast cash flows. This necessitated impairment reviews being performed on the Group's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Guinea-Bissau, \par     Guinea-Conakry, Liberia, Ghana, Afghanistan, Sudan, Yemen and Syria where the carrying amounts of these \par     cash-generating units were compared to their respective recoverable amounts. The recoverable amounts were \par     determined through value-in-use calculations where future cash flows were estimated and discounted at the weighted \par     average cost of capital discount rates. The discount rates and the perpetuity growth rates used in the value-in-use \par     calculations of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  impairment changes were recorded during the period under review are as follows:   \par                                      June 2017                      December 2016                       \par                                Growth        Discount           Growth          Discount     \par                                  rate            rate             rate              rate     \par           %               %                %                 %    \par     MTN Afghanistan               6,0            18,8              7,0              20,2    \par     MTN Sudan                    14,0            33,0             13,7              32,9    \par     MTN Yemen                     5,0            24,8              9,0              23,9    \par     MTN Syria (JSC)              15,0            35,2            15,0              35,5    \par \par     The following impairment losses were recognised in the income statement in the goodwill impairment and other \par     operating expenses lines, respectively:                                                                         \par                                                         Impairment           \par                                                       of property,           \par                                                          plant and           \par                                                          equipment                       \par                                      Goodwill       and intangible       Recoverable   \par                                    impairment               assets            amount  \par              Rm                   Rm                Rm  \par     MTN Afghanistan                       841                    -             1 971    \par     MTN Sudan                             983                1 690             3 416    \par     MTN Yemen                             807                    -             2 758    \par     MTN Syria (JSC)                         -                1 125             2 007    \par     Total                               2 631                2 815            10 152    \par \par     MTN Sudan was operating in a hyperinflationary economy up to 30 June 2016 while MTN Syria (JSC) continues to \par     operate in a hyperinflationary economy. Hyperinflation accounting resulted in the write up of non-monetary \par     assets and a resulting increase in the carrying value of these \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The total impairment of property, \par     plant and equipment and intangible assets amounting to R2 815 million and R192 million of the MTN Sudan goodwill \par     impairment for the current period relate to the carrying value previously written up to account for the impact \par     of hyperinflation, exceeding the calculated value in use.\par \par     The goodwill of MTN Sudan and MTN Syria has been fully impaired as at 30 June 2017 and the remaining goodwill \par     in MTN Afghanistan and MTN Yemen amount to R527 million and R1 984 million at 30 June 2017 respectively.\par \par     Prior period impairments\par     Areeba Guinea S.A.\par     Areeba Guinea S.A. (Conakry) experienced a decline in EBITDA and Guinea-Conakry experienced poor economic \par     performance countrywide. Consequently, a review of the recoverable amount of Conakry was undertaken during \par     2016 subsequent to which an impairment loss amounting to R402 million was recognised. As at 31 December 2016, \par     the goodwill balance relating to Conakry is fully impaired. No further impairments were deemed necessary \par     as at 30 June 2017.\par \par     Afrihost\par     Based on an agreement concluded by the Group to sell its 50,02% investment in Afrihost Proprietary Limited \par     (Afrihost) for R325 million, a goodwill impairment loss of R202 million was recognised at 30 June 2016 on \par     the remeasurement of the assets to fair value less cost to sell in accordance with IFRS 5 Non-current Assets \par     Held for Sale and Discon