 cost except for \par     the borrowings and the loan set out below, as their carrying amounts closely approximate their fair values.\par \par     Listed long-term borrowings\par     The Group has listed long-term fixed interest rate senior unsecured notes in issue which were issued in \par     prior years, with a carrying amount of R23 117 million at 30 June 2017 (June 2016: R11 031 million, \par     December 2016: R24 059 million) and a fair value of R23 036 million (June 2016: R10 731 million \par     December 2016: R23 179 million). The fair values of these instruments are determined by reference to \par     quoted prices in the Irish bond market. The market for these bonds is not liquid and consequently the \par     fair value measurement is categorised within level 2 of the fair value hierarchy.\par \par     Loan to Nigeria Tower InterCo B.V.\par     The Group has a loan to INT with a carrying amount of R2 864 million (June 2016: R2 877 million, \par     December 2016: R2 863 million) and a fair value of R2 982 million (June 2016: R3 373 million, \par     December 2016: R2 969 million). The fair value of this instrument is determined by a discounted cash \par     flow analysis using a market-related interest rate. The fair value measurement is categorised within \par     level 3 of the fair value hierarchy.\par \par     Financial instruments measured at fair value\par     The fair values of financial instruments measured at fair value are determined as follows: \par \par     Treasury bills \par     The fair value of these investments is determined by reference to published price quotations in an \par     active market. The Group has classified treasury bills with a carrying amount of R293 million (June 2016: \par     R121 million, December 2016: R282 million) as available for sale and with a carrying amount of R697 million \par     (June 2016: R nil, December 2016: R669 million) as at fair value through profit or loss. The fair value of \par     these investments is categorised within level 1 of the fair value hierarchy.\par \par     Fair value measurement of investment in \f28 \uc2 \f2 IHS\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par     Included in investments in the condensed consolidated statement of financial position is an equity \par     investment in IHS Group at fair value of R24 859 million at 30 June 2017. As stated in note 7, the Group \par     increased its interest in IHS Group during the period under review following an exchange of its 51% interest \par     in Nigeria Tower InterCo B.V. Prior to the increase, the Group reported fair values of R11 240 million and \par     R11 354 million at 31 December 2016 and 30 June 2016 respectively, for the investment classified as available \par     for sale. The fair value at 30 June 2016 and 31 December 2016 was determined with reference to recent transactions \par     between market participants, consequently the investment was previously categorised within level 2 of the fair \par     value hierarchy. For 30 June 2017, the absence of transactions between market participants resulted in the fair \par     value being determined using models considered to be appropriate by management. The fair value was calculated \par     using an earnings multiple technique and was based on unobservable market inputs including tower industry \par     earnings multiples of between 13x to 17x applied to MTN management estimates of earnings, less estimated \par     net debt.\par \par     Given the confidentiality restrictions in the shareholders' agreement with IHS Group, MTN does not have \par     access to the IHS Group business plans or 2016 actual financial information. Any estimated earnings used to \par     derive the existing fair value are therefore based solely on MTN management, market estimates and assumptions \par     on financial growth, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements, costs and performance. The investment has therefore been transferred \par     from level 2 to level 3 in the fair value hierarchy for the current reporting period. An increase of one in the \par     low and high end of the multiple range would have resulted in an increase in the fair value of R1 014 million \par     and a decrease of one in the low and high end of the multiple range would have resulted in a decrease in the \par     fair value by R1 014 million as at 30 June 2017. An increase of R817 million (June 2016 and December 2016: \par     R2 672 million) has been recognised for the period under review in other comprehensive income resulting from \par     the change in fair value.\par \par     Reconciliation of level 3 financial assets                 \par     The table below sets out the reconciliation of financial assets that are measured at fair value based on \par     inputs that are not based on observable market data (level 3):                 \par     Balance at 1 January 2016                         9 707    \par     Transfers to level 2 (\f28 \uc2 \f2 IHS\f28 \uc2 \f2 )                       (9 250)   \par     Acquisitions                                         61    \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  differences                       (138)   \par     Balance at 1 January 2017                           380    \par     Transfers from level 2 (\f28 \uc2 \f2 IHS\f28 \uc2 \f2 )                     11 240    \par     Acquisitions                                        143    \par     Exchange right exercise                          13 767    \par     Gain on available-for-sale investment               817    \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  differences                       (981)   \par     Balance at 30 June 2017                          25 366    \par \par                                                                                   Financial    \par                                                 Six months      Six months             y