lion) for the current period. \par     Following the exercise of the exchange rights in INT Towers Limited (note 7), a foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  translation \par     loss of R3 298 million was released to the condensed consolidated income statement.\par \par     Net investment hedges\par     During 2016 and for the period ended 30 June 2017, the Group hedged a designated portion of its dollar net \par     assets in MTN (Dubai) Limited (MTN Dubai) for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exposure arising between the USD and ZAR as part of the \par     Group's risk management objectives. The Group designated external borrowings (Eurobonds) denominated in USD \par     held by MTN Mauritius with a value of R23 billion (December 2016: R23,1 billion) and external borrowings \par     denominated in USD held by MTN Nigeria Communications Limited with a value of R3,5 billion (December 2016: \par     R4,5 billion) as hedging instruments. For the period of the hedge relationship, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements \par     on these hedging instruments are recognised in other comprehensive income as part of the foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par     translation reserve (FCTR), offsetting the exchange differences recognised in other comprehensive income, \par     arising on translation of the designated dollar net assets of MTN Dubai to ZAR. The cumulative \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par     movement recognised in other comprehensive income will only be reclassified to profit or loss upon loss \par     of control over MTN Dubai. There was no hedge ineffectiveness recognised in profit or loss during the current \par     or prior period.    \par \par     Nigerian exchange rate\par     On 24 April 2017, a new quoted exchange rate window was established in Nigeria, the Nigerian Autonomous \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Fixing (NAFEX). For the current reporting cycle, due to the short time period that has \par     elapsed between the introduction of this rate and period end, the Group has exercised judgement in determining \par     the most appropriate rate to be used to convert foreign denominated balances at period end in Nigeria, and \par     concluded to continue using the Interbank exchange rate for purposes of its interim results. The Group will \par     monitor the market liquidity in these quoted rates going forward in assessing the most appropriate rate at \par     which foreign denominated transactions and balances are to be translated for reporting purposes. The Interbank \par     exchange rate quoted was N325,00:USD1 and the exchange rate quoted in the NAFEX window was approximately \par     N366,41:USD1 as at 30 June 2017. \par \par 19. RELATED PARTY TRANSACTIONS\par     Transactions between members of the Group\par     Scancom Limited (MTN Ghana) entered into operating lease agreements with Ghana Tower InterCo B.V. The expense \par     recorded amounted to R348 million for the six months ended 30 June 2017 (June 2016: R313 million, December 2016: \par     R532 million). The rental amounts escalate every year by inflation and the initial term is 10 years, followed \par     by four times five-year renewal periods. \par \par     MTN Uganda Limited entered into operating lease agreements with Uganda Tower InterCo B.V. The expense recorded \par     amounted to R232 million for the six months ended 30 June 2017 (December 2016: R432 million; June 2016: \par     R244 million). The rental amounts escalate every year by inflation and the initial term is 10 years, followed \par     by four times five-year renewal periods.\par \par     MTN Nigeria Communications Limited entered into operating lease agreements with INT Towers Limited, a wholly \par     owned subsidiary of Nigeria Tower InterCo B.V, which are considered related parties up to the date of exercise \par     of the exchange right (note 7). The expense recorded from the beginning of the current reporting period to the \par     date of the exercise of the exchange right (note 7) amounted to R464 million (December 2016: R4 254 million; \par     June 2016: R2 502 million). The initial term is 10 years (extended to 15 years in 2016), followed by four times \par     five-year renewal periods.    \par \par 20. EVENTS AFTER REPORTING PERIOD\par     Bonds issued and repaid\par     Subsequent to the end of the reporting period the Group issued two variable rate bonds totalling R2,5 billion \par     under its domestic medium term note (DMTN) programme. Both bonds were issued on 13 July 2017, R1,5 billion \par     bearing interest at three-month JIBAR plus 1,8% maturing on 13 July 2020; and R1 billion bearing interest \par     at three-month JIBAR plus 2% maturing on 13 July 2022.\par \par     On the same date, notes previously issued under the DMTN programme amounting to R1,25 billion matured \par     and were settled.\par \par     Dividends declared \par     Dividends declared at the board meeting held on 2 August 2017 amounted to 250 cents per share.\par \par ADMINISTRATION\par Registration number: 1994/009584/06\par ISIN: ZAE000042164\par Share code: MTN\par \par Board of directors\par PF Nhleko**\par RA Shuter#*\par RT Mupita*\par PB Hanratty$***\par A Harper#***\par KP Kalyan***\par S Kheradpir\u8224\'20\'20\u8224\'20\'20***\par NP Mageza***\par MLD Marole***\par AT Mikati\u8224\'20\'20**\par SP Miller^***\par KC Ramon***\par NL Sowazi***\par AF van Biljon***\par J van Rooyen*** \par \par \u8224\'20\'20\u8224\'20\'20  American\par \u8224\'20\'20   Lebanese\par #   British\par $   Irish\par ^   Belgian\par *   Executive\par **  Non-executive\par *** Independent non-executive director\par \par Group secretary\par SB Mtshali\par Private Bag X9955, Cresta, 2118\par \par Registered office\par 216 - 14th