\par\par \b \uc2 African Markets - Factors to watch on July 5\b0\par\par\uc2 603 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juli 2017\par 07:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     NAIROBI, July 5 (Reuters) - The following company announcements, scheduled economic\par indicators, debt and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market moves and political events may affect African markets on\par Wednesday.\par     - - - - -\par  EVENTS:\par  *RWANDA - Statistics office to release first quarter 2017\par  gross domestic product growth data.\par  \par  GLOBAL MARKETS\par  Asian share markets were subdued for a second session on\par  Wednesday as simmering tensions on the Korean peninsula\par  supported safe-harbours including the yen, bonds and\par  gold.                       \par  \par  WORLD OIL PRICES\par  Oil markets were firm on Wednesday on worries over\par  geopolitical tensions in the Korean peninsula and the Middle\par  East, although prices were capped as supply remains ample\par  despite an \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2 -led drive to rein in production.            \par       \par  \par  EMERGING MARKETS\par  For the top emerging markets news, double click on\par             \par  \par  AFRICA STOCKS\par  For the latest news on African stocks, click on     \par  \par  SOUTH AFRICA MARKETS\par  South Africa's rand firmed slightly on Tuesday as the July 4\par  holiday in the United States restricted market activity,\par  while stocks weakened as investors took profits in bourse\par  heavyweight \f28 \uc2 \f2 Naspers\f28 \uc2 \f2 .            \par  \par  NIGERIA MARKETS\par  Nigerian banking shares dropped 2.3 percent on Tuesday after\par  regulators said they had \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to save the country's\par  fourth largest telecoms firm from collapse as talks with\par  local lenders to renegotiate a $1.2 billion loan\par  failed.            \par  \par  NIGERIA TELECOMS\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Nigeria\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and its telecoms industry regulator\par  have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to save the country's fourth largest\par  telecoms firm from collapse after talks with local banks to\par  renegotiate a $1.2 billion loan failed, a regulatory source\par  said on Tuesday.            \par  \par  NIGERIA BREWER SHARE SALE \par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Guinness Nigeria\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Tuesday launched a share sale to raise\par  39.7 billion naira ($126 million) from existing shareholders\par  to help lower its financing costs after reporting its first\par  annual loss in 30 years last year.                    \par  \par  KENYA MARKETS\par  The Kenyan shilling        was broadly stable against the\par  dollar on Tuesday with hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from diaspora\par  remittances meeting end-of-month demand from manufacturers\par  and oil importers, traders said.            \par  \par  TANZANIA MINERAL LAWS\par  Tanzania put more pressure on foreign mining companies on\par  Tuesday by amending mining and tax laws to make it mandatory\par  for the state to own at least 16 percent of mining projects,\par  while also raising export royalties.            \par      Also, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Acacia Mining\f28 \uc2 \f2           said on Tuesday it was\par  seeking an adjudicator to resolve its dispute with the\par  Tanzanian government over mining contracts as President John\par  Magufuli ordered the suspension of any new\par  licences.            \par  \par  CONGO ECONOMY\par  The economy of Congo Republic is likely to shrink for a\par  second consecutive year, the oil-producing nation's\par  government said on Tuesday, forecasting a 1.9 percent\par  contraction in 2017, a downgrade from its earlier projection\par  of 1 percent growth.            \par  \par  GAMBIA PETROLEUM\par  Gambia has ended talks with \f28 \uc2 \f2 African Petroleum\f28 \uc2 \f2            to\par  extend oil exploration in two of the West African country's\par  most promising offshore blocks and is now in preliminary\par  discussions with new investors, a senior oil official told\par  Reuters.            \par  \par  NAMIBIA \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RESERVES\par  Namibia\u8217\'20\'19s foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves fell slightly to 25.4\par  billion Namibian dollars ($1.93 billion) at the end of May\par  2017 compared with N$25.7 billion in April, data from \f28 \uc2 \f2 the\par  Bank of Namibia\f28 \uc2 \f2  showed on Tuesday.            \par  \par  MOZAMBIQUE DEBT\par  Switzerland's financial watchdog FINMA remains in touch with\par  Credit Suisse CSGN.S over its role in arranging loans for\par  Mozambique state-owned companies, a spokesman for the\par  regulator said on Tuesday, after an independent report\par  concluded it was unclear how the money had been\par  spent.            \par  \par      For the latest precious metals report click on        \par      For the latest base metals report click on         \par      For the latest crude oil report click on\f28 \par \par \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-7-5T06:06:09.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170705ed750076n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
