\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-\uc2 Nigeria's  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  sells $195 mln at  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  market auction\b0\par\par\uc2 334 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 2017\par 22:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds amounts sold and background.)\par \par \uc2 By Oludare Mayowa\par \par \uc2 LAGOS, June 19 (Reuters) - \uc2 Nigeria's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $195 million on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Monday as part of its effort to improve dollar liquidity and reduce pressure on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the naira, a bank spokesman said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Nigeria is battling a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis brought on by low oil prices, which has tipped its economy into recession and created chronic dollar shortages. It wants to attract foreign investors and at the same time maintain a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ward off inflation.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the official market in recent months to try to narrow the spread between rates on the official market and black market. It has sold over $5 billion since February.\par \par \uc2 In an emailed statement, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said $100 million was released to authorized \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers and $50 million to small and medium-size enterprises. A further $45 million was provided for such items as medical fees, tuition fees and business travel.\par \par \uc2 The regulator, in notices to commercial lenders on Monday, said an auction would be held for spot and forward deals which would be settled within the next 60 days.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the bank said it would continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the naira.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2  has at least six different exchange rates including a retail rate set by licensed exchange bureaus, the official and black market rates and a window for investors where the naira can be traded at rates set freely between buyers and sellers.\par \par \uc2 The naira was quoted at 371.41 on the investors' window >NAFEXD=FMDQ> on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded at 368 on the black market >NGNFX=BDCN> and traded at around 305.80 to the dollar on the official window. (Additional reporting by Camillus Eboh in Abuja and Alexis Akwagyiram in Lagos, editing by Larry King) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-6-19T21:34:53.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170619ed6j00kj0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
