\par\par \b \uc2 African Markets - Factors to watch on April 11\b0\par\par\uc2 779 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2017\par 06:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The following company announcements, scheduled economic indicators, debt and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market\par moves and political events may affect African markets on Tuesday.\par     - - - - -\par  \par  GLOBAL MARKETS\par  Asian stocks fell in choppy trade on Tuesday as the\par  political tinderbox in the Middle East and the Korean\par  Peninsula added to uncertainty over the looming French vote,\par  pushing edgy investors into safer assets such as the yen and\par  Treasuries.                       \par  \par  WORLD OIL PRICES\par  U.S. crude oil rose for a sixth consecutive session on\par  Tuesday to hit its highest level in five weeks, underpinned\par  by tensions following a U.S. missile strike on Syria and a\par  shutdown at Libya's largest oilfield.                 \par  \par  EMERGING MARKETS\par  For the top emerging markets news, double click on\par             \par  \par  AFRICA STOCKS\par  For the latest news on African stocks, click on     \par  \par  \par  SOUTH AFRICA MARKETS\par  South Africa's rand traded weaker early on Monday as credit\par  downgrades to "junk" by two ratings firms last week\par  following the sudden firing of the finance minister\par  continued to weigh.            \par  \par  \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 NIGERIA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKETS\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Nigeria\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the spot \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market\par  on Monday selling $1.5 million at 305.65 naira, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thomson\par  Reuters\f28 \uc2 \f2   data, propping up the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  which had\par  weakened to 328.50 during mid-trades.            \par  \par  \par  NIGERIA SECURITY\par  President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  's administration is pushing forward\par  with plans to sell up to a dozen aircraft to Nigeria's air\par  force for the fight against the extremist group \f28 \uc2 \f2 Boko Haram\f28 \uc2 \f2  ,\par  sources said on Monday, in a deal that could be worth up to\par  $600 million.                \par  \par  \par  NIGERIA SECURITY/BOKO HARAM\par  Niger security forces killed 57 members of Islamist militant\par  group \f28 \uc2 \f2 Boko Haram\f28 \uc2 \f2   who attacked a village in the southeastern\par  Diffa region overnight, the defence ministry said in a\par  statement on Monday.               \par  \par  \par  KENYA MARKETS\par  The Kenyan shilling        weakened slightly against the\par  dollar on Monday due to demand from oil importers but\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  inflows from diaspora remittances were seen\par  offering support, traders said.            \par  \par  \par  IVORY COAST COCOA\par  Heavy rains in Ivory Coast's western cocoa growing regions\par  and persistent dry conditions in the east have raised\par  concerns about bean quality for the April-to-September\par  mid-crop, farmers said on Monday.            \par  \par  \par  UGANDA MARKETS\par  The Ugandan shilling        was little changed on Monday but\par  was inclined toward the stronger side on the back of waning\par  appetite for hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  among players in the interbank\par  market.            \par  \par  \par  UGANDA POLITICS\par  The Ugandan government charged a prominent academic with a\par  "cyber harassment" offence on Monday after she repeatedly\par  posted criticism of President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Yoweri Museveni\f28 \uc2 \f2   and his wife\par  on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Facebook\f28 \uc2 \f2  , according to court documents.    \par  \par  \par  MOZAMBIQUE RATES\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mozambique's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   cut its benchmark lending rate by\par  50 basis points to 22.75 percent, it said on\par  Monday.            \par  \par  \par  CONGO POLITICS\par  Opposition calls for mass protests against Democratic\par  Republic of Congo President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Joseph Kabila\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell flat on\par  Monday when only a handful of people showed up, undermining\par  efforts to oust him after his refusal to quit on expiry of\par  his mandate last year.                    \par  \par  \par  SOMALIA SECURITY\par  A suicide bomber wearing army uniform killed at least nine\par  soldiers at a campin Somalia's capital on Monday,\par  authorities said, and a government official was killed by a\par  bomb planted in his car.            \par  \par  \par  MALI COTTON\par  Cotton production in Mali jumped 18 percent in the 2016/2017\par  season to 647,212 tonnes, the country's highest yield in\par  more than a decade, an official at the Malian Company for\par  the Development of Textiles (CMDT) said on\par  Monday.            \par  \par  \par  TANZANIA INFLATION\par  Tanzania's year-on-year inflation rose to 6.4 percent in\par  March, the statistics office said on Friday, from 5.5\par  percent the previous month.            \par  \par  \par  RWANDA INFLATION\par  Rwanda's year-on-year inflation              eased to 7.7\par  percent in March from 8.1 percent a month earlier, the\par  National Bureau of Statistics said on Monday.            \par  \par  \par  BURUNDI POLITICS\par  Burundian opposition leader and former rebel chief Agathon\par  Rwasa has said he fears for his life and has asked the\par  government to speed up talks to end a crisis that began in\par  2015, when President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pierre Nkurunziza\f28 \uc2 \f2   said he wanted a\par  third term.            \par  \par  \par  MAURITIUS TOURISM\par  The number of tourists visiting Mauritius rose 3.6 percent\par  in the first quarter of 2017 from a year ago, mainly due to\par  more visitors from Europe and Asia, the statistics bureau\par  said on Monday.            \par  \par  \par  SOUTH SUDAN UNREST\par  At least 16 people were killed in the South Sudanese town of\par  Wau on Monday, said the United Nations, as witnesses said\par  ethnic militiamen went house to house searching for people\par  from other groups.                                        \par  \par  \par      For the latest precious metals report click on        \par      For the latest base metals report click on         \par      For the latest crude oil report click on\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-4-11T05:32:46.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170411ed4b005k3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
