\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Nigerian regulators push for deal in Etisalat debt talks\b0\par\par\uc2 627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2017\par 21:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds \uc2 IHS\uc2  statement)\par \par \uc2 * Etisalat missed payment on $1.2 bln loan\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , telecom regulator \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Etisalat, lenders to meet regulators on Friday\par \par \uc2 By Camillus Eboh and Chijioke Ohuocha\par \par \uc2 ABUJA/LAGOS, March 9 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's telecoms regulator and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday to try to help \uc2 Etisalat \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2  resolve debt restructuring talks with its lenders, after the company missed a payment on a $1.2 billion loan.\par \par \uc2 A banking source told Reuters on Wednesday that the Nigerian affiliate of Abu Dhabi-listed telecoms company Etisalat had given notice to its Nigerian lenders that it would miss a payment in February. Debt talks were triggered 10 days ago but the two sides have not been able to agree on terms.\par \par \uc2 Etisalat is the biggest foreign victim of dollar shortages in Nigeria. Firms aggressively invested in the West African nation in the era of high oil prices but are struggling to repay loans or keep operating as the oil producer suffers from a slump in oil revenues, hitting its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and dollars reserves.\par \par \uc2 One of the consortium of 13 affected lenders, \uc2 Access Bank\uc2 , said on Thursday it was owed 40 billion naira ($131 million) by \uc2 Etisalat Nigeria\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Nigerian Communications Commission (NCC) \uc2 said in a statement it was worried about the negative impact the matter could have on \uc2 Etisalat Nigeria\uc2  subscribers and the industry, and wanted to prevent a possible takeover of the unit by the banks.\par \par \uc2 NCC Chairman Umar Danbatta met with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor \uc2 Godwin Emefiele\uc2  to try to find a solution and ordered \uc2 Etisalat Nigeria\uc2  and the banks to meet again on Friday. It gave no details.\par \par \uc2 "NCC was worried about the fate of the over 20 million Etisalat subscribers and the wrong signals this may send to potential investors in the telecom industry," it said in the statement.\par \par \uc2 Etisalat and \uc2 Etisalat Nigeria\uc2  were not immediately available for comment.\par \par \uc2 LEASE PAYMENT AT RISK\par \par \uc2 Emirates Telecommunications Group (Etisalat) owns a 40 percent stake in its Nigerian affiliate, which accounted for around 3.7 percent of the group's revenue in 2013.\par \par \uc2 Etisalat Nigeria\uc2  signed a $1.2 billion medium-term facility with 13 Nigerian banks in 2013, which it used to refinance an existing $650 million loan and fund a modernisation of its network.\par \par \uc2 Banks involved in the loan deal include: \uc2 Zenith Bank\uc2  , \uc2 GT Bank\uc2 , \uc2 First Bank\uc2 , UBA, \uc2 Fidelity Bank\uc2 , \uc2 Access Bank\uc2 , Ecobank, FCMB, \uc2 Stanbic IBTC Bank\uc2  and Union Bank.\par \par \uc2 Access Bank's Chief Executive \uc2 Herbert Wigwe\uc2  told an analysts' call that Etisalat had converted a shareholder loan to the Nigerian arm to equity to free up cashflows and that it may need to inject fresh equity.\par \par \uc2 As well as the loan from banks, Etisalat had also entered into a sale and lease-back of its phone towers with tower firm \uc2 IHS Nigeria\uc2  to free up cash.\par \par \uc2 IHS\uc2  said a payment of $8.5 million was more than 120 days overdue from Etisalat as per Dec. 31. "We ... will continue to pursue our contractual rights in collecting the outstanding amounts," \uc2 IHS\uc2  said in a filing to the Irish stock exchange.\par \par \uc2 JP Morgan analyst Zafar Nazim said in a note on Thursday that on Wednesday it had downgraded \uc2 IHS\uc2  bonds due 2021 because of \uc2 Etisalat Nigeria\uc2  as it was uncertain whether the company could keep up with lease commitments.\par \par \uc2 Nazim also said it was unclear whether Etisalat's parent firm would recapitalise the Nigerian \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  given its small market share in the country, but added a quick resolution to the loan issue would boost \uc2 IHS\uc2  bonds. (Additional reporting by Andrew Torchia in Dubai; Writing by Chijioke Ohuocha and Ulf Laessing; Editing by Susan Fenton and Mark Potter) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-3-9T21:31:25.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170309ed3900nru\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
