\par\par \b \uc2 Nigeria sells $1 bln to help clear backlog in biggest dollar sale since June\b0\par\par\uc2 413 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Dezember 2016\par 14:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LAGOS, Dec 29 (Reuters) - \uc2 Nigeria's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold about $1 billion on the forward market last week to clear a backlog of dollar obligations in selected sectors, traders said on Thursday, its largest special auction since a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg was removed in June.\par \par \uc2 Outstanding dollar demand was about $4 billion before June, when the 16-month-old peg was removed. Efforts to cut dollar demand have been largely unsuccessful due to low oil prices.\par \par \uc2 Crude sales account for about 90 percent of Nigeria's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  told banks to prioritise airlines, manufacturing firms, petroleum products importers and agriculture sectors, the sectors worst hit by the dollar shortage, in the auction.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $1 billion at last week's special \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  auction and directed banks to issue fresh letters of credit to reflect the amount sold in favour of the affected sectors," a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold 30-day and 60-day forwards at the auction.\par \par \uc2 On Dec. 19, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  instructed commercial lenders to submit their backlog of dollar demand from fuel importers, airlines, raw materials and machinery for manufacturing firms and agricultural chemicals for the special \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in its first recession for 25 years, caused by the oil price drop which has cut the supply of dollars needed to fund imports. Attacks by militants on pipelines in the Niger Delta since January have cut crude output, further reducing dollar inflows.\par \par \uc2 The dollar shortage in the \uc2 OPEC\uc2  member, whose crude sales make up two thirds of government revenue, has caused many companies to halt \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and lay off workers, compounding the economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 Some foreign airlines have closed down or reduced their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  over an inability to repatriate the proceeds of their earnings due to the dollar shortage.\par \par \uc2 An acute shortage of jet oil in the last few months - caused by the inability of importers to secure the dollars needed to buy the fuel - has led to many operators refuelling in neighbouring countries.\par \par \uc2 Flight cancellations by local airlines have become commonplace as a result of the shortages.\par \par \uc2 The naira was trading at about 305.25 to the dollar on the interbank market on Thursday and was quoted at 490 a dollar on the unofficial market. (Editing by Alexis Akwagyiram and Louise Ireland) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-12-29T14:19:08.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161229ecct00kxc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
