\par\par \b \uc2 Nigeria to provide dollars to airlines, fuel marketers in special  \highlight22\uc2 fx \highlight\uc2  auction -traders\b0\par\par\uc2 402 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Dezember 2016\par 14:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LAGOS, Dec 19 (Reuters) - \uc2 Nigeria's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday asked banks to submit bids for a "special \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  auction" to clear the backlog of matured outstanding dollar obligations for selected sectors of the economy, traders said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  instructed commercial lenders to submit backlog dollar demand from fuel importers, airlines, raw materials and machinery for manufacturing firms and agricultural chemicals by 1500 GMT for a special \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in its first recession in 25 years, caused by low global oil prices which have cut the supply of dollars needed to fund imports. Attacks by militants on pipelines in the oil-rich Niger Delta since January have reduced crude output, reducing dollars earned.\par \par \uc2 The dollar shortage in the \uc2 OPEC\uc2  member, whose crude sales make up two thirds of government revenue, has caused many companies to halt \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and lay off workers, compounding the economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to sell "funded forwards of two to five months tenor" dollars to the targeted sectors at an auction ahead of the closure of the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to close all \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions this Friday ahead of its financial year end and the Christmas period.\par \par \uc2 The naira \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has traded around 305.5 naira to the dollar on the official interbank market since August, while it was quoted at 487 to the dollar on the parallel market on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Two weeks ago, the bank had asked commercial lenders to submit bids for a special \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  auction targeting fuel importers, but the result of the auction has not yet been released, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Africa's largest economy has suffered from an acute shortage of jet oil used by airlines in the last few months, causing many operators in the sector to refuel from neighbouring countries, while flight cancellations by local airlines have become commonplace as a result of the shortages.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the \uc2 Nigerian National Petroleum Corporation\uc2  said it had imported about 38.7 million litres of aviation fuel, which it said "represented about 26-day sufficiency", as part of its "ensure a hitch-free air travel across the country during and after the yuletide period". (Reporting by Oludare Mayowa and Camillus Eboh; Editing by Alexis Akwagyiram and Toby Chopra) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-12-19T14:42:46.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161219eccj00om7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
