\par\par \b \uc2 Nigeria naira ends at 305 after  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  dollar sales\b0\par\par\uc2 198 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 2016\par 15:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LAGOS, Oct 17 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday with dollar sales to support the naira, which ended at 305.50 per dollar, a range it has closed at for nearly two months, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the bank, which has been selling dollars regularly to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sold around $1.5 million towards the close of Monday's trade after the market was open for nearly four hours with no deals.\par \par \uc2 The naira was quoted at 455 to the dollar on the black market on Monday, firmer than 460 at its previous session, as international money transfer operators had started to sell dollars to retail \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  outlets. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had weakened to a record low of 495 per dollar on the black market last month.\par \par \uc2 The bank held a dollar forward auction last Friday to clear a backlog of demand from airlines, manufacturers and other companies, as the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis deepened. Result of the auction was yet to be published, traders say. (Reporting by Oludare Mayowa; Writing by Chijioke Ohuocha; Editing by Toby Chopra) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-10-17T14:47:48.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161017ecah00lzt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
