\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Nigeria lowers 2016 forecast, expects economy to shrink 1.3 pct\b0\par\par\uc2 468 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2016\par 13:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ABUJA, Oct 5 (Reuters) - Nigeria's economy is likely to shrink 1.3 percent in 2016, the National Bureau of Statistics executive said on Wednesday, a sharp downward revision of its estimates he said was prompted by sharp falls in the naira after dollar peg was dropped.\par \par \uc2 The NBS had predicted the Nigerian economy to grow 3.8 percent in 2016, but low oil prices have hammered the \uc2 OPEC\uc2  member's government income and the naira, and recession first appeared in the second quarter with 2.1 percent contraction.\par \par \uc2 A contraction in 2016 would mark Nigeria's first year of recession in 25 years. NBS head Yemi Kale said the economy was likely to shrink in the third quarter.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ) already predicted in July that Nigeria's economy would contract 1.8 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 The NBS had also revised its inflation forecasts, Kale said. Year-end inflation was estimated at between 17.1 percent and 18 percent, he said, up from 9 percent at the start of the year.\par \par \uc2 "All things remaining constant, year-end GDP should be around -1.3 percent from our internal model," he told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Prior to ditching the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg in June, Nigeria had fixed its exchange rate at 197 to the dollar. Kale said the new projection factored in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  float.\par \par \uc2 The naira has hit record lows on the black market in the last month, weakening to as low as 485 per dollar. It has held firm at around 305 on the official market, supported by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic growth started to slow by the second quarter of 2014 after an oil price collapse hurt government revenues and caused severe dollar shortages on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Oil sales, which generate 90 percent of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the economy, contributed around 10 percent of Nigeria's GDP directly and around 52 percent indirectly through its links with other sectors, Kale said.\par \par \uc2 A record 2016 budget of 6.06 trillion naira aimed at tackling the recession was based on generating non-oil receipts largely from widening the tax net and raising debt at home and abroad to augment spending. So far the government has spent 720.5 billion naira on capital spending.\par \par \uc2 But Nigeria has yet to raise funds abroad and revenue problems have been exacerbated by militant attacks on energy facilities, which have cut crude production by a third.\par \par \uc2 The debt office launched a roadshow to Britain and the United States to promote a planned Eurobond issue while the \uc2 African Development Bank\uc2  will this month consider a $1 billion loan to Nigeria to help cover its budget deficit. (Editing by Alexis Akwagyiram and Raissa Kasolowsky)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-10-8T11:47:08.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161005eca500ku1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
