\par\par \b \uc2 Nigeria  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Devaluation: ETF Craters 22% & Fitch Warns Investors -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dimitra DeFotis   \par \uc2 588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2016\par 17:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Nigeria \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation Monday won't be followed by greater foreign direct investment if the process doesn't clear a backlog of unmet dollar demand, \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  warns.\par \par \uc2 The country must shore up its balance sheet in the absence of oil revenue, which is hampered not only by low prices for oil exports, but also by violence hampering Nigerian oil production by targeting companies doing business with the state-owned oil company. Affected integrated oil companies include \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  ( XOM) and \uc2 Royal Dutch Shell\uc2  ( RDSA).\par \par \uc2 Nigeria said this week it will introduce a market-driven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system, and will abandon a policy that restricted access and hampered its financing efforts and the economy. After the announcement, the Global X MSCI Nigeria exchange-traded fund ( NGE) got slammed this week, down 22%, while the VanEck Vectors Africa Index ETF ( AFK) fell more than 2% and the \uc2 iShares MSCI Frontier 100 ETF\uc2  ( FM) fell 0.7%. Fitch writes:\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Nigeria\uc2 's (CBN) previous policy of restricting access to the official \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and supporting the naira, rather than risk the inflationary impact of devaluation, has been negative for Nigeria's sovereign credit profile. Defending the naira has lowered reserves and increased external vulnerabilities, while a shortage of hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has weighed on the non-oil economy. The change of policy is consistent with our view that the CBN would struggle to defend the naira indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 But a backlog of unmet dollar demand (estimates range from $4 billion to $9 billion) has built up and any inability to clear a significant portion of that backlog early in the transition would hinder the effectiveness of the new framework. The CBN will introduce a new non-deliverable forward to try to limit exchange-rate volatility under the new system, by moving some of the dollar demand to the futures market and away from the spot market. Even so, the CBN will probably have to deploy a large portion of its international reserves during the first week(s) of implementation. It also reserves the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  by buying and selling \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth market movements, although it has made no specific announcements about trading bands or break points that might lead to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unorthodox \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy has made raising external financing more difficult. Allowing the market to determine the value of the naira could ease this, although we think much potential FDI may remain on the sidelines until a clearer picture emerges of how the new system is functioning. Foreign investment in the domestic bond market is very low and not likely to increase in the near term. High demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a devaluation may also limit the benefit to the current account from recovering oil prices ...\par \par \uc2 Our base case for Nigerian banks is that regulatory total capital ratios will not decline significantly under the new regime. Any impact will be offset by still strong profitability and high levels of internal capital generation. The new \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime crucially also provides access to US dollars for the banks to meet their internal and external obligations."\par \par \uc2 See our posts Nigeria \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Devaluation: Inflation Pain, Green Shoots?, Nigeria: Conflict Strangles Oil Output & Economy In Buhari's First Year and \uc2 MSCI\uc2  Jazzed On Argentina & Pakistan, Not Nigeria Or China. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at Barron's Emerging Markets blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.barrons.com/emergingmarketsdaily/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.barrons.com/emergingmarketsdaily/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 17, 2016 11:53 ET (15:53 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160617ec6h00225\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
