\par\par \b \uc2 African Markets - Factors to watch on Feb 10\b0\par\par\uc2 479 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2016\par 06:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NAIROBI, Feb 10 (Reuters) - The following company\par announcements, scheduled economic indicators, debt and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par market moves and political events may affect African markets on\par Wednesday.\par     - - - - -\par  EVENTS:\par  RWANDA - Inflation data due to be released\par  \par  GLOBAL MARKETS\par  Asian stocks fell on Wednesday on growing concerns\par  about the health of the world's banks, particularly in\par  Europe, pushing investors into safer assets such as the\par  yen, which stood near a 15-month high versus the\par  dollar.            \par  \par  WORLD OIL PRICES\par  Crude oil prices pushed higher on Wednesday after Iran\par  said it was open to cooperation with Saudi Arabia,\par  partly recovering from an 8 percent fall in the\par  previous session on concerns over demand and weak\par  equities.     \par  \par  EMERGING MARKETS\par  For the top emerging markets news, double click on\par             \par  \par  AFRICA STOCKS\par  For the latest news on African stocks, click on     \par  \par  SOUTH AFRICA MARKETS\par  South African stocks ended in the red for the second\par  consecutive day on Tuesday as resources and financials\par  weighed on the bourse, while the rand strengthened to\par  the dollar in line with a with a stronger\par  euro.            \par  \par  NIGERIA MARKETS\par  Nigeria's interbank lending rate jumped to 2 percent on\par  Tuesday from 1 percent on Monday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par  directed commercial lenders to fund their naira\par  accounts to enable them take part in its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Thursday, trader said.             \par  \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 NIGERIA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  BUDGET\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Nigeria\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's parliament has postponed voting on the 2016\par  budget from Feb. 25 because ministers cannot agree on\par  revised public spending plans, lawmakers said on\par  Tuesday.             \par  \par  KENYA MARKETS\par  Kenya's shilling        gained ground on Tuesday with\par  the help of inflows of dollars from charities, while\par  stocks also edged up.       \par  \par  MOZAMBIQUE INFLATION\par  Mozambique's consumer inflation accelerated to 11.25\par  percent year-on-year in January from 10.55 percent in\par  December, driven by the higher cost of electricity and\par  non-alcoholic beverages, the statistics office said on\par  Tuesday.             \par  \par  MAURITIUS INFLATION\par  Mauritius' rate of inflation              eased to 0.4\par  percent year-on-year in January, the statistics office\par  said on Tuesday.                \par  \par  GHANA GDP\par  Ghana is targeting 5.4 percent gross domestic product\par  growth in 2016, up from 4.1 percent last year and will\par  aim to reduce inflation to 10 percent this year from\par  17.7 percent in 2015, Finance Minister Seth Terkper\par  said on Tuesday.             \par  \par  GHANA BUDGET\par  Ghana may have to revise its 2016 budget after the\par  collapse in oil prices, Finance Minister Seth Terkper\par  told a news conference on Tuesday, giving no specific\par  figures.             \par  \par  BURUNDI ECONOMY\par  Burundi's economy should expand by 3.5 percent this\par  year, the finance ministry said on Tuesday, after\par  contracting sharply in 2015 amid political turmoil and\par  daily violence over President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pierre Nkurunziza\f28 \uc2 \f2 's\par  reelection.             \par  \par  \par  \par    For the latest precious metals report click on     