\par\par \b \uc2 The NEXIM Bank and The \uc2 World Bank\uc2  to Collaborate on Developing Nigeria\u8217\'20\'19s Mining Sector; \u8220\'20\'1C\u8230\'20\'26The clear intention of The \uc2 World Bank\uc2  to collaborate with the NEXIM Bank towards a structured  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  in the  \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s solid minerals sector is quite expedient. More so, with commitment and firm resolve of President Muhammad Buhari to diversify the economy, revitalize the mining sector towards boosting job creation and enhancing  \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange \highlight\uc2  earnings\u8230\'20\'26.\u8221\'20\'1D Orya\b0\par\par\uc2 1,415 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2015\par 07:24\par \uc2 African Press Organization\par \uc2 APOENG\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2015 African Press Organization, All Rights Reserved \par \par \uc2 The Managing Director of the Nigerian Export-Import Bank (NEXIM Bank) ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://neximbank.com.ng"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://neximbank.com.ng}}}\uc2 ), Mr. Roberts Ungwaga Orya, received a team from the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  led by Dr. Francisco Igualada, Senior Mining Specialist, Energy & Extractives Unit, and Mr. Linus Adie Utsu of Mining Investments Consult that visited the Bank to share ideas on how to provide structured \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  towards revamping and deepening \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s mining sector.\par \par \uc2 On the work of NEXIM, Mr. Orya informed the team that the Bank was set up to assist the Government in diversifying the Nigerian economy away from the oil through the provision of export credit facility, risk bearing facilities, trade and market information and export advisory services to export-oriented investors in the manufacturing, agro-processing, solid minerals and services (MASS) sectors. Focusing on solid minerals extraction, Mr. Orya stated that Nigeria is endowed with huge solid mineral deposits, with about 34 products identified in commercial quantities in different parts of Nigeria. However, the failure of Nigeria, since independence in 1960, to put in place a structure that will make the benefits of the exploitation available to all Nigerians has been the bane of the country. The low activity in the solid mineral sector is not yielding the desired financial revenues as there are scanty records of payment of taxes and royalty to the government. Nigeria is losing lots of resources from untapped mineral deposit as well as from the little that is being mined mostly by illegal miners who smuggle the products out of the country. At the moment, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Nigeria (CBN) \uc2 reports, the sector accounts for only 0.14 per cent of GDP, and 3.8% of the non-oil export revenue.\par \par \uc2 Continuing, Mr. Roberts Orya stated that despite its huge potentials, the sector is still grossly underdeveloped and dominated by SME/ artisanal and informal miners using crude machinery and equipment. There are other challenges of poor access to long term funding due to the capital intensive nature of the industry; lack of accredited laboratory for testing and poor infrastructure \u8212\'20\'14 roads, rail facilities \u8212\'20\'14 to move the products.\par \par \uc2 Notwithstanding, according to Orya, NEXIM since inception till date has provided \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the sector to the tune of N7.17billion. This has created more than 4,302 direct jobs plus many indirect jobs in addition to facilitating \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  flow inflow of US$80, 142 annually. Besides direct funding, towards providing assistance to Nigerian miners, NEXIM had in 2013 supported the Miners Association to move from their inaccessible office in Jos, Plateau State and provided them with free office spaces and facilities at the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s headquarters in Abuja.\par \par \uc2 Notwithstanding, Mr. Orya indicated that Nigeria could and should actually make the exploitation of solid minerals the mainstay of the economy. He pointedly queried how the country could be earning such paltry revenue and dividends with all the huge solid mineral reserves while in South Africa, the mining industry remains a cornerstone of the economy; making a significant contribution to economic activity (accounts for about 18% of GDP - 8.6% direct, 10% indirect and induced), job creation (one million jobs - 500 000 direct and 500 000 indirect); and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings creates (more than 50% of all \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings).\par \par \uc2 Accordingly, for NEXIM, the critical issues ranges from how to get the Government and other stakeholders to properly structure the mining sector, increase funding, and attract much needed investment capital; infrastructural development for the industry, especially the establishment of internationally certified laboratories and setting up of internationally recognized and endorsed mining calendar for Nigeria as prevalent in other regimes with dedicated attention to solid mineral exploitation. In his own views, Dr Francisco Igualada, the World Bank Senior Mining Specialist, expressed appreciation to the NEXIM MD and his colleagues for welcoming him and his team to the Bank. He shared his knowledge of the mining industry and his organisation\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the sector over the years indicating that it is fraught with risks and uniquely capital intensive. He wondered why Nigeria, with all its huge resources and potentials has continued to earn less than Ghana, Mali and Burkina Faso from mining activities. According to him, the blame is on the overriding focus on oil and gas by the country at the detriment of neglecting the mining sector which is an enabler for manufacturing, services and other sectors. This is addition to the huge opportunities of job creation, revenue earnings and the development of other support services in the value chain that could have boosted the economy especially with the decline in world oil prices. According to Dr. Igualada, there is need for a structured consolidation of efforts towards developing the sector. This should focus on building the right capacity both at human and institutional levels; as well as establishing and enforcing the requisite legal and policy frameworks. To him, this is the difference between Nigeria and South Africa. He pointed out that the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  has a PPP (Public Private Partnership) arrangement which it could recommend for the development of the solid minerals sector in Nigeria. He concluded by suggesting that there is a need to revamp efforts and link \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to develop the sector through arrangements like the Solid Minerals Fund in addition to renewed involvement by the Government. He stated that he will seek the commitment of his office on the development of the mining sector and collaboration with Nexim Bank, especially in regard to workshop participation and capacity training. Sharing his views, Mr. Linus Adie of Mining Investments Consults indicated that there is a strong need to seize the prevailing opportunity to develop the mining sector. He expressed his dissatisfaction that the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  has not been manifestly keen in its approach to the Nigerian mining sector considering the huge potentials. He cited the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \uc2 World Bank\uc2  in the Sri Lankan gemstone industry, which is now a big business; and collaborations with \uc2 Exim Bank\uc2  in India, among others, through \uc2 MIGA\uc2  and \uc2 IFC\uc2 ; and demanded that Nigeria should be accorded same through NEXIM Bank using same multi- sectorial approach. In closing, Mr. Roberts Orya thanked the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  team and lauded Mr. Linus Adie\u8217\'20\'19s strong advocacy for NEXIM Bank. He regretted the inability of the Solid Minerals Fund to fully take off, especially owing to unresolved legal and other bureaucratic issues. He however reassured the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  team that with the commitment and firm resolve of President Muhammad Buhari to diversify the economy, revitalize the mining sector towards boosting job creation and enhancing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings, that solid minerals will now become the focal sector of the economy. Accordingly, it is unlikely that the challenges of bureaucratic bottle necks and other such issues would continue to hinder the development and optimal exploitation of the mining sector.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Orya stressed on the need to work together with the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  and reiterated that the issues of capacity training, both at institutional and SME levels, is key to achieving meaningful success in the sector. He requested that the NEXIM and the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  should come up with an MOU, which Dr. Francesco Igualada agreed could, in the interim, be in the form of a \u8216\'20\'18Letter of Intent\u8217\'20\'19 to work together.\par \par \uc2 Distributed by APO (African Press Organization) on behalf of Nigerian Export-Import Bank (NEXIM).\par \par \uc2 Media Contact:Chinedu Moghalu (234-8088-353-804,moghaluc@neximbank.com.ng)\par \par \uc2 About NEXIM Bank -The Nigerian Export-Import Bank ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://neximbank.com.ng"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://neximbank.com.ng}}}\uc2 ) was established by Act 38 of 1991 as an Export Credit Agency with the broad mandate to promoting the diversification of the Nigerian economy and deepening the external sector, particularly the non-oil through the provision of credit facilities in both local and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; risk-bearing facilities through export credit guarantee & export credit insurance; business development and financial advisory services etc.\par \par \uc2 In pursuit of its mandate of promoting export diversification and deepening the non-oil sector, the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s current strategic initiatives are targeted towards boosting employment creation and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings in the Manufacturing, Agro-processing, Solid Minerals and Services (Tourism, Transportation and Entertainment) industries.\par \par \uc2 Dokument APOENG0020160517ebbb0032g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4500\pichgoal600
