\par\par \b \uc2 African Markets - Factors to watch on Sept 17\b0\par\par\uc2 732 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2015\par 06:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NAIROBI, Sept 17 (Reuters) - The following company announcements, scheduled economic\par indicators, debt and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market moves and political events may affect African markets on\par Thursday.\par     - - - - -\par  EVENTS:\par  *KENYA - The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  auctions 91-day \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  bills worth a total 4\par  billion shillings.\par  \par  GLOBAL MARKETS\par  Asian stocks edged up to a three-week high on Thursday while the dollar\par  drifted lower against other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as investors consolidated positions\par  ahead of a nail-biting \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  policy\par  decision.                          \par  \par  WORLD OIL PRICES\par  Oil prices dipped on Thursday after Asia's economies showed new signs of\par  weakness, but prices largely held on to a big jump in the previous session\par  after a U.S. stock draw, with some analysts saying oil markets may have\par  bottomed out.                    \par  \par  EMERGING MARKETS\par  For the top emerging markets news, double click on            \par  \par  AFRICA STOCKS\par  For the latest news on African stocks, click on     \par  \par  SOUTH AFRICA MARKETS\par  South Africa's stocks rose on Wednesday, reaching a 4-1/2 week high with\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 SABMiller\f28 \uc2 \f2          surging after news that \f28 \uc2 \f2 AB InBev\f28 \uc2 \f2           was to make an\par  offer to acquire the British firm, while the rand firmed on\par  better-than-expected retail data.               \par  \par  NIGERIA MARKETS\par  Nigeria's interbank money market was halted for a second day on Wednesday\par  after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  told commercial lenders to provide cash backing for\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  purchases at its proposed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Friday.\par                 \par      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Nigeria\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  plans to raise 100.88 billion naira in treasury bills with\par  maturities between three months and one year at an auction on Sept. 23, the\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said on Wednesday.               \par  \par  NIGERIA \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  DEVALUATION\par  Nigeria's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should not be devalued further, President Muhammadu\par  Buhari said on Wednesday, despite the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's growing struggles to\par  keep the naira at current levels.                \par  \par  NIGERIA CORPORATE DEBT\par  Already feeling the pinch from slumping oil prices and slowing economic\par  growth, Nigerian companies are finding it increasingly hard to get hold of\par  foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  due to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  restrictions and may struggle to repay\par  their debts.                           \par  \par  KENYA MARKETS\par  Kenya's shilling        made modest gains on Wednesday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par  bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold an undisclosed amount of dollars, while stocks were up.\par                 \par  \par  KENYA UCHUMI SUPERMARKETS\par  Kenya's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uchumi Supermarkets\f28 \uc2 \f2           , which fired its top managers in June,\par  will shift to renting outlets instead of owning them and expects to return\par  to profit growth within the next year, its chief executive said.\par                 \par  \par  KENYA IMF\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kenya's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has acted appropriately by tightening interest rates\par  and its economy remains on track despite headwinds from rising global market\par  volatility and local security challenges, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par  said on Wednesday.               \par  \par  UGANDA MARKETS\par  The Ugandan shilling        gained on Wednesday as corporate demand for\par  dollars subsided and expectations grew that rising yields on Ugandan debt\par  would attract more money from foreign investors.                \par  \par  EAST AFRICA PETROLEUM\par  Uganda's government is still considering a pipeline through Tanzania to\par  export oil if it proved cheaper than alternatives, an official said on\par  Wednesday, even though the Ugandan president indicated last month that a\par  route through Kenya had been picked.                \par  \par  DEMOCRATIC REPUBLIC OF CONGO POLITICS\par  Seven senior political figures were kicked out of Democratic Republic of\par  Congo's ruling coalition on Wednesday for signing a letter urging President\par  Joseph Kabila not to cling to power after his term expires next year,\par  government officials said.                \par  \par  CAMEROON PETROLEUM\par  Tower Resources          plans to invest at least $43 million over seven\par  years to explore for oil in a shallow-water block in Cameroon's Rio del Rey\par  basin, the company and Cameroonian officials said on Wednesday.\par                 \par  \par  SENEGAL ECONOMY\par  Senegal's economic growth will accelerate to 6 percent next year, boosted by\par  a government development plan, increasing trade with neighbouring Mali and\par  lower oil prices, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) forecast.\par                 \par  \par  BURKINA FASO POLITICS\par  United Nations Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon expressed outrage on Wednesday\par  at reports of the detention of Burkina Faso's president and prime minister\par  and several ministers, calling for their immediate release.                \par  \par  ZAMBIA ECONOMY\par  Zambia's economy is likely to grow by less than 5 percent in 2015 due to a\par  power crunch that has hit output from mining companies already grappling\par  with a slide in global copper prices, Finance Minister Alexander Chikwanda\par  said.               \par  \par      For the latest precious metals report click on        \par      For the latest base metals report click on         \par      For the latest crude oil report click on\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-9-17T05:44:54.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150917eb9h0056b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
