\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-\uc2 Nigeria's  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  sells dollars to prop up  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 434 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2015\par 17:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds oil firm dollar sale, dealers comment)\par \par \uc2 LAGOS, Feb 17 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the third straight session on Tuesday to defend the naira by selling dollars below its official band but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded weaker in the interbank market.\par \par \uc2 Dollar sales by an oil firm were traded at a weaker level than in the earlier sale by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.\par \par \uc2 The bank once again sold dollars below its official band, at 198 naira to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and again banned banks from reselling dollars bought at its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  auction to other banks to curb speculation.\par \par \uc2 All the trades by the bank have been outside its own target band of 160-176 to the dollar set in November when it devalued the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by 8 percent to save its foreign reserves. On Monday and Tuesday, it sold dollars at 198 naira, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 "The market is weaker than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate ... counterparties are selling dollars based on how they were able to buy it," a dealer at a major lender said.\par \par \uc2 The naira crashed through a psychologically important level of 200 to the dollar last week in a rout triggered by weak oil prices and escalating tension over the postponement of a presidential election Africa's biggest economy, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to step up its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been selling dollars in a special \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Friday, spending $380 million in two-days to shore up the naira and curb speculative attacks, which it says is the reason behind the weaknesses in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Addax Petroleum\uc2  sold $7 million to some lenders to buy naira for its local obligations, testing the naira's true level as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately weakened to 203.50 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's special \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have kept the naira trading within a range of between 197 to 199.50.\par \par \uc2 However, there has been no interbank trading at that level as liquidity is thin on the market and commercial banks are restricted from selling dollars bought from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tight interbank liquidity undermines Nigeria's credibility as a smoothly functioning capital market, which could trigger its ejection from a \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  emerging market bond index.\par \par \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  added Nigeria to the widely followed index in 2012, when liquidity was improving, making it only the second African country after South Africa to be included. (Reporting by Oludare Mayowa; Writing by Chijioke Ohuocha; Editing by James Macharia and Alison Williams) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-2-17T17:04:49.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150217eb2h00g7j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
