\par\par \b \uc2 African Markets - Factors to watch on Feb 17\b0\par\par\uc2 411 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2015\par 06:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The following company announcements, scheduled economic indicators, debt and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market\par moves and political events may affect African markets on Tuesday.\par     - - - - - -\par  GLOBAL MARKETS\par  Asian share markets and the euro retreated on Tuesday as\par  talks between Greece and euro zone finance ministers broke\par  down after Athens rejected a proposal to request a six-month\par  extension of its international bailout programme.\par             \par  \par  WORLD OIL PRICES\par  Brent crude extended its rally on Tuesday, moving further\par  away from six-year lows hit in January, but some analysts\par  warned that prices had risen too far and could face a\par  downward correction.\par       \par  \par  EMERGING MARKETS\par  For the top emerging markets news, double click on\par             \par  \par  AFRICA STOCKS\par  For the latest news on African stocks, click on     \par  \par  SOUTH AFRICA MARKETS\par  South Africa's rand steadied against the dollar on Monday\par  amid thin volumes with the U.S. financial markets closed for\par  a holiday but traders said a slew of data due later in the\par  week could offer direction.\par                 \par  \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 NIGERIA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKETS\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Nigeria\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to defend the beleaguered\par  naira          on Monday by selling dollars below its\par  official band for the second time, a sign the bank could\par  weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to save its dwindling foreign reserves.\par                 \par  \par  NIGERIA DOLLAR BONDS\par  Nigeria's dollar bonds have been battered by tumbling oil\par  prices and domestic politics, but thanks to low debt ratios,\par  a light repayment schedule and demand from local banks they\par  should weather the storm.\par                 \par  \par  NIGERIA INSECURITY\par  Nigerian soldiers recaptured two towns from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Boko Haram\f28 \uc2 \f2  on\par  Monday as U.S. and regional troops began war games in\par  neighbouring Chad in a growing international campaign to\par  counter the Islamist group.\par                 \par  \par  KENYA MARKETS\par  Kenya's main share index rose for a sixth straight session,\par  with the country's sole electricity supplier Kenya Power in\par  demand with investors betting on its growth prospects.\par                 \par  \par  ZIMBABWE AID\par  The \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  has given Zimbabwe 234 million euros\par  ($267 million), it said on Monday, the first time the bloc\par  has given financial aid to the southern African nation's\par  government since imposing sanctions in 2002.\par                 \par  \par  ZIMBABWE INFLATION\par  Zimbabwe's consumer prices contracted by 1.28 percent in\par  January on a year-on-year basis compared to a 0.8 percent\par  decline in December, the statistical agency Zimstats said on\par  Monday.\par                 \par  \par  \par  \par      For the latest precious metals report click on        \par      For the latest base metals report click on         \par      For the latest crude oil report click on\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-2-17T06:34:33.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150217eb2h005bs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
