\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED-UPDATE 1-Trading delayed on  \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  market for  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 288 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Februar 2015\par 11:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Removes reference to daily delay in headline)\par \par \uc2 LAGOS, Feb 13 (Reuters) - Trading on Nigeria's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will be delayed until after 10 a.m. (0900 GMT) on Friday to allow dealers to submit demand for dollars to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the head of the Financial Market Dealers Association (FMDA) said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will then inject dollars into the market in what FMDA president David Adepoju described as "a special \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  auction". "The banks are compiling the level of the demand in the market," he added.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had said the move would not be a one-off, Adepoju said.\par \par \uc2 The change is part of efforts by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to put naira trading on a more orderly footing after a weeks-long slide in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has seen it burn through more than $110 million a day trying to defend the naira's target band.\par \par \uc2 By 10.30 a.m. on Friday there was still no trading going on in the market.\par \par \uc2 Nigeria's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief said on Thursday there was "no need to panic" about the plunge in the naira, which fell through the psychological level of 200 to the dollar this week.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been battered by weak oil prices and tensions around the postponement of a presidential election in Africa's biggest economy and leading energy producer.\par \par \uc2 Nigeria relies on oil for 90 percent of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  started to come under serious pressure in early November, forcing the bank to devalue it. (Reporting by Chijioke Ohuocha; Writing by Tim Cocks; Editing by Catherine Evans) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-2-13T11:24:19.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150213eb2d00dj8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
