\par\par \b \uc2 African Markets - Factors to watch on Jan 30\b0\par\par\uc2 512 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2015\par 06:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NAIROBI, Jan 30 (Reuters) - The following company announcements, scheduled economic\par indicators, debt and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market moves and political events may affect African markets on\par Friday.\par     - - - - -\par  EVENTS:\par  *Kenya and Uganda due to release January consumer inflation\par  data.\par  *MAURITIUS - The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  auctions Bank 364-day Treasury\par  bills worth a total 500 million rupees. \par  \par  GLOBAL MARKETS\par  Asian shares edged up on Friday, talking heart from a late\par  earnings-led surge on Wall Street even as continuing concerns\par  about global growth tempered gains.                          \par  \par  WORLD OIL PRICES\par  Oil prices dipped in early trading on Friday following slight\par  gains in the previous sessions, and analysts said the outlook\par  remained weak with production high and producers reducing\par  operating costs to adjust to lower export\par  revenues.                    \par  \par  EMERGING MARKETS\par  For the top emerging markets news, double click on           \par  \par  AFRICA STOCKS\par  For the latest news on African stocks, click on     \par  \par  SOUTH AFRICA MARKETS\par  South Africa's rand recovered earlier losses against the\par  dollar on Thursday, after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  kept domestic\par  rates unchanged and moved to dampen market expectations of a\par  rate cut this year due to lower oil prices.               \par      Stocks ended lower on Thursday as a rise in retailers\par  such as Shoprite          failed to offset a slide in gold\par  mining houses, which were hit by lower metal prices.\par                 \par  \par  AFRICA \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par  Nigeria's volatile naira is likely to steady next week on\par  dollar sales by oil companies, while East African \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par  are expected to be supported by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .               \par  \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 NIGERIA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKETS\par  Nigerian naira           shed 1.64 percent to close at 189.10\par  against the greenback on Thursday, despite dollar sales by\par  the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and oil companies, dealers said.\par                 \par  \par  NIGERIA ECONOMY\par  Nigeria expects economic growth this year of 5.54 percent,\par  down from an estimated 6.23 percent for 2014, after the\par  government trimmed expenditure because of the oil price\par  slump, the statistics office said on Thursday.               \par  \par  KENYA MARKETS\par  The Kenyan shilling        was a touch weaker on Thursday on\par  dollar demand from importers, while stocks nudged\par  lower.               \par  \par  TANZANIA ECONOMY\par  Tanzania needs urgently to deal with its growing fiscal\par  deficit to boost economic growth, which is expected to reach\par  7 percent this year, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  said on Thursday.\par                 \par  \par  GHANA OIL PRODUCTION\par  Ghana's new oil fields are on schedule to start production\par  next year as development has passed the half-way stage, lead\par  operator \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tullow Oil\f28 \uc2 \f2  said on Thursday.                    \par  \par  IVORY COAST EUROBOND\par  Ivory Coast aims to issue a Eurobond of less than $1 billion\par  next month, Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Daniel Kablan Duncan\f28 \uc2 \f2  said on\par  Thursday, as it seeks extra funds to help its economy recover\par  after a civil war.               \par  \par  ANGOLA ECONOMY\par  Angola's oil-dependent economy is set to slow this year, key\par  infrastructure projects will be shelved and swathes of social\par  spending are facing the chop as a global crude price slump\par  takes its toll on Africa's second-biggest producer.\par                 \par  \par      For the latest precious metals report click on        \par      For the latest base metals report click on         \par      For the latest crude oil report click on\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-1-30T06:03:26.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150130eb1u005k1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
