\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria \highlight\uc2  naira closes at record low, despite  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 199 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2015\par 14:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds naira close)\par \par \uc2 LAGOS, Jan 26 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's naira ended at a record closing low on Monday, despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to weaken on strong demand for the greenback, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 The unit closed at 191.10, a new record low against the U.S. dollar, compared with Friday's record low close of 190.60 naira. One dealer traded the naira at a one-off rate of 194.10 during mid-day session.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had initially gained 1.66 percent to 186.80 naira after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and two oil companies sold dollars to lift the naira, before it then slipped into negative territory.\par \par \uc2 The naira has hit a string of record lows as dollar liquidity on the interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market thins out amidst a collapse in the price of oil, Nigeria's main export.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said Total offered to sell $80 million to some lenders on Monday while Shell sold an undisclosed amount of dollars, to buy naira for their local \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (Reporting by Chijioke Ohuocha and Oludare Mayowa; Editing by Toby Chopra) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-1-26T14:31:52.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150126eb1q00ean\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
