\par\par \b \uc2 WEEKAHEAD-AFRICA- \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -African  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  on the ropes, more  \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions \highlight\uc2  eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 982 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2015\par 16:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 LAGOS, Jan 22 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Nigeria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's naira is expected to lose more ground next week, hit by the slide in crude prices and a decline in dollar liquidity, while other African \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to remain on the back foot unless their respective central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 NIGERIA\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The Nigerian naira could face more volatility next week amid tight dollar liquidity in spite of sustained \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, and an upward revision of commercial banks' foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  position limits to 0.5 percent from 0.1 percent previously.\par \par \uc2 The naira was trading around 189.90 to the dollar on the interbank market, down from 185.6 a week ago.\par \par \uc2 "The situation in the market remains dicey because of insignificant dollar flows from independent sources other than the regular \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," one dealer said.\par \par \uc2 "What the market needs is a large flow of dollars to offset pent-up demand built up in the market."\par \par \uc2 Traders expected month-end dollar flows from some oil companies next week to provide a boost, but said they would have to be large enough to reduce pressure on the naira.\par \par \uc2 Most oil companies in Africa's top crude exporters sell dollars every month to obtain local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for their domestic obligations.\par \par \uc2 KENYA\par \par \uc2 Kenya's shilling is expected to weaken in the next week, hurt by increased importer dollar demand against scant inflows.\par \par \uc2 At 1133 GMT, commercial banks quoted the shilling at 91.70/80 to the dollar, compared with last Thursday's close of 91.45/55. The shilling touched a new three-year low of 91.80/90 to the dollar during Thursday's session.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the week traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold an unspecified amount of dollars to help the shilling, but that the local unit was likely to weaken past 92.00 without more support.\par \par \uc2 "Demand (for dollars) is still building up, especially now that we are approaching end-month. But I am also looking at the supply side which is quite mute at the moment," Robert Gatobu, a trader at \uc2 Commercial Bank of Africa\uc2 , said.\par \par \uc2 Traders forecast the shilling, which has lost 1.1 percent against the dollar so far this month, would trade in the 91.50-92.00 range in coming days.\par \par \uc2 Technical analysis of the 14-day and 50-day weighted moving averages showed the shilling was expected to maintain a weakening trend in the near term.\par \par \uc2 UGANDA\par \par \uc2 The shilling is forecast to strengthen in the next week after a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try and stem demand pressure.\par \par \uc2 Commercial banks quoted the shilling at 2,860/2,870, stronger than last Thursday's close of 2,890/2,900.\par \par \uc2 The shilling is down 3.1 percent against the dollar this month due to demand from commercial banks and importers.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold dollars five times so far this month to try and cushion the shilling, which traded just shy of its softest level of 2,910/2,920 reached on Sept. 23, 2011.\par \par \uc2 "After the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decisive action I think we'll see a lot of unwinding of the long positions," said Shahzad Kamaluddin, trader at \uc2 Crane Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely trade in the 2,875-2,900 range, Kamaluddin added.\par \par \uc2 TANZANIA\par \par \uc2 The shilling is expected to extend losses, hit by demand from energy and manufacturing sectors and subdued inflows of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after touching a five-year low on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Commercial banks quoted the shilling at 1,835/1,845 to the dollar, weaker than 1,770/1,780 a week ago.\par \par \uc2 "The weakening of the shilling will likely continue next week because demand is still high and inflows are not expected anytime soon," said Moses Kawiche, a trader at CRDB Bank.\par \par \uc2 "There is substantial demand for dollars particularly coming from the energy sector, oil importers and manufacturers."\par \par \uc2 Market participants expected the shilling to trade in the 1,840-1,850 range over the coming days.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Tanzania\uc2  said on its website it had traded $30.3 million on the interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market over the past week.\par \par \uc2 GHANA\par \par \uc2 Ghana's cedi is expected to remain under pressure in coming weeks due to a persistent lack of dollar inflows amid seasonal high demand from local importers, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has declined about two percent against the dollar so far this year, and traded at 3.2900 to the greenback by 1300 GMT on Thursday compared to 3.2185 on Jan. 2.\par \par \uc2 "We see GHS near-term bearish outlook intact in the coming sessions," \uc2 Barclays Bank Ghana\uc2  trader Michael Akpakli said.\par \par \uc2 Ghana, which exports cocoa, gold and oil, is currently in talks with the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  for an assistance package to fix its economy and support the cedi, which slumped 32 percent last year.\par \par \uc2 ZAMBIA\par \par \uc2 Traders expected the kwacha to take its direction from how the country handles the outcome of the Jan. 20 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 At 1200 GMT on Thursday, commercial banks quoted the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Africa's second-largest copper producer at 6.4350 from 6.5400 a week ago.\par \par \uc2 This was the kwacha's second daily gain as Tuesday's peaceful vote eased investor jitters about stability in Africa's second biggest copper producer.\par \par \uc2 "We anticipate that the local unit will remain in limbo in the time being and direction will be derived from domestic events," Banc ABC said in a market note.\par \par \uc2 Zambia's ruling party presidential candidate Edgar Lungu took a slim lead over his rival Hakainde Hichilema after votes from half of the constituencies were counted, the electoral commission said on Thursday. (Reporting by Oludare Mayowa, George Obulutsa, Elias Biryabarema, Fumbuka Ng'wanakilala, Kwasi Kpodo and Chris Mfula; Editing by James Macharia) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-1-22T16:03:25.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150122eb1m00fkz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
