\par\par \b \uc2 African Markets - Factors to watch on Jan 19\b0\par\par\uc2 432 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 2015\par 06:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The following company announcements, scheduled economic indicators, debt and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market\par moves and political events may affect African markets on Monday.\par     - - - - -\par  EVENTS:\par  MALAWI - Expecting inflation data for December. The rate\par  edged up to 23.7 percent in November from 23.3 percent.\par  \par  GLOBAL MARKETS\par  Most Asian share markets followed Wall Street higher on\par  Monday, though investors were wary of being disappointed yet\par  again by economic data from China and policy stimulus in the\par  euro zone.\par             \par  \par  WORLD OIL PRICES\par  Oil prices fell in early Asian trade on Monday, with markets\par  expecting gloomy Chinese economic data to be published this\par  week.\par       \par  \par  EMERGING MARKETS\par  For the top emerging markets news, double click on\par             \par  \par  AFRICA STOCKS\par  For the latest news on African stocks, click on     \par  \par  AFRICA DEBT\par  Yields on Kenyan Treasury bills on sale next week are\par  expected to hold steady in subdued bidding due to mid-month\par  tax payments, while yields on two bonds being auctioned are\par  likely to rise.\par                 \par  \par  SOUTH AFRICA MARKETS\par  South Africa's government bonds fell to their lowest in\par  nearly 22 months on Friday, in a market increasingly seeing\par  the chance that slowing inflation due to sliding oil prices\par  will keep interest rates depressed.\par                 \par  \par  NIGERIA MARKETS\par  Nigeria's naira          firmed 0.46 percent on Friday,\par  recovering from near record lows against the dollar after\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , dealers said.\par                 \par  \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 NIGERIA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  LIQUIDITY\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Nigeria\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  denied there was any shortage of\par  liquidity in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market on Friday, rejecting \f28 \uc2 \f2 JP\par  Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's stated reason for putting the status of its bonds\par  under review.\par                    \par  \par  NIGERIA INSURGENTS\par  West African leaders will seek authority next week from the\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 African Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  to create a multi-national force to fight\par  Nigeria's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Boko Haram\f28 \uc2 \f2  Islamist insurgents, Ghanaian President\par  John Mahama told Reuters on Friday.\par                 \par  \par  KENYA MARKETS\par  Kenya's shilling        hovered at a three-year low on\par  Friday and could face renewed pressure next week as\par  companies seek dollars to meet payments due at the end of\par  the month, while shares closed marginally higher.\par                  \par  \par  GHANA MARKETS\par  The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Ghana\f28 \uc2 \f2  said the yield on its 91-day bill rose to\par  25.8426 percent at an auction on Friday, from 25.8335 at the\par  last sale.\par                 \par  \par  ANGOLA CENTRAL BANKER\par  Angola's President Jose Eduardo dos Santos has replaced\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor Jose de Lima Massano with Jose Pedro\par  de Morais, the official news agency Angop said on its\par  website.\par                 \par  \par      For the latest precious metals report click on        \par      For the latest base metals report click on         \par      For the latest crude oil report click on\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-1-19T06:12:05.000Z   \par \par \uc